{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh43dW75YGB7SdzjmB3c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01CMETCm8XcpNgnn4B1p5Bc6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","limit":370},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1783,"cache_read_input_tokens":22513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1783},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"f108da91-7e09-4843-ae04-d91b0f505b4c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:53:37.047Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh43dJDDGDA4WDgEicM9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01CMETCm8XcpNgnn4B1p5Bc6":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","limit":370}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CMETCm8XcpNgnn4B1p5Bc6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Eye of the Bolt-Holder\n2\t\n3\tA build plan for a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by **Eye of the Beholder** (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991). It is built on the my-3D2dge engine and ships as one HTML file. It is written for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from TASK.md and this file alone.\n4\t\n5\t- **Title.** I brainstormed 50 playful riffs on the source's name and picked one with a real RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned index 5): **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**.\n6\t- **File-name forms.** GameTitle = `EyeOfTheBoltHolder`, SOURCE = `EyeOfTheBeholder1991`. Fill these into your TASK.md's bracket pattern, for example `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<timestamp>`.\n7\t- **Pitch.** Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks, is being locked in from below. Doors bolt themselves, wells cap shut, and a granary will not open for axes. The Council of Keys sends a party of four (later up to six) down through the Drains, the Tumbler Halls of the dead locksmiths' guild, the Gloam Deeps of the eye-cult and, at last, the Eye's Vault. The party moves one cell at a time in real time: it swings each hand as it comes off cooldown, memorizes spells at camp, eats rations, weighs plates with rocks and finds the loose stone. At the bottom it turns three Great Locks to strip the cage from **the Bolt-Holder**, a floating amber Eye held in a cage of seven iron bolts that hurls lightning, gazes joints stiff and rains iron.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 0. Ground rules for the builders\n12\t\n13\t1. **TASK.md wins on deliverables.** Names, folders, the single HTML, the About screen, the reply and `exit-interview.json` follow your TASK.md exactly. This plan says how; §11 has the templates.\n14\t2. **Folders.** Your dev folder is the bracket name; the bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` go in the root beside TASK.md.\n15\t   - Keep every clone, cache and `node_modules` inside your dev folder (in `_ref/` and similar). `/tmp` on this machine is a shared tmpfs and it was full during planning (a `git clone` into it failed with \"No space left on device\").\n16\t   - Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need. Other agents share the machine.\n17\t3. **Processes and ports.**\n18\t   - Prefer `file://` and headless Node.\n19\t   - When you need a server (only the AI Hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and read the port it gets. Check that the page is your game before trusting a result.\n20\t   - Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill, killall or `fuser -k`.\n21\t4. **Tests.** One command runs every test and prints only failures plus a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n22\t5. **No stubs.** Everything in this plan is required unless it is marked *optional*. If time runs short, cut quantity (fewer side items, smaller side rooms), never a system or a floor. There must be no TODOs, placeholders or dead menu entries.\n23\t6. **Engine changes are allowed.** Keep each one small and list it in `PATCHES.md` (what and why). The About screen and the Engine Feedback answers draw on it.\n24\t\n25\t---\n26\t\n27\t## 1. Research summary\n28\t\n29\t### 1.1 The source, and what we keep\n30\t\n31\t**Confirmed facts** (Giant Bomb, MobyGames, oldgames.sk):\n32\t- Eye of the Beholder was developed by Westwood Associates and published by SSI in 1991, for MS-DOS and almost at once for the Amiga.\n33\t- A party of four created heroes is joined by up to two recruitable NPCs.\n34\t- The Lords of Waterdeep send the party into the sewers, and the way out is sealed behind them.\n35\t- At the bottom waits the beholder crime lord Xanathar.\n36\t\n37\t**What made it distinctive** (from knowledge of the game, not re-verified), and how this plan uses each part:\n38\t\n39\t| Source trait | How we turn it into our game |\n40\t|---|---|\n41\t| First person on a grid, 90° turns, everything in real time | Step, turn and strafe one cell at a time. Monsters act on their own clocks, so there are no turns. |\n42\t| Party of 4 to 6 in rows; the front two melee; the back rows need reach, missiles or spells | Formation of three rows of two, with reach weapons in row 2 and missiles, thrown weapons and spells from anywhere (§4.6) |\n43\t| Every hand is an action; it greys out on cooldown; damage shows on the portraits | Two hand slots on every party card, each with a cooldown wipe and damage pops (§5.2) |\n44\t| Vancian magic: mages learn from scrolls into a spellbook and memorize; clerics pray; both at camp | Camp menu with Rest, Memorize, Pray and Scribe (§4.8, §4.9) |\n45\t| Paperdoll and backpack; the item held on the cursor is thrown, placed and dropped; food and rations | The party's **Hand** (the held item), throwing, putting down and alcoves; a food meter (§4.5, §4.9) |\n46\t| Puzzle vocabulary: pressure plates held down by items, levers, buttons, keys of many shapes, illusory walls, loose-stone switches, teleporters, spinners, pits to the level below, wall niches, inscriptions, dart traps, hidden \"special quests\" | All of them, wired as data (§4.2). Floors 1 to 3 each have one hidden deed (special quest). |\n47\t| Monster groups share a cell; the sidestep \"dance\" | Cell capacity by size. Attack wind-ups that a step can dodge make sidestepping an intended skill (§4.7). |\n48\t| Terse second-person messages, a stately intro, formal NPCs, a few moral choices | Style guide and scripts (§7.2, §7.3), plus Veil's choice on floor 3 |\n49\t\n50\t**What we change:**\n51\t- The world and characters are original: no Waterdeep, Forgotten Realms, Xanathar, beholders or AD&D rule text.\n52\t- The UI is controller-first for a TV at 10 feet.\n53\t- Steps are tweened, and the 3D view fills the screen with dynamic light and shadow.\n54\t- Monster attacks are telegraphed.\n55\t- Music has combat layers, and voices are babble.\n56\t- Hints escalate, so no puzzle can strand the player.\n57\t\n58\t### 1.2 The engine (my-3D2dge v0.85.2)\n59\t\n60\t- **The handoff** (`dist/handoff/`) is the 2D engine. `my-3d2dge.js` is readable and includes every module, among them `grid` (E.Grid, E.Fog), `story` (E.Story) and `rpg`. It also holds the docs (API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md), the tools (`tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `replay.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`), 22 2D kits and a template game whose `game/test.mjs` shows how to test without a browser.\n61\t- **The 3D engine.** The handoff's genre playbook sends \"First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder)\" to the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera and the **3D CRAWLER** kit. That engine is not in the handoff folder but sits in the same repository: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, v0.85.2, an ES module whose header is its manual. It uses three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER` to the 2D engine, which still runs the loop, the scenes, the input, the menus, the sound and the rigs.\n62\t- **The crawler slice** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`, 561 lines) already does most of the core loop:\n63\t  - cells of 2×2 tiles with tweened steps, turns, strafes and a bump lurch;\n64\t  - a fixed camera at eye height 25;\n65\t  - a party of four on cards with `rig.drawPortrait`;\n66\t  - gates (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock), a lever `THING`, plates and a dart trap;\n67\t  - monsters walked on `E.Grid` paths, an `E.Fog` automap and a bolt projectile;\n68\t  - real time via `E.Turns 'atb'`, and a bot.\n69\t\n70\t  Use it as the reference for the camera, the tweens and the things. Restructure the rest: the kit mixes rules with the 3D world, and we separate them (§3.5).\n71\t- **Spike run during planning.** I took the repository's `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-crawler.html` and pointed its import map at jsDelivr: `three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`. These are byte-identical to the vendored copies by SHA-256. Opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 (Playwright 1.63.0) with SwiftShader WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl#crawler`):\n72\t  - it booted with no console errors;\n73\t  - `CRAWLER.run(600)` gave the same hash twice (`e3880e33`);\n74\t  - `CRAWLER.bot()` won: lever, key, unlock, plate, Warden and stair, all four alive.\n75\t\n76\t  The native look is chunky pixel 3D with brick walls, flagstone floors and live portrait cards. **So the core technical bet works: one HTML file, opened from disk, with the engines inlined and three.js and Rapier from a CDN.**\n77\t- **Engine names this plan relies on** (look up details in API-INDEX.md and the 3D header):\n78\t\n79\t  | Area | Names |\n80\t  |---|---|\n81\t  | Game and scenes | `new E.Game({ canvas, res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input, menu })`, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { steps } })`, `game.go`, `game.advance(s)`, `game.debug` |\n82\t  | Timing and scripts | `game.timeScale`, `game.script` (coroutines), `game.tween`, `game.after` |\n83\t  | Input | `game.input` (`pressed`, `down`, `tap`, `hold`, `press(code)`, `clicked()`, `pointer()`, `padAxes`, `lastDevice`, `label(action)`, `glyphs`) |\n84\t  | Settings and saves | `game.settings.define`, `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave), `menu: { items, settings, saves }` |\n85\t  | Sound | `game.audio` (`define`, `sfx`, `music`, `layer`, `sting`, `duck`, `ctx`) |\n86\t  | Text, UI and rigs | `E.Dialog` (`talk.shown`, `talk.voice`), `E.Story`, `E.Menu`, `E.ui.box/bar`, `E.font.text/title`, `E.Humanoid` / `E.Blob` with `rig.drawPortrait`, `E.MOVES` |\n87\t  | Grid module | `E.Grid` (`path`), `E.Fog` |\n88\t  | 3D world | `new E.World3D(game, { level, … })`, `world.thing(kind, …)`, `E.World3D.THINGS.<kind>` (pen-drawn things), `world.mob`, `world.pen`, `world.glow`, `world.light`, `world.arc`, `world.project`, `world.camera` (`'fixed'`), `overlay: { fov }` plus `world.overlay.pen` (first-person hands), `world.prepare()`, `world.dispose()` |\n89\t  | 3D settings | `E.World3D.SETTINGS` (cards or puppets, picture resolution, shadows, filter) |\n90\t\n91\t### 1.3 AI Hub (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n92\t\n93\t- **Modes.** `parallel`, `ghost` (each copy runs its own round and shows the others' characters when they are in the same area) and `shared-world`.\n94\t- **The client.** `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. In a session it seeds `Math.random`, gives the page its own in-memory storage and hands fullscreen to the hub.\n95\t- **Tags.** `hub:session`, `hub:session-players`, `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal` (at most 60 characters), `hub:session-howto` (at most 80) and `hub:session-minutes`.\n96\t- **The flow.** `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` returns `info`, or `null` when there is no hub (then the game plays on its own). `AIHub.ready()` is called once, with no key press. The round starts on `start`, and play begins when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n97\t- **Reporting.**\n98\t  - `progress({ score, label })` is taken about once a second.\n99\t  - `result({ time | score })` is sent once.\n100\t  - On `time-up` the game reports within `graceMs`.\n101\t  - On `end` input and sound stop.\n102\t- **Messages.** `send(data, { reliable })` takes JSON of at most 16 KB, at most 20 a second.\n103\t- **Rules.**\n104\t  - The network goes only through the hub.\n105\t  - There are no dialogs or popups, and no quitting or returning to the title in a session.\n106\t  - Names come from the hub and never become HTML.\n107\t  - Options is ignored while Create is held.\n108\t- **The test.** The harness (`harness.html` plus `bridge.js` and `round.js`, served beside the game) passes the game when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. Run it with `?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`, then with `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1` and `&scenario=host-leave`.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 2. The game at a glance\n113\t\n114\t- **Genre.** A real-time, first-person, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n115\t- **Perspective.**\n116\t  - First person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns.\n117\t  - A top-down parchment automap screen.\n118\t  - Full-screen overlay screens (party, camp, map), drawn over the dimmed 3D view.\n119\t- **Pillars.**\n120\t  1. *Every cell is a decision.* Step, turn and sidestep in real time.\n121\t  2. *Every hand is a weapon.* Per-hand cooldowns and formation rows.\n122\t  3. *Every lock has a key, and some keys are rocks.* Physical puzzles built from a small, consistent vocabulary.\n123\t  4. *A party you care about.* Live portraits, hunger, levels, deaths and NPCs with voices.\n124\t- **Length.** Four floors plus a race floor, about 50 to 75 minutes for a first play of the campaign. Every system appears by the end of floor 2, and floors 3 and 4 deepen them.\n125\t- **Modes.**\n126\t  - Campaign: New Game, Continue and Load.\n127\t  - **Rat Run**: a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n128\t  - **AI Hub ghost race** (the Rat Run, 1 to 4 players).\n129\t  - Attract demo.\n130\t- **The player can:**\n131\t  - move, strafe and turn;\n132\t  - fight with any hand, fire crossbows and bows, throw anything, and cast 9 arcane spells and 9 prayers;\n133\t  - read and scribe scrolls, zap wands;\n134\t  - equip 10 body slots and a 12-slot backpack per member;\n135\t  - pick up, put down, throw and place items in alcoves;\n136\t  - open doors, pull levers, press buttons and loose stones, weigh plates, insert keys and wards, and pick locks or disarm traps (thieves);\n137\t  - cross pits (Feather Step), see secrets (Find Seams, goggles), rest, eat, memorize and pray;\n138\t  - level up, raise the dead, recruit and dismiss NPCs, make one moral choice, and save and load anywhere out of combat.\n139\t- **The player cannot:** jump, look freely (the view is grid-locked), walk diagonally, buy things (there are no shops or gold), or cross pits without the spell.\n140\t\n141\t---\n142\t\n143\t## 3. Architecture\n144\t\n145\t### 3.1 Stack (pinned)\n146\t\n147\t| Piece | Version | Where from | Used for |\n148\t|---|---|---|---|\n149\t| my-3D2dge 2D engine (full, readable) | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` | the loop of exact 1/120 s steps, scenes, input with rebinding and glyphs, pause menu, settings, saves, chip synth and music, rigs and moves, font, UI, Menu, Dialog, `grid` and `story` modules |\n150\t| my-3D2dge 3D engine | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (ES module) | `E.World3D`: level from text, things, mobs drawn as cards, pen, lights, fog, 3D particles, fixed camera, overlay pen |\n151\t| three.js | r182 (npm 0.182.0) | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js` and `three.tsl.min.js` (the core loads relative to them) | the renderer: WebGPU, falling back to WebGL 2 |\n152\t| Rapier | 0.19.3 | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs` (wasm inlined) | the 3D engine's physics, used for mobs only |\n153\t| AI Hub session client | 1.4.0 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | sessions (pasted unmodified) |\n154\t| Playwright | 1.63.0 | npm (Chromium 1243 is already installed here, in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`) | browser tests, the hub harness |\n155\t| Node | 24 | local | build and tests |\n156\t\n157\tThe game itself is plain JavaScript with no other libraries.\n158\t\n159\t### 3.2 Fetching (tested during planning; about 12 MB)\n160\t\n161\t```sh\n162\tcd <dev folder>\n163\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _ref/engine-src\n164\tgit -C _ref/engine-src sparse-checkout set --no-cone /dist/handoff/ /dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js /src/starter3d/ /vendor/ /docs/ENGINE-3D.md\n165\tmkdir -p hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n166\t  curl -fsS -o hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n167\tnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm i -D playwright@1.63.0\n168\t```\n169\t\n170\t- The planning spike used commit `4390a98` (2026-11-03); its `my-3d2dge.js` header reads v0.85.2. If HEAD has moved, check that both engine files still say v0.85.2. If they don't, pin that commit with `git fetch --depth 1 origin 4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, then check it out.\n171\t- Copy `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`; these are the copies you patch.\n172\t- `_ref/` is reference only: read the handoff docs, the kits, `src/starter3d/00-shell.js` and `70-crawler.js` there.\n173\t\n174\t### 3.3 Folder layout\n175\t\n176\t```\n177\t<root>/                                   TASK.md, STAGE.md, PLAN.md (given)\n178\t  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>.html     the deliverable (built)\n179\t  exit-interview.json\n180\t  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>/         the dev folder\n181\t    README.md            how to build, test and play\n182\t    PATCHES.md           every engine change and why\n183\t    CONTENT.md           the content formats for designers (maps, legends, wiring, text)\n184\t    package.json         { \"type\": \"module\" }, playwright 1.63.0\n185\t    engine/              my-3d2dge.js (2D, patched), my-3d2dge-3d.js (3D, patched)\n186\t    hub/                 session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\n187\t    src/                 the game; the files are concatenated in this order (see ORDER in tools/build.mjs)\n188\t      00-core.js         namespace B, constants, rng, events, tuning B.T (defaults and ranges)\n189\t      01-util.js         dice, grid helpers, direction maths, text helpers\n190\t      10-data-items.js  11-data-monsters.js  12-data-spells.js  13-data-classes.js\n191\t      14-data-text.js    every player-facing string: messages, inscriptions, descriptions, dialogue\n192\t      15-data-floors.js  four floors and the race floor: maps, features, wiring, items, monsters, events, walkthroughs\n193\t      16-data-music.js   songs, stings, sfx voices, voice profiles\n194\t      20-sim-state.js    new game, serialize and deserialize, hash\n195\t      21-sim-map.js      floor runtime, cell queries, E.Grid and E.Fog\n196\t      22-sim-wiring.js   triggers and actions\n197\t      23-sim-party.js    movement, formation, hunger, light\n198\t      24-sim-items.js    inventory, equipment, Hand, use, throw, put down\n199\t      25-sim-combat.js   hands, to-hit, damage, rows, projectiles, statuses\n200\t      26-sim-monsters.js AI, movement, attacks, telegraphs, drops\n201\t      27-sim-magic.js    slots, memorize, cast, spell effects\n202\t      28-sim-camp.js     rest, interruptions, eating\n203\t      29-sim-progress.js XP, levels, special quests, death and raising, NPC join and leave\n204\t      30-sim-boss.js     the Bolt-Holder's phases and attacks\n205\t      31-sim.js          B.Sim: update(dt, commands) -> events; the commands' dispatcher\n206\t      40-view3d.js       Presenter: builds a World3D per floor, mirrors state, consumes events\n207\t      41-view-things.js  THINGS: wall features, doors, floor items, decor\n208\t      42-view-creatures.js  creature recipes (cards and pen-drawn bodies), animation from state\n209\t      43-view-fx.js      spells, hits, telegraphs, first-person weapon overlay\n210\t      44-view-null.js    NullView for Node and tests\n211\t      50-audio.js        sfx, music director (layers), babble voice synth\n212\t      60-input.js        action map (pad, keys, mouse), Controls -> commands, idle detection\n213\t      61-ui-kit.js       panel, focus system, glyph badges, prompts, layout helpers\n214\t      62-ui-hud.js       party bar, log, compass, Hand, prompts, boss bar, race HUD\n215\t      63-ui-party.js     paperdoll, backpack, details, sheet\n216\t      64-ui-magic.js     cast menu, targeting\n217\t      65-ui-camp.js      camp, rest, memorize, pray, scribe, formation, eat\n218\t      66-ui-map.js       automap, journal, reader (notes)\n219\t      67-ui-dialog.js    E.Story and E.Dialog glue, choices, babble hookup\n220\t      68-ui-shell.js     title, creation, about and credits, game over, ending, touch notice, floor cards\n221\t      70-dev.js          Dev Mode\n222\t      71-attract.js      attract director and vignettes\n223\t      72-bot.js          walkthrough runner (tests, attract, dev auto-play)\n224\t      73-hub.js          AI Hub session, ghosts, race\n225\t      80-cutscenes.js    intro and ending stages and scripts\n226\t      90-main.js         E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n227\t    tools/build.mjs      bundles the single HTML\n228\t    tests/run.mjs        the one command; tests/*.test.mjs suites; tests/browser/*.mjs\n229\t    shots/               screenshots for review (not tests)\n230\t    _ref/                clones (not shipped)\n231\t```\n232\t\n233\t### 3.4 The build: one HTML file\n234\t\n235\t`node tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/` in ORDER into one IIFE and writes the root `.html` in this order:\n236\t\n237\t```html\n238\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n239\t<script>/* hub/session.js, byte for byte (the build checks its SHA-256 against hub/session.js) */</script>\n240\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n241\t<title>Eye of the Bolt-Holder</title>\n242\t<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">  <meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">  <meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n243\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n244\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n245\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest party out of the Rat Run wins\">\n246\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move WASD/stick · Turn Q E/d-pad · Use F/✕ · Strike Space/□\">\n247\t<script>window.__hubInfo = (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) ? AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;</script>\n248\t<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": {\n249\t  \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n250\t  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n251\t  \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n252\t  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }</script>\n253\t<style>/* as the 3D template: #stage, #view under #screen; black; no selection */</style></head><body>\n254\t<div id=\"e3-boot\">EYE OF THE BOLT-HOLDER · Starting…</div>\n255\t<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n256\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (classic) */</script>\n257\t<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js (module) */</script>\n258\t<script type=\"module\">/* the game: src/ concatenated, no import/export */</script>\n259\t</body></html>\n260\t```\n261\t\n262\t- Module scripts run in document order after the 3D engine's imports resolve, so the game sees `E.World3D`; this is the template's own ordering and the spike proved it.\n263\t- If `E.World3D` is missing (the CDN was unreachable), the game draws a boot error: \"The 3D libraries could not load. Check the connection and reload.\" It never shows a blank screen.\n264\t- Write the game code with no `import` or `export`, so Node's `vm` can run the same files as a script.\n265\t- The build prints the file's size, the session client's checksum, and a lint warning if a `src/` file in the sim layer mentions `world`, `World3D`, `document` or `audio`.\n266\t\n267\t### 3.5 Runtime layers\n268\t\n269\t```\n270\t pad / keys / mouse ─► Controls (60-input) ─┐\n271\t bot (tests, attract, dev) ─────────────────┼─► commands ─► SIM (20–31: pure, deterministic, JSON state) ─► events\n272\t hub ghost messages ─► ghosts (view only)   │                    ▲                                         │\n273\t UI screens (61–68) ─ read state, send ─────┘                    │                                         ▼\n274\t                                                     B.T tuning (Dev sliders)        Presenter (40–44) · Audio (50) · HUD feedback\n275\t```\n276\t\n277\t- **Sim.** It owns all state (`B.S`), advances on `sim.update(dt, cmds)` in each fixed step using the scaled `dt`, and pushes events (`B.S.events`, cleared each step). It never touches `world`, the DOM, audio or `Math.random`; all randomness comes from `B.rng(stream)`. It runs the same in Node.\n278\t- **Presenter.** It builds one `E.World3D` per floor and mirrors the sim every frame:\n279\t  - It draws the camera pose from the party tween, doors from their open values, items, monsters' positions and animations, and lights.\n280\t  - It turns events into one-shot effects: sparks, damage numbers, shakes, flashes.\n281\t  - It never writes sim state.\n282\t  - In Node it is `NullView`.\n283\t- **UI.** Drawn on the 2D overlay (`r.overlay`, `E.ui`, `E.font`) over the clear canvas (`bg: null`).\n284\t  - It changes the game only through commands.\n285\t  - It animates on unscaled real time, so it stays snappy while the world is slowed.\n286\t- **Audio.** Driven by events (sfx), by state (music layers) and by dialogue (babble).\n287\t- **Commands** are plain JSON objects, so the bot, the attract mode, the tests and the hub race all drive the game the same way:\n288\t\n289\t  | Family | Commands |\n290\t  |---|---|\n291\t  | Movement | `{ c: 'move', d: 'f'\\|'b'\\|'l'\\|'r' }`, `{ c: 'turn', s: -1\\|1 }` |\n292\t  | World | `{ c: 'use' }`, `{ c: 'putDown' }`, `{ c: 'throw' }`, `{ c: 'examine' }` |\n293\t  | Combat | `{ c: 'strike' }`, `{ c: 'hand', m, h: 'main'\\|'off' }`, `{ c: 'cast', m, spell, target }` |\n294\t  | Inventory | `{ c: 'inv', op: 'take'\\|'place'\\|'swap'\\|'use'\\|'drop', ref }` |\n295\t  | Camp | `{ c: 'camp', op: 'rest'\\|'stopRest'\\|'memorize'\\|'forget'\\|'scribe'\\|'eat'\\|'swapRows', … }` |\n296\t  | Party | `{ c: 'select', m }`, `{ c: 'dismiss', m }` |\n297\t\n298\t### 3.6 State (also the save format; version 1)\n299\t\n300\t```js\n301\tS = { v: 1, seed, rng: { combat, ai, loot, world },   // 32-bit states of a small PRNG (mulberry32), one per stream\n302\t  time, playTime, floor: 'f1', diff: 'normal',\n303\t  party: { cx, cy, dir, tween: { from:[x,y,yaw], t, dur, kind } | null, queued: cmd|null, hand: itemRef|null, selected: 0,\n304\t           members: [ member×≤6 ], formation: [i0..i5], light: 1, floating: 0, seeing: 0, steady: 0, freeHinge: 0 },\n305\t  member = { id, name, race, cls, look, lvl, xp, hp, max, food, stats: { str, dex, con, int, wis }, state: 'ok'|'down'|'dead',\n306\t             status: { poison, held, … (seconds left) }, slots: { head, neck, body, arms, main, off, ring1, ring2, feet, quiver },\n307\t             pack: [12], book: [spellIds], prep: { 1: [ids], 2: [...], 3: [...] }, slotsUsed: {1, 2, 3}, cool: { main, off },\n308\t             ward: 0, layOnHands: 1, npc: null|'tamsin'|'durgan'|'veil', voice },\n309\t  floors: { f1: { doors: { id: { open, locked } }, plates: { id: down }, levers: { id: on }, items: { 'x,y': [itemRef] },\n310\t                  alcoves: { id: [itemRef] }, monsters: [ mon ], fog: '<base64 bitset>', flags: {}, wanderers, visited }, … },\n311\t  mon = { id, type, cx, cy, slot, hp, max, state, timer, wind, aware, status: {}, dir, drop },\n312\t  itemRef = { id, n?, charges?, rust?, uid },\n313\t  flags: {}, story: E.Story state, quests: [...], special: { sq1, sq2, sq3 }, notes: [ids], log: [last 100],\n314\t  stats: { kills, secrets, deaths, potions, rests, steps } }\n315\t```\n316\t\n317\t- **Saves.** `game.saves.write = () => ({ data: S, place: floorName, level: party's top level })`; `read` rebuilds the sim, then the presenter. The save version is migrated through `menu.saves.migrate`.\n318\t- **Determinism.** `B.hash(S)` is a 32-bit FNV of a stable JSON of the state without `events`. The same seed and the same command script must give the same hash (§14).\n319\t\n320\t### 3.7 Scenes and flow\n321\t\n322\t| Scene | What it is |\n323\t|---|---|\n324\t| `boot` | the engine boot screen: `W3.init`, building the title stage, the worlds of floors 1–4 and the race, `prepare()`. In a hub session only the race world is built. |\n325\t| `title` | the menu; the attract mode starts after 10 s idle |\n326\t| `attract` | the demo vignettes |\n327\t| `create` | the party of four |\n328\t| `cutscene` | the intro and the ending stages |\n329\t| `play` | the dungeon, with overlays: party, cast, camp, map, journal, reader, dialogue, dev |\n330\t| `floorcard` | the fade on stairs and the floor's name |\n331\t| `over` | game over |\n332\t| `ending` | the end, stats and credits |\n333\t| `about` | About and Credits |\n334\t| `race` | the Rat Run, solo or in a hub session |\n335\t| `hubcard` | the session's own card |\n336\t\n337\t- Overlays are states of `play`, not scenes, so the 3D view keeps drawing behind them.\n338\t- While a party, cast or camp overlay is open, `game.timeScale` is set by the Settings → *Time in menus* option: 0 (pause), 0.25 (slow, the default) or 1 (real time).\n339\t- Dialogue always pauses the world. The engine's pause menu (Options or Esc) pauses fully.\n340\t\n341\t### 3.8 Units and conventions\n342\t\n343\t- **Cells.** A cell is 32 world units (2 m) and is written (cx, cy); its centre is (cx·32+16, cy·32+16). The level text has 2×2 tiles per cell, as the kit has.\n344\t- **Directions.** Direction `dir` is 0 for N, 1 for E, 2 for S and 3 for W; `DX = [0, 1, 0, -1]`, `DY = [-1, 0, 1, 0]`; yaw = −π/2 + dir·π/2.\n345\t- **Heights.** The eye is at z 25 and the camera sits 4 units behind the cell centre (as in the kit). Walls are 48 tall, and the ceiling is a slab at z 50 drawn by the pen.\n346\t- **Wall features** are addressed by **the open cell you stand in and the way you face**: `(x, y, 'N')` means the feature on the wall north of (x, y). The thing is drawn on that wall's face, half a cell from the centre.\n347\t- **Monster slots in a cell.** Four quadrants (NW, NE, SW, SE) for small monsters, two halves for medium ones and the centre for large ones, drawn 8 units from the centre.\n348\t\n349\t### 3.9 Engine changes to make (log them in PATCHES.md)\n350\t\n351\t1. **Button symbols.** Default to `'playstation'`, offer only PlayStation and Xbox, and label the setting \"Button symbols\". Patch the `glyphs` entry in the 2D engine's standard settings.\n352\t2. **Pause items.** Make `menu.items` accept a function, so a session hides Dev Mode, Load and Quit. Check `v()` in the pause builder first; patch it if it doesn't call functions.\n353\t3. **Audio.**\n354\t   - Add a public `audio.bus(name)` returning the `sfx`, `music` or new `voice` gain node.\n355\t   - Add a \"Voices\" volume setting.\n356\t   - Add `sfx(name, { pan })` through a StereoPannerNode, used for flank and rear hits and for monster sounds by bearing.\n357\t4. **Beards.** Add `beard: 'full' | 'braided' | null` to `E.Humanoid`, drawn in the hair colour over the jaw in `draw` and `drawPortrait`. Dwarves need beards.\n358\t5. **Touch.** Make sure `input.touchEnabled = false` hides every touch button and the touch stick while keeping the top-right menu button (§5.7).\n359\t6. Anything else you need, kept small. For example, if `world.box` with a side style does not match the level's walls, a `world.wallBlock(cx, cy, …)` helper (§13, risk 3).\n360\t\n361\t---\n362\t\n363\t## 4. Systems and mechanics\n364\t\n365\tAll the numbers below are defaults in `B.T` (tuning). The **★ ones are Dev Mode sliders** (§8). Difficulty multiplies on top: Easy has monster damage ×0.7, monster HP ×0.8 and hunger ×0.5; Hard has monster damage ×1.3, monster HP ×1.25 and rest interruptions ×2.\n366\t\n367\t### 4.1 Movement and the grid\n368\t\n369\t- **The commands.**\n370\t  - Forward, back, strafe left and strafe right move one cell."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"126d5a9b-8a69-4c48-b862-ecdcb0276918","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:53:37.195Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Eye of the Bolt-Holder\n\nA build plan for a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by **Eye of the Beholder** (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991). It is built on the my-3D2dge engine and ships as one HTML file. It is written for the developers (human or AI) who will build the demo from TASK.md and this file alone.\n\n- **Title.** I brainstormed 50 playful riffs on the source's name and picked one with a real RNG (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)` returned index 5): **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**.\n- **File-name forms.** GameTitle = `EyeOfTheBoltHolder`, SOURCE = `EyeOfTheBeholder1991`. Fill these into your TASK.md's bracket pattern, for example `EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<timestamp>`.\n- **Pitch.** Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks, is being locked in from below. Doors bolt themselves, wells cap shut, and a granary will not open for axes. The Council of Keys sends a party of four (later up to six) down through the Drains, the Tumbler Halls of the dead locksmiths' guild, the Gloam Deeps of the eye-cult and, at last, the Eye's Vault. The party moves one cell at a time in real time: it swings each hand as it comes off cooldown, memorizes spells at camp, eats rations, weighs plates with rocks and finds the loose stone. At the bottom it turns three Great Locks to strip the cage from **the Bolt-Holder**, a floating amber Eye held in a cage of seven iron bolts that hurls lightning, gazes joints stiff and rains iron.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Ground rules for the builders\n\n1. **TASK.md wins on deliverables.** Names, folders, the single HTML, the About screen, the reply and `exit-interview.json` follow your TASK.md exactly. This plan says how; §11 has the templates.\n2. **Folders.** Your dev folder is the bracket name; the bundled `.html` and `exit-interview.json` go in the root beside TASK.md.\n   - Keep every clone, cache and `node_modules` inside your dev folder (in `_ref/` and similar). `/tmp` on this machine is a shared tmpfs and it was full during planning (a `git clone` into it failed with \"No space left on device\").\n   - Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need. Other agents share the machine.\n3. **Processes and ports.**\n   - Prefer `file://` and headless Node.\n   - When you need a server (only the AI Hub harness does), bind `127.0.0.1` on port 0 and read the port it gets. Check that the page is your game before trusting a result.\n   - Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never use pkill, killall or `fuser -k`.\n4. **Tests.** One command runs every test and prints only failures plus a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can't check. Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n5. **No stubs.** Everything in this plan is required unless it is marked *optional*. If time runs short, cut quantity (fewer side items, smaller side rooms), never a system or a floor. There must be no TODOs, placeholders or dead menu entries.\n6. **Engine changes are allowed.** Keep each one small and list it in `PATCHES.md` (what and why). The About screen and the Engine Feedback answers draw on it.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research summary\n\n### 1.1 The source, and what we keep\n\n**Confirmed facts** (Giant Bomb, MobyGames, oldgames.sk):\n- Eye of the Beholder was developed by Westwood Associates and published by SSI in 1991, for MS-DOS and almost at once for the Amiga.\n- A party of four created heroes is joined by up to two recruitable NPCs.\n- The Lords of Waterdeep send the party into the sewers, and the way out is sealed behind them.\n- At the bottom waits the beholder crime lord Xanathar.\n\n**What made it distinctive** (from knowledge of the game, not re-verified), and how this plan uses each part:\n\n| Source trait | How we turn it into our game |\n|---|---|\n| First person on a grid, 90° turns, everything in real time | Step, turn and strafe one cell at a time. Monsters act on their own clocks, so there are no turns. |\n| Party of 4 to 6 in rows; the front two melee; the back rows need reach, missiles or spells | Formation of three rows of two, with reach weapons in row 2 and missiles, thrown weapons and spells from anywhere (§4.6) |\n| Every hand is an action; it greys out on cooldown; damage shows on the portraits | Two hand slots on every party card, each with a cooldown wipe and damage pops (§5.2) |\n| Vancian magic: mages learn from scrolls into a spellbook and memorize; clerics pray; both at camp | Camp menu with Rest, Memorize, Pray and Scribe (§4.8, §4.9) |\n| Paperdoll and backpack; the item held on the cursor is thrown, placed and dropped; food and rations | The party's **Hand** (the held item), throwing, putting down and alcoves; a food meter (§4.5, §4.9) |\n| Puzzle vocabulary: pressure plates held down by items, levers, buttons, keys of many shapes, illusory walls, loose-stone switches, teleporters, spinners, pits to the level below, wall niches, inscriptions, dart traps, hidden \"special quests\" | All of them, wired as data (§4.2). Floors 1 to 3 each have one hidden deed (special quest). |\n| Monster groups share a cell; the sidestep \"dance\" | Cell capacity by size. Attack wind-ups that a step can dodge make sidestepping an intended skill (§4.7). |\n| Terse second-person messages, a stately intro, formal NPCs, a few moral choices | Style guide and scripts (§7.2, §7.3), plus Veil's choice on floor 3 |\n\n**What we change:**\n- The world and characters are original: no Waterdeep, Forgotten Realms, Xanathar, beholders or AD&D rule text.\n- The UI is controller-first for a TV at 10 feet.\n- Steps are tweened, and the 3D view fills the screen with dynamic light and shadow.\n- Monster attacks are telegraphed.\n- Music has combat layers, and voices are babble.\n- Hints escalate, so no puzzle can strand the player.\n\n### 1.2 The engine (my-3D2dge v0.85.2)\n\n- **The handoff** (`dist/handoff/`) is the 2D engine. `my-3d2dge.js` is readable and includes every module, among them `grid` (E.Grid, E.Fog), `story` (E.Story) and `rpg`. It also holds the docs (API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md), the tools (`tools/check.mjs`, `filmstrip.mjs`, `replay.mjs`, `asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`), 22 2D kits and a template game whose `game/test.mjs` shows how to test without a browser.\n- **The 3D engine.** The handoff's genre playbook sends \"First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder)\" to the 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera and the **3D CRAWLER** kit. That engine is not in the handoff folder but sits in the same repository: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, v0.85.2, an ES module whose header is its manual. It uses three.js r182 and Rapier 0.19.3. It attaches `E.World3D`, `E.THREE`, `E.TSL` and `E.RAPIER` to the 2D engine, which still runs the loop, the scenes, the input, the menus, the sound and the rigs.\n- **The crawler slice** (`src/starter3d/70-crawler.js`, 561 lines) already does most of the core loop:\n  - cells of 2×2 tiles with tweened steps, turns, strafes and a bump lurch;\n  - a fixed camera at eye height 25;\n  - a party of four on cards with `rig.drawPortrait`;\n  - gates (`world.thing('door')`, one with an iron lock), a lever `THING`, plates and a dart trap;\n  - monsters walked on `E.Grid` paths, an `E.Fog` automap and a bolt projectile;\n  - real time via `E.Turns 'atb'`, and a bot.\n\n  Use it as the reference for the camera, the tweens and the things. Restructure the rest: the kit mixes rules with the 3D world, and we separate them (§3.5).\n- **Spike run during planning.** I took the repository's `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-crawler.html` and pointed its import map at jsDelivr: `three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` and `@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs`. These are byte-identical to the vendored copies by SHA-256. Opened from `file://` in headless Chromium 1243 (Playwright 1.63.0) with SwiftShader WebGL 2 (`?backend=webgl#crawler`):\n  - it booted with no console errors;\n  - `CRAWLER.run(600)` gave the same hash twice (`e3880e33`);\n  - `CRAWLER.bot()` won: lever, key, unlock, plate, Warden and stair, all four alive.\n\n  The native look is chunky pixel 3D with brick walls, flagstone floors and live portrait cards. **So the core technical bet works: one HTML file, opened from disk, with the engines inlined and three.js and Rapier from a CDN.**\n- **Engine names this plan relies on** (look up details in API-INDEX.md and the 3D header):\n\n  | Area | Names |\n  |---|---|\n  | Game and scenes | `new E.Game({ canvas, res: 'ps1', expand: true, bg: null, input, menu })`, `game.start({ scenes, scene, boot: { steps } })`, `game.go`, `game.advance(s)`, `game.debug` |\n  | Timing and scripts | `game.timeScale`, `game.script` (coroutines), `game.tween`, `game.after` |\n  | Input | `game.input` (`pressed`, `down`, `tap`, `hold`, `press(code)`, `clicked()`, `pointer()`, `padAxes`, `lastDevice`, `label(action)`, `glyphs`) |\n  | Settings and saves | `game.settings.define`, `game.saves` (3 slots plus autosave), `menu: { items, settings, saves }` |\n  | Sound | `game.audio` (`define`, `sfx`, `music`, `layer`, `sting`, `duck`, `ctx`) |\n  | Text, UI and rigs | `E.Dialog` (`talk.shown`, `talk.voice`), `E.Story`, `E.Menu`, `E.ui.box/bar`, `E.font.text/title`, `E.Humanoid` / `E.Blob` with `rig.drawPortrait`, `E.MOVES` |\n  | Grid module | `E.Grid` (`path`), `E.Fog` |\n  | 3D world | `new E.World3D(game, { level, … })`, `world.thing(kind, …)`, `E.World3D.THINGS.<kind>` (pen-drawn things), `world.mob`, `world.pen`, `world.glow`, `world.light`, `world.arc`, `world.project`, `world.camera` (`'fixed'`), `overlay: { fov }` plus `world.overlay.pen` (first-person hands), `world.prepare()`, `world.dispose()` |\n  | 3D settings | `E.World3D.SETTINGS` (cards or puppets, picture resolution, shadows, filter) |\n\n### 1.3 AI Hub (Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0)\n\n- **Modes.** `parallel`, `ghost` (each copy runs its own round and shows the others' characters when they are in the same area) and `shared-world`.\n- **The client.** `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. In a session it seeds `Math.random`, gives the page its own in-memory storage and hands fullscreen to the hub.\n- **Tags.** `hub:session`, `hub:session-players`, `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal` (at most 60 characters), `hub:session-howto` (at most 80) and `hub:session-minutes`.\n- **The flow.** `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` returns `info`, or `null` when there is no hub (then the game plays on its own). `AIHub.ready()` is called once, with no key press. The round starts on `start`, and play begins when `AIHub.time() >= 0`.\n- **Reporting.**\n  - `progress({ score, label })` is taken about once a second.\n  - `result({ time | score })` is sent once.\n  - On `time-up` the game reports within `graceMs`.\n  - On `end` input and sound stop.\n- **Messages.** `send(data, { reliable })` takes JSON of at most 16 KB, at most 20 a second.\n- **Rules.**\n  - The network goes only through the hub.\n  - There are no dialogs or popups, and no quitting or returning to the title in a session.\n  - Names come from the hub and never become HTML.\n  - Options is ignored while Create is held.\n- **The test.** The harness (`harness.html` plus `bridge.js` and `round.js`, served beside the game) passes the game when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. Run it with `?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`, then with `players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1` and `&scenario=host-leave`.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game at a glance\n\n- **Genre.** A real-time, first-person, grid-based party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles.\n- **Perspective.**\n  - First person at the party's eyes on a 4-way grid, with tweened steps and quarter turns.\n  - A top-down parchment automap screen.\n  - Full-screen overlay screens (party, camp, map), drawn over the dimmed 3D view.\n- **Pillars.**\n  1. *Every cell is a decision.* Step, turn and sidestep in real time.\n  2. *Every hand is a weapon.* Per-hand cooldowns and formation rows.\n  3. *Every lock has a key, and some keys are rocks.* Physical puzzles built from a small, consistent vocabulary.\n  4. *A party you care about.* Live portraits, hunger, levels, deaths and NPCs with voices.\n- **Length.** Four floors plus a race floor, about 50 to 75 minutes for a first play of the campaign. Every system appears by the end of floor 2, and floors 3 and 4 deepen them.\n- **Modes.**\n  - Campaign: New Game, Continue and Load.\n  - **Rat Run**: a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n  - **AI Hub ghost race** (the Rat Run, 1 to 4 players).\n  - Attract demo.\n- **The player can:**\n  - move, strafe and turn;\n  - fight with any hand, fire crossbows and bows, throw anything, and cast 9 arcane spells and 9 prayers;\n  - read and scribe scrolls, zap wands;\n  - equip 10 body slots and a 12-slot backpack per member;\n  - pick up, put down, throw and place items in alcoves;\n  - open doors, pull levers, press buttons and loose stones, weigh plates, insert keys and wards, and pick locks or disarm traps (thieves);\n  - cross pits (Feather Step), see secrets (Find Seams, goggles), rest, eat, memorize and pray;\n  - level up, raise the dead, recruit and dismiss NPCs, make one moral choice, and save and load anywhere out of combat.\n- **The player cannot:** jump, look freely (the view is grid-locked), walk diagonally, buy things (there are no shops or gold), or cross pits without the spell.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack (pinned)\n\n| Piece | Version | Where from | Used for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| my-3D2dge 2D engine (full, readable) | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` | the loop of exact 1/120 s steps, scenes, input with rebinding and glyphs, pause menu, settings, saves, chip synth and music, rigs and moves, font, UI, Menu, Dialog, `grid` and `story` modules |\n| my-3D2dge 3D engine | v0.85.2 | repo `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` (ES module) | `E.World3D`: level from text, things, mobs drawn as cards, pen, lights, fog, 3D particles, fixed camera, overlay pen |\n| three.js | r182 (npm 0.182.0) | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js` and `three.tsl.min.js` (the core loads relative to them) | the renderer: WebGPU, falling back to WebGL 2 |\n| Rapier | 0.19.3 | `https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs` (wasm inlined) | the 3D engine's physics, used for mobs only |\n| AI Hub session client | 1.4.0 | `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` | sessions (pasted unmodified) |\n| Playwright | 1.63.0 | npm (Chromium 1243 is already installed here, in `~/.cache/ms-playwright`) | browser tests, the hub harness |\n| Node | 24 | local | build and tests |\n\nThe game itself is plain JavaScript with no other libraries.\n\n### 3.2 Fetching (tested during planning; about 12 MB)\n\n```sh\ncd <dev folder>\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git _ref/engine-src\ngit -C _ref/engine-src sparse-checkout set --no-cone /dist/handoff/ /dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js /src/starter3d/ /vendor/ /docs/ENGINE-3D.md\nmkdir -p hub && for f in README.md session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js; do\n  curl -fsS -o hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\nnpm init -y >/dev/null && npm i -D playwright@1.63.0\n```\n\n- The planning spike used commit `4390a98` (2026-11-03); its `my-3d2dge.js` header reads v0.85.2. If HEAD has moved, check that both engine files still say v0.85.2. If they don't, pin that commit with `git fetch --depth 1 origin 4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72`, then check it out.\n- Copy `dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge.js` and `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` to `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`; these are the copies you patch.\n- `_ref/` is reference only: read the handoff docs, the kits, `src/starter3d/00-shell.js` and `70-crawler.js` there.\n\n### 3.3 Folder layout\n\n```\n<root>/                                   TASK.md, STAGE.md, PLAN.md (given)\n  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>.html     the deliverable (built)\n  exit-interview.json\n  EyeOfTheBoltHolder-EyeOfTheBeholder1991-<model>-<stamp>/         the dev folder\n    README.md            how to build, test and play\n    PATCHES.md           every engine change and why\n    CONTENT.md           the content formats for designers (maps, legends, wiring, text)\n    package.json         { \"type\": \"module\" }, playwright 1.63.0\n    engine/              my-3d2dge.js (2D, patched), my-3d2dge-3d.js (3D, patched)\n    hub/                 session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md\n    src/                 the game; the files are concatenated in this order (see ORDER in tools/build.mjs)\n      00-core.js         namespace B, constants, rng, events, tuning B.T (defaults and ranges)\n      01-util.js         dice, grid helpers, direction maths, text helpers\n      10-data-items.js  11-data-monsters.js  12-data-spells.js  13-data-classes.js\n      14-data-text.js    every player-facing string: messages, inscriptions, descriptions, dialogue\n      15-data-floors.js  four floors and the race floor: maps, features, wiring, items, monsters, events, walkthroughs\n      16-data-music.js   songs, stings, sfx voices, voice profiles\n      20-sim-state.js    new game, serialize and deserialize, hash\n      21-sim-map.js      floor runtime, cell queries, E.Grid and E.Fog\n      22-sim-wiring.js   triggers and actions\n      23-sim-party.js    movement, formation, hunger, light\n      24-sim-items.js    inventory, equipment, Hand, use, throw, put down\n      25-sim-combat.js   hands, to-hit, damage, rows, projectiles, statuses\n      26-sim-monsters.js AI, movement, attacks, telegraphs, drops\n      27-sim-magic.js    slots, memorize, cast, spell effects\n      28-sim-camp.js     rest, interruptions, eating\n      29-sim-progress.js XP, levels, special quests, death and raising, NPC join and leave\n      30-sim-boss.js     the Bolt-Holder's phases and attacks\n      31-sim.js          B.Sim: update(dt, commands) -> events; the commands' dispatcher\n      40-view3d.js       Presenter: builds a World3D per floor, mirrors state, consumes events\n      41-view-things.js  THINGS: wall features, doors, floor items, decor\n      42-view-creatures.js  creature recipes (cards and pen-drawn bodies), animation from state\n      43-view-fx.js      spells, hits, telegraphs, first-person weapon overlay\n      44-view-null.js    NullView for Node and tests\n      50-audio.js        sfx, music director (layers), babble voice synth\n      60-input.js        action map (pad, keys, mouse), Controls -> commands, idle detection\n      61-ui-kit.js       panel, focus system, glyph badges, prompts, layout helpers\n      62-ui-hud.js       party bar, log, compass, Hand, prompts, boss bar, race HUD\n      63-ui-party.js     paperdoll, backpack, details, sheet\n      64-ui-magic.js     cast menu, targeting\n      65-ui-camp.js      camp, rest, memorize, pray, scribe, formation, eat\n      66-ui-map.js       automap, journal, reader (notes)\n      67-ui-dialog.js    E.Story and E.Dialog glue, choices, babble hookup\n      68-ui-shell.js     title, creation, about and credits, game over, ending, touch notice, floor cards\n      70-dev.js          Dev Mode\n      71-attract.js      attract director and vignettes\n      72-bot.js          walkthrough runner (tests, attract, dev auto-play)\n      73-hub.js          AI Hub session, ghosts, race\n      80-cutscenes.js    intro and ending stages and scripts\n      90-main.js         E.Game, boot steps, scenes, game.debug\n    tools/build.mjs      bundles the single HTML\n    tests/run.mjs        the one command; tests/*.test.mjs suites; tests/browser/*.mjs\n    shots/               screenshots for review (not tests)\n    _ref/                clones (not shipped)\n```\n\n### 3.4 The build: one HTML file\n\n`node tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/` in ORDER into one IIFE and writes the root `.html` in this order:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* hub/session.js, byte for byte (the build checks its SHA-256 against hub/session.js) */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<title>Eye of the Bolt-Holder</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"…\">  <meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">  <meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">  <meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Fastest party out of the Rat Run wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move WASD/stick · Turn Q E/d-pad · Use F/✕ · Strike Space/□\">\n<script>window.__hubInfo = (window.AIHub && AIHub.active) ? AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true }) : null;</script>\n<script type=\"importmap\">{ \"imports\": {\n  \"three\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/tsl\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }</script>\n<style>/* as the 3D template: #stage, #view under #screen; black; no selection */</style></head><body>\n<div id=\"e3-boot\">EYE OF THE BOLT-HOLDER · Starting…</div>\n<div id=\"stage\"><canvas id=\"view\"></canvas><canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas></div>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (classic) */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">/* engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js (module) */</script>\n<script type=\"module\">/* the game: src/ concatenated, no import/export */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\n- Module scripts run in document order after the 3D engine's imports resolve, so the game sees `E.World3D`; this is the template's own ordering and the spike proved it.\n- If `E.World3D` is missing (the CDN was unreachable), the game draws a boot error: \"The 3D libraries could not load. Check the connection and reload.\" It never shows a blank screen.\n- Write the game code with no `import` or `export`, so Node's `vm` can run the same files as a script.\n- The build prints the file's size, the session client's checksum, and a lint warning if a `src/` file in the sim layer mentions `world`, `World3D`, `document` or `audio`.\n\n### 3.5 Runtime layers\n\n```\n pad / keys / mouse ─► Controls (60-input) ─┐\n bot (tests, attract, dev) ─────────────────┼─► commands ─► SIM (20–31: pure, deterministic, JSON state) ─► events\n hub ghost messages ─► ghosts (view only)   │                    ▲                                         │\n UI screens (61–68) ─ read state, send ─────┘                    │                                         ▼\n                                                     B.T tuning (Dev sliders)        Presenter (40–44) · Audio (50) · HUD feedback\n```\n\n- **Sim.** It owns all state (`B.S`), advances on `sim.update(dt, cmds)` in each fixed step using the scaled `dt`, and pushes events (`B.S.events`, cleared each step). It never touches `world`, the DOM, audio or `Math.random`; all randomness comes from `B.rng(stream)`. It runs the same in Node.\n- **Presenter.** It builds one `E.World3D` per floor and mirrors the sim every frame:\n  - It draws the camera pose from the party tween, doors from their open values, items, monsters' positions and animations, and lights.\n  - It turns events into one-shot effects: sparks, damage numbers, shakes, flashes.\n  - It never writes sim state.\n  - In Node it is `NullView`.\n- **UI.** Drawn on the 2D overlay (`r.overlay`, `E.ui`, `E.font`) over the clear canvas (`bg: null`).\n  - It changes the game only through commands.\n  - It animates on unscaled real time, so it stays snappy while the world is slowed.\n- **Audio.** Driven by events (sfx), by state (music layers) and by dialogue (babble).\n- **Commands** are plain JSON objects, so the bot, the attract mode, the tests and the hub race all drive the game the same way:\n\n  | Family | Commands |\n  |---|---|\n  | Movement | `{ c: 'move', d: 'f'\\|'b'\\|'l'\\|'r' }`, `{ c: 'turn', s: -1\\|1 }` |\n  | World | `{ c: 'use' }`, `{ c: 'putDown' }`, `{ c: 'throw' }`, `{ c: 'examine' }` |\n  | Combat | `{ c: 'strike' }`, `{ c: 'hand', m, h: 'main'\\|'off' }`, `{ c: 'cast', m, spell, target }` |\n  | Inventory | `{ c: 'inv', op: 'take'\\|'place'\\|'swap'\\|'use'\\|'drop', ref }` |\n  | Camp | `{ c: 'camp', op: 'rest'\\|'stopRest'\\|'memorize'\\|'forget'\\|'scribe'\\|'eat'\\|'swapRows', … }` |\n  | Party | `{ c: 'select', m }`, `{ c: 'dismiss', m }` |\n\n### 3.6 State (also the save format; version 1)\n\n```js\nS = { v: 1, seed, rng: { combat, ai, loot, world },   // 32-bit states of a small PRNG (mulberry32), one per stream\n  time, playTime, floor: 'f1', diff: 'normal',\n  party: { cx, cy, dir, tween: { from:[x,y,yaw], t, dur, kind } | null, queued: cmd|null, hand: itemRef|null, selected: 0,\n           members: [ member×≤6 ], formation: [i0..i5], light: 1, floating: 0, seeing: 0, steady: 0, freeHinge: 0 },\n  member = { id, name, race, cls, look, lvl, xp, hp, max, food, stats: { str, dex, con, int, wis }, state: 'ok'|'down'|'dead',\n             status: { poison, held, … (seconds left) }, slots: { head, neck, body, arms, main, off, ring1, ring2, feet, quiver },\n             pack: [12], book: [spellIds], prep: { 1: [ids], 2: [...], 3: [...] }, slotsUsed: {1, 2, 3}, cool: { main, off },\n             ward: 0, layOnHands: 1, npc: null|'tamsin'|'durgan'|'veil', voice },\n  floors: { f1: { doors: { id: { open, locked } }, plates: { id: down }, levers: { id: on }, items: { 'x,y': [itemRef] },\n                  alcoves: { id: [itemRef] }, monsters: [ mon ], fog: '<base64 bitset>', flags: {}, wanderers, visited }, … },\n  mon = { id, type, cx, cy, slot, hp, max, state, timer, wind, aware, status: {}, dir, drop },\n  itemRef = { id, n?, charges?, rust?, uid },\n  flags: {}, story: E.Story state, quests: [...], special: { sq1, sq2, sq3 }, notes: [ids], log: [last 100],\n  stats: { kills, secrets, deaths, potions, rests, steps } }\n```\n\n- **Saves.** `game.saves.write = () => ({ data: S, place: floorName, level: party's top level })`; `read` rebuilds the sim, then the presenter. The save version is migrated through `menu.saves.migrate`.\n- **Determinism.** `B.hash(S)` is a 32-bit FNV of a stable JSON of the state without `events`. The same seed and the same command script must give the same hash (§14).\n\n### 3.7 Scenes and flow\n\n| Scene | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `boot` | the engine boot screen: `W3.init`, building the title stage, the worlds of floors 1–4 and the race, `prepare()`. In a hub session only the race world is built. |\n| `title` | the menu; the attract mode starts after 10 s idle |\n| `attract` | the demo vignettes |\n| `create` | the party of four |\n| `cutscene` | the intro and the ending stages |\n| `play` | the dungeon, with overlays: party, cast, camp, map, journal, reader, dialogue, dev |\n| `floorcard` | the fade on stairs and the floor's name |\n| `over` | game over |\n| `ending` | the end, stats and credits |\n| `about` | About and Credits |\n| `race` | the Rat Run, solo or in a hub session |\n| `hubcard` | the session's own card |\n\n- Overlays are states of `play`, not scenes, so the 3D view keeps drawing behind them.\n- While a party, cast or camp overlay is open, `game.timeScale` is set by the Settings → *Time in menus* option: 0 (pause), 0.25 (slow, the default) or 1 (real time).\n- Dialogue always pauses the world. The engine's pause menu (Options or Esc) pauses fully.\n\n### 3.8 Units and conventions\n\n- **Cells.** A cell is 32 world units (2 m) and is written (cx, cy); its centre is (cx·32+16, cy·32+16). The level text has 2×2 tiles per cell, as the kit has.\n- **Directions.** Direction `dir` is 0 for N, 1 for E, 2 for S and 3 for W; `DX = [0, 1, 0, -1]`, `DY = [-1, 0, 1, 0]`; yaw = −π/2 + dir·π/2.\n- **Heights.** The eye is at z 25 and the camera sits 4 units behind the cell centre (as in the kit). Walls are 48 tall, and the ceiling is a slab at z 50 drawn by the pen.\n- **Wall features** are addressed by **the open cell you stand in and the way you face**: `(x, y, 'N')` means the feature on the wall north of (x, y). The thing is drawn on that wall's face, half a cell from the centre.\n- **Monster slots in a cell.** Four quadrants (NW, NE, SW, SE) for small monsters, two halves for medium ones and the centre for large ones, drawn 8 units from the centre.\n\n### 3.9 Engine changes to make (log them in PATCHES.md)\n\n1. **Button symbols.** Default to `'playstation'`, offer only PlayStation and Xbox, and label the setting \"Button symbols\". Patch the `glyphs` entry in the 2D engine's standard settings.\n2. **Pause items.** Make `menu.items` accept a function, so a session hides Dev Mode, Load and Quit. Check `v()` in the pause builder first; patch it if it doesn't call functions.\n3. **Audio.**\n   - Add a public `audio.bus(name)` returning the `sfx`, `music` or new `voice` gain node.\n   - Add a \"Voices\" volume setting.\n   - Add `sfx(name, { pan })` through a StereoPannerNode, used for flank and rear hits and for monster sounds by bearing.\n4. **Beards.** Add `beard: 'full' | 'braided' | null` to `E.Humanoid`, drawn in the hair colour over the jaw in `draw` and `drawPortrait`. Dwarves need beards.\n5. **Touch.** Make sure `input.touchEnabled = false` hides every touch button and the touch stick while keeping the top-right menu button (§5.7).\n6. Anything else you need, kept small. For example, if `world.box` with a side style does not match the level's walls, a `world.wallBlock(cx, cy, …)` helper (§13, risk 3).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Systems and mechanics\n\nAll the numbers below are defaults in `B.T` (tuning). The **★ ones are Dev Mode sliders** (§8). Difficulty multiplies on top: Easy has monster damage ×0.7, monster HP ×0.8 and hunger ×0.5; Hard has monster damage ×1.3, monster HP ×1.25 and rest interruptions ×2.\n\n### 4.1 Movement and the grid\n\n- **The commands.**\n  - Forward, back, strafe left and strafe right move one cell.","numLines":370,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2145}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01CMETCm8XcpNgnn4B1p5Bc6","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh43dW75YGB7SdzjmB3c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0149xGXeVTuxv77vKrBh65qh","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","offset":370,"limit":380},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1783,"cache_read_input_tokens":22513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1783},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"1a68670a-e17f-4129-93ef-a06050f1c2ce","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:53:37.948Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh43dJDDGDA4WDgEicM9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0149xGXeVTuxv77vKrBh65qh":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","offset":370,"limit":380}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0149xGXeVTuxv77vKrBh65qh","type":"tool_result","content":"370\t  - Forward, back, strafe left and strafe right move one cell.\n371\t  - Turning left or right takes a quarter turn.\n372\t  - **One move is buffered** while a tween runs (the last one pressed), and a held direction repeats as soon as the tween ends.\n373\t- **Durations.** A step takes ★0.28 s and a turn ★0.20 s, eased smoothstep (the kit's `place()`). Settings → *Step animation* offers Smooth (those values), Quick (0.16 s / 0.12 s) or Instant (0 s / 0 s, as the source did).\n374\t- **When things happen.** The party's logical cell changes when the step **starts**, so targeting and monster adjacency use the new cell at once. Arrival effects happen when the step **ends**: plates, pits, teleporters, spinners, trap plates, item messages and auto-hints.\n375\t- **Blocked moves.** A wall, a closed door, gate or secret wall, a monster, or the boss blocks a move. The view lurches forward and back (the kit's `bump`), a thud plays, and a message shows at most once every 2 s:\n376\t  - \"You can't go that way.\"\n377\t  - \"The gate will not budge.\"\n378\t  - \"Something blocks the way!\" (a monster)\n379\t  - \"It is locked.\" (a locked door)\n380\t- **Head bob.** A 0.8-unit dip on each step. It is off under *reduce motion*.\n381\t- **Stairs.** Entering a stair cell fades out over 0.4 s, shows the floor card (the floor's name in the title font and one line of flavour, 1.6 s, skippable with any button), fades in at the paired arrival cell and autosaves.\n382\t  - Floor 1's up-stair is sealed: \"The grate above is bolted fast. There is no way back.\"\n383\t\n384\t### 4.2 Dungeon features and the wiring language\n385\t\n386\tEvery feature is data in `15-data-floors.js` (§7.4). Each wall feature is a `THING` drawn on the wall face (§6.2).\n387\t\n388\t| Feature | Map / table | Behaviour | Interaction (✕ / F / click) |\n389\t|---|---|---|---|\n390\t| Wooden door `+` | map | swings on hinges; monsters can't pass a closed one | ✕ facing it: open or close |\n391\t| Gate (portcullis) `=` | map + wiring | rises and falls (0.9 s); passable when open > 0.85 | none; the wiring opens it |\n392\t| Locked door `L` / `K` | map + `lock: keyId, kind: 'simple'\\|'warded'` | opens with its key; the key is used up (\"The Iron Key turns. The door opens.\") | ✕ with the key in the Hand or anywhere in a pack (it picks the right one); a thief can pick a *simple* lock |\n393\t| Magic seal `M` | map | a door of runes; opens to the Unlatch spell, scroll or wand | cast Unlatch while facing it |\n394\t| Secret wall `H` | map + wiring | looks exactly like a wall; slides into the floor (1.2 s, grinding) when wired | none; a loose stone, a catch or an event opens it |\n395\t| Illusory wall `I` | map | looks exactly like a wall; walks like floor; passing through plays a 0.15 s dissolve flash and \"The wall is an illusion!\" | walk into it |\n396\t| Pressure plate `P` | map + wiring | down while the party, any item or a medium or large monster is on it. Modes: `hold` (open while down), `latch` (stays done once triggered), `toggle` | stand on it, put an item down on it, throw an item onto it |\n397\t| Dart trap `T` | map | on entry fires darts at 2 members for ★1d4 each (\"Darts fly from the walls!\") | a thief sees it glint; a thief facing it with ✕ disarms it (DEX check: 50% + 10% × DEX bonus + 5% × level; a failure fires it) |\n398\t| Open pit `O` | map | entering it drops the party to the floor below at the pit's landing cell: 1d6 to each member, a camera drop and a fade. Feather Step floats over it. Items put into it fall to the landing. | none; wiring can bridge it |\n399\t| Trapdoor `o` | map | looks like floor (a thief or Find Seams shows thin seams); on entry it opens and falls as a pit, then stays an open pit | none |\n400\t| Teleporter `^` | map + `to: [x, y, dir], silent, on` | moves the party instantly. A *silent* one has no effect (the source's trick); a visible one swirls and whooshes. Wiring can switch it off. | walk in |\n401\t| Spinner `S` | map | sets a random facing on entry (from the `world` RNG stream) with a soft grinding sound (\"You feel a twisting underfoot.\"). Only the compass gives it away. | walk in |\n402\t| Stairs `<` `>` | map + `to: floor, at: [x, y, dir]` | change floors | walk in |\n403\t| Lever | wall feature | toggles up and down (clank); fires its wire on each change | ✕ facing it |\n404\t| Button | wall feature | momentary press (click); fires its wire | ✕ facing it |\n405\t| Loose stone / catch | wall feature | a subtle, visible odd stone or brass rivet (not invisible); glints under Find Seams or the goggles; fires its wire once | ✕ facing it |\n406\t| Alcove | wall feature + `holds: [items], accepts: filter` | a niche that shows its items | ✕: take the top item into the Hand, or place the held item |\n407\t| Keyhole | part of a door or a wall feature | takes keys of its shape | ✕ with the key |\n408\t| Inscription | wall feature + `text` | a carved plaque; the text goes to the log and to Journal → Notes | ✕ or Examine facing it |\n409\t| Candle sconce | wall feature | lit or unlit; a light; part of a sequence wire | ✕ facing it: light it |\n410\t| Great Lock | wall feature (floor 4) | turns over 2.0 s; boss mechanic (§4.7.4) | ✕ facing it |\n411\t| Brazier niche | wall feature `torch` | decor light, from the level's `t` tile (the kit's NICHE trick) | none |\n412\t| Safe room | cell list | no rest interruptions there | none |\n413\t\n414\t**The wiring language** (data, designer-facing). `wires: [{ when, mode, do, fail, once }]`.\n415\t\n416\t- **Triggers (`when`):**\n417\t  - `{ lever: id }`, `{ button: id }`, `{ catch: id }`\n418\t  - `{ plate: id }` and `{ plates: [ids], all: true }`\n419\t  - `{ buttons: [ids], within: s }` (all pressed within s seconds)\n420\t  - `{ enter: [x, y], from?: dir }`, `{ leave: [x, y] }`\n421\t  - `{ alcove: id, accepts: 'kind:key' | itemId }`\n422\t  - `{ killed: [monster ids] }` or `{ group: name }`\n423\t  - `{ flag: name }`\n424\t  - `{ sequence: [feature ids], order: true }` (wrong order: `fail`)\n425\t  - `{ timer: s }` after another wire\n426\t  - `{ loops: n, at: [x, y] }` (the teleporter hint)\n427\t- **Actions (`do` / `fail`):**\n428\t  - doors: `['open'|'close'|'toggle', doorId]`\n429\t  - pits: `['bridge', pitId]`, `['pitOpen', id]`\n430\t  - teleporters: `['teleporter', id, on]`\n431\t  - moving the party or items: `['teleport', [x, y, dir]]`, `['spin']`\n432\t  - text: `['msg', textId]`, `['hint', textId]`, `['story', nodeId]`, `['cutscene', id]`\n433\t  - things in the world: `['give', itemId, [x, y] | alcoveId]`, `['spawn', type, [x, y], n]`\n434\t  - progress: `['xp', n]`, `['flag', name, value]`, `['quest', id, state]`, `['special', id]`\n435\t  - feedback: `['sfx', name]`, `['music', cue]`, `['shake', n]`, `['light', id, on]`\n436\t  - other: `['return']` (an alcove gives its item back), `['reset', ids]`\n437\t- **Lint.** The content lint (§14) checks every id and every coordinate.\n438\t- **Hints escalate.** When `Hints` is on, each puzzle carries `hints: [[seconds or loops, textId], …]`, shown when the player lingers or loops. No puzzle can softlock:\n439\t  - a spare rock sits in every plate room;\n440\t  - the riddle returns its key;\n441\t  - the Bent Key is placed at the riddle;\n442\t  - the Wand of Unlatching is placed before the magic seal;\n443\t  - the pits' landing has a ladder back up.\n444\t\n445\t### 4.3 The party: races, classes, stats, formation\n446\t\n447\t- **Stats.** STR, DEX, CON, INT and WIS each run from 3 to 18. The modifier is −2 (3–5), −1 (6–8), 0 (9–12), +1 (13–15), +2 (16–17) or +3 (18).\n448\t  - STR: melee and thrown to-hit and damage.\n449\t  - DEX: missile to-hit, Defense and cooldown (−4% per point of modifier).\n450\t  - CON: HP per level, poison resistance.\n451\t  - INT: mage slots (+1 level-1 slot at 16+).\n452\t  - WIS: cleric slots (+1 level-1 slot at 15+, +1 level-2 slot at 17+).\n453\t\n454\t| Race | Mods | Classes | Trait |\n455\t|---|---|---|---|\n456\t| Human | — | all six | gains 5% more XP |\n457\t| Dwarf | +1 CON, −1 DEX | Fighter, Cleric, Thief | resists poison 50%; has a beard |\n458\t| Elf | +1 DEX, −1 CON | Fighter, Ranger, Mage, Thief | resists Hold 50% |\n459\t| Halfling | +1 DEX, −1 STR | Fighter, Cleric, Thief | +1 to-hit with slings, darts and thrown weapons |\n460\t\n461\t| Class | HP die | To-hit bonus | Armour | Weapons | Abilities | XP × |\n462\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n463\t| Fighter | d10 | +level | all, shields | all | +1 to-hit at every level | 1.0 |\n464\t| Paladin | d10 | +level | all, shields | all | Lay on Hands: heal 2×level once per rest; Mend ×1 from level 3 | 1.2 |\n465\t| Ranger | d8 | +level | up to chain, no shield | all | two weapons with no penalty; +1 to-hit with bows and crossbows; *Tracking* (the automap shows monsters within 4 cells) | 1.15 |\n466\t| Cleric | d8 | +¾ level | up to scale, shields | blunt only: mace, flail, warhammer, staff, sling | prayers; Turn the Dead | 0.95 |\n467\t| Mage | d4 | +½ level | robes only | dagger, staff, darts, sling | spells; reads scrolls | 1.1 |\n468\t| Thief | d6 | +¾ level | leather and studded, buckler | light weapons, short bow, crossbows, darts, sling | picks simple locks (needs Lockpicks); sees and disarms traps and trapdoors; backstab ×2 damage on sleeping, held or unaware monsters | 0.9 |\n469\t\n470\t- **Off-hand weapons** take −2 to-hit, except for Rangers. **Hit points:** level 1 gets the full die plus the CON modifier; each later level gets half the die plus 1 plus the CON modifier, at least 1.\n471\t- **Formation.** Six places in three rows of two: front 1–2, middle 3–4, back 5–6. With four members, rows 1 and 2 are filled. Members are swapped in Camp → Formation, or with ✕ on two portraits in the Party screen.\n472\t  - **Row 1** melees.\n473\t  - **Row 2** melees only with *reach* weapons (spear, glaive).\n474\t  - **Row 3** uses missiles, thrown weapons and spells only.\n475\t\n476\t### 4.4 Character creation (`create` scene)\n477\t\n478\t- **The roster.** A 2×2 roster of slots, each showing **Create** or **Premade**, plus **Quick Start**, which fills all four with the premade party (§7.9).\n479\t- **Editor steps.** Each step is one big panel with ✕ to go on and ○ to go back:\n480\t  1. **Race:** 4 tiles.\n481\t  2. **Class:** the allowed ones.\n482\t  3. **Look:** ◀ ▶ through 6 presets for each race and sex. Each preset is an `E.Humanoid` option set (hair, skin, palette, hat, beard), drawn as a large live portrait.\n483\t  4. **Stats:** 4d6 drop lowest, then race modifiers, re-rolled until the class minimums are met (Fighter STR 9, Paladin STR 12 and WIS 13, Ranger STR 13 and DEX 13, Cleric WIS 9, Mage INT 9, Thief DEX 9). □ re-rolls without limit; ✕ keeps.\n484\t  5. **Name:** a list of 12 suggestions for the race; △ picks a random one; □ opens the on-screen keyboard, a 10×4 letter grid (✕ types, □ deletes, R1 for case, Options when done). Physical keys type directly.\n485\t- **Starting gear by class.**\n486\t  - Fighter: long sword, wooden shield, leather armour.\n487\t  - Paladin: long sword, wooden shield, chain shirt.\n488\t  - Ranger: two short swords, leather armour.\n489\t  - Cleric: mace, Key of the Threshold (holy symbol), leather armour.\n490\t  - Mage: dagger and spellbook (Spark Dart, Find Seams, Slumber), plus a Traveller's Robe.\n491\t  - Thief: short sword, darts ×6, leather armour, Lockpicks.\n492\t  - Everyone gets 1 Ration and starts at food 100%.\n493\t- **Begin the Descent** starts the intro cutscene.\n494\t\n495\t### 4.5 Items, inventory, equipment and the Hand\n496\t\n497\t- **Slots for each member.** `head, neck, body, arms` (bracers), `main, off` (hands), `ring1, ring2, feet, quiver`, plus a **backpack of 12**.\n498\t  - Two-handed items fill main and lock off.\n499\t  - Ammo (bolts, arrows, sling stones) and darts stack up to 30; nothing else stacks.\n500\t  - There is no weight.\n501\t- **The Hand.** The party holds one item at a time, as the source held one on the mouse cursor. With the mouse it follows the cursor; otherwise it sits above the cards, bottom centre, with its name.\n502\t  - **Use ✕** with an item in the Hand:\n503\t    - facing an alcove, it places the item;\n504\t    - facing a keyhole with a fitting key, it inserts it;\n505\t    - with no target, it stows the item in the first free pack slot (the selected member first).\n506\t  - **Hold ✕ for 0.4 s** (**H** on the keyboard) puts it down on the front cell's floor, or on your own cell if the front is blocked. Putting it down on a plate presses the plate; putting it into a pit drops it to the landing.\n507\t  - **Throw ○** (**G**) sends it forward up to ★4 cells (+1 at STR 16+).\n508\t    - It passes over pits and plates.\n509\t    - It stops before walls and closed doors and lands in the last open cell, pressing a plate if it lands on one.\n510\t    - It stops at the first monster and hits it: thrown weapons do their dice plus STR; other items do 1d2. It then drops in that cell.\n511\t- **Picking up.** Picking up (✕ with nothing in the Hand) takes the top item in the front cell if it is open and free of monsters, otherwise in your own cell. Floor items show at the 4 quadrant spots; a fifth item and more show as a little pile.\n512\t- **Use** (□ in the Party screen):\n513\t  - eat a ration, drink a potion;\n514\t  - read a scroll (a mage casts it at once, or uses Scribe at camp to learn it);\n515\t  - read a note;\n516\t  - zap a wand (as a hand action when it is held).\n517\t- **Monster drops.** Each monster has a drop table rolled on the `loot` stream (§7.6). Named monsters have fixed drops. Thieving Latchkins drop what they stole.\n518\t- **Rust.** A Rustling's hit can rust a metal main-hand weapon (−2 damage, up to −4, shown as \"Rusted\"). It is permanent; spare weapons are placed.\n519\t\n520\t### 4.6 Combat (real time)\n521\t\n522\t- **Every hand is an action.** Using a hand does what its item does:\n523\t\n524\t  | Item in the hand | What using it does |\n525\t  |---|---|\n526\t  | weapon | attacks the front cell, if the member's row may reach it |\n527\t  | missile weapon | fires ammo from the quiver along the facing line, up to 6 cells |\n528\t  | thrown weapon (dagger, darts, hand axe, spear) | throws it |\n529\t  | spellbook or holy symbol | opens the cast menu |\n530\t  | wand | zaps |\n531\t  | shield | **Brace**: +3 Defense for 2 s |\n532\t  | nothing | punches (1d2) |\n533\t\n534\t  Using a hand starts its cooldown: the weapon's speed (§7.7) × ★attack cooldown × (1 − 0.04 × DEX modifier). Casting a spell puts **both** of the caster's hands on 2.5 s.\n535\t- **Party Strike** (□, Space, right-click in the view). Every member whose main hand is ready, and whose main-hand item can act from their row, acts at once:\n536\t  - the front row melees;\n537\t  - row 2 attacks with reach weapons;\n538\t  - anyone holding a missile or thrown weapon fires it.\n539\t\n540\t  Spellbooks and holy symbols are skipped, because a spell needs a choice. With nothing in front, the front row \"swings at the air.\"\n541\t- **To-hit.** d20 + to-hit bonus (class) + STR modifier (melee and thrown) or DEX modifier (missile) + weapon bonus + ★party to-hit + buffs ≥ the target's Defense.\n542\t  - A natural 20 always hits for double dice; a natural 1 always misses.\n543\t  - Damage is the weapon's dice + STR modifier (1.5× for two-handed weapons), × ★party damage, at least 1.\n544\t  - A miss shows MISS on the hand slot.\n545\t- **Party Defense.** 10 + armour + shield + DEX modifier + helm, bracers and rings + buffs.\n546\t- **Monster attacks.** A monster's attack roll is d20 + its attack bonus ≥ the member's Defense; its damage is its dice × ★monster damage.\n547\t- **Who gets hit.** It depends on where the attacker stands relative to the party's facing:\n548\t  - from the front: a random conscious member of the front-most occupied row;\n549\t  - from the left: the left column (places 1, 3, 5), front-most first;\n550\t  - from the right: the right column (2, 4, 6);\n551\t  - from behind: the back-most occupied row.\n552\t\n553\t  The HUD shows red chevrons on the edge the hit came from (§5.2), and the hit sound is panned.\n554\t- **Projectiles** (bolts, arrows, darts, thrown items, Spark Darts, Locking Bolts) move cell by cell at their own speed (8 cells/s for missiles, 6 for Spark Dart, 5 for monster bolts) and hit the first creature in their path.\n555\t  - A monster bolt reaching the party's cell hits the party, through the side it came from.\n556\t  - A party projectile hits the monster in the target cell's slot nearest the shooter's column.\n557\t  - Spent ammo drops in the cell where it stopped, with a 50% chance to be recovered.\n558\t- **Statuses** (seconds left, in `member.status` and `mon.status`):\n559\t\n560\t  | Status | Effect |\n561\t  |---|---|\n562\t  | Poisoned | 1 HP every 6 s for 60 s, or until Purge or an Antidote |\n563\t  | Held | can't act for 4 to 10 s; Free Hinge or the Lidless Ward prevents it |\n564\t  | Asleep (monsters) | wakes when damaged; takes ×2 damage |\n565\t  | Slowed | intervals ×1.5 |\n566\t  | Blessed / Steady | +1 to-hit and +1 to saves |\n567\t  | Warded | an absorb pool |\n568\t  | Floating | over pits; no plates |\n569\t  | Seeing | secrets glint |\n570\t  | Turned | undead flee |\n571\t\n572\t  A **save** is a d20 roll ≥ 15 − level/2 − WIS or CON modifier.\n573\t\n574\t### 4.7 Monsters and their AI\n575\t\n576\t#### 4.7.1 Size and cells\n577\t- Small monsters take 1 of 4 units in a cell, medium 2 and large 4; a cell holds 4 units.\n578\t- Monsters never enter the party's cell or walls, closed doors or pits. Fliers ignore pits and plates.\n579\t- Small monsters don't weigh plates; medium and large ones do.\n580\t\n581\t#### 4.7.2 Minds (per type in §7.6)\n582\t- **States.**\n583\t  - `asleep`: placed sleeping; noise wakes it.\n584\t  - `idle` and `patrol`: a list of cells to walk.\n585\t  - `hunt`: paths to the party with `E.Grid` A*. Cells that are full are blocked, so others take the side and back cells, and **flanking happens on its own**.\n586\t  - `engage`: next to the party it faces, winds up and strikes.\n587\t  - `ranged`: in line with a clear view and in range, it shoots.\n588\t  - `keepAway`: casters hold 3 cells off and step back if you close in.\n589\t  - `flee`: cowards below 25% HP run for 6 s; thieves flee after a steal.\n590\t  - `turned`, `held`, `asleep`.\n591\t- **Awareness.**\n592\t  - A monster becomes aware when the party's cell is in its sight radius (★sight) and its field of view (E.Fog-style shadowcasting from the monster, or the symmetric check `fog.visible` from the party, as the kit does).\n593\t  - **Noise** (combat, doors, levers, falls) wakes monsters within 4 cells; hounds hear to 6.\n594\t  - A monster forgets the party after 12 s out of sight and goes back to `patrol`.\n595\t- **Movement.** One cell per `move` seconds (per type), drawn as a glide.\n596\t\n597\t#### 4.7.3 Attacks\n598\t- **Every melee attack telegraphs.** The wind-up (★0.5 s; heavy monsters 0.8 s) is a visible animation with a sound. The strike lands only if the party is still next to the monster when the wind-up ends; otherwise \"The rat's bite snaps on air.\" **This makes the sidestep a skill.**\n599\t- **Re-engaging.** After the party changes cell, a monster next to it waits ★0.6 s before starting a new wind-up (the \"dance\" is fair but not free).\n600\t- **Intervals.** Each type has its own interval × ★monster interval.\n601\t\n602\t#### 4.7.4 The Bolt-Holder (boss, floor 4)\n603\t\n604\tIt hovers over the arena's centre cell (6,3) with **HP ★320** and Defense 15. It is immune to Hold, Sleep and Poison, and it **absorbs lightning**: Forked Lightning heals it for half, and it gloats \"The Bolt-Holder drinks the lightning!\"\n605\t\n606\t| Phase | When | Behaviour |\n607\t|---|---|---|\n608\t| **1: Caged** | the start | It takes ×0.2 damage through the cage and attacks every 3.5 s. Each of the 3 **Great Locks** (west, east and north walls) turned with ✕ (2.0 s, during which the party can't act) slides out 2 of its 7 bolts (\"The Bolt-Holder shrieks!\"). When all 3 are turned, the cage opens. |\n609\t| **2: Unbolted** | all locks turned | It takes full damage, attacks every 2.8 s and **drifts** one cell toward the party's flank every 4 s (it never enters pillar cells). At 50% HP it **relocks** one lock at random (\"It wrenches a bolt home!\"): damage taken is ×0.5 until that lock is turned again. |\n610\t| **3: Wrath** | HP below 25% | It attacks every 2.2 s and summons 2 Gazers every 12 s (4 at most). |\n611\t\n612\tIts attacks, picked by its position and line of sight:\n613\t- **Chain Lightning** (any phase): telegraphed for 0.8 s by a cyan glow and a flicker along the row or column toward the party. The bolt runs along the line until a pillar or wall: 3d6 to 2 members. Leave the line, or put a pillar between you, to dodge it.\n614\t- **Locking Gaze:** the iris narrows for 1.0 s, then 1 or 2 members without the Lidless Ward or Free Hinge are Held for 4 s (\"Your joints lock!\"). It needs line of sight, and pillars block it.\n615\t- **Bolt Rain:** marks the party's cell and 2 open neighbouring cells with glowing rings (`world.arc`) for 1.5 s, then iron bolts slam down: 2d6 to 2 members if the party is still in a marked cell.\n616\t- **Cage Slam** (adjacent only): 2d8 to the side facing it.\n617\t\n618\tOn death the Eye cracks, the bolts fly off, and the north lock wall becomes the **Stair to the Light** (✕ there starts the ending).\n619\t\n620\t### 4.8 Magic\n621\t\n622\t- **Mages** cast from their **spellbook** (an off-hand item). The book holds the spells they know; new ones come from scrolls through Camp → Scribe, which always succeeds and uses up the scroll.\n623\t- **Clerics** need their **holy symbol** in a hand. They may prepare any prayer of a level they can cast.\n624\t- **Memorizing.** Both choose what fills their slots in Camp → Memorize or Pray. The choice persists (\"same as before\" is the default), and slots refill after a full rest. Casting spends a slot.\n625\t- **Scrolls.** A mage can cast a scroll directly, and the scroll is used up. The Scroll of Raising works for clerics and paladins.\n626\t- **Wands** have charges and anyone can use one.\n627\t- **Slots by caster level, 1 to 8:**\n628\t\n629\t  | Caster | Spell level | Lv1 | Lv2 | Lv3 | Lv4 | Lv5 | Lv6 | Lv7 | Lv8 |\n630\t  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n631\t  | Mage | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n632\t  | Mage | 2 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 |\n633\t  | Mage | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 |\n634\t  | Cleric | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 4 | 4 |\n635\t  | Cleric | 2 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n636\t  | Cleric | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 |\n637\t\n638\t  Bonus slots come from INT and WIS (§4.3) and from the Robe of the Lintel (+1 level-1 slot).\n639\t- **Targets.**\n640\t  - `front`: the cell in front.\n641\t  - `line`: down the facing line.\n642\t  - `member`: picked on the cards.\n643\t  - `party`.\n644\t  - `door`: the door in front.\n645\t- **Spell effects** are × ★spell power. The spell list is in §7.8.\n646\t\n647\t### 4.9 Camp, rest and food\n648\t\n649\t- **Camp** (L3, C, or the HUD's camp icon) is refused while any aware monster is within 6 cells: \"You may not rest with enemies near.\"\n650\t- **Camp menu tiles:** Rest, Memorize, Pray, Scribe, Formation, Eat, Save Game, Load Game, Settings and Leave Camp.\n651\t- **Resting.** An hour passes every 0.4 s of real time. Each hour every conscious member heals ★1 + CON modifier (at least 1) × ★rest heal.\n652\t  - After 8 hours, slots refill to the prepared spells, Lay on Hands returns, and Down members wake at 1 HP.\n653\t  - During rest, clerics first cast their prepared Mends on the wounded (as the source let the party do), then pray to restore them.\n654\t  - Rest stops early when everyone is full and every slot is ready, or when ○ is pressed.\n655\t- **Interruptions.** Each hour has a ★4% chance (0 in safe rooms and once the floor's wanderer budget is spent: floor 1: 3, floor 2: 3, floor 3: 4, floor 4: 0) of spawning a wanderer group from the floor's spawn list, 2 cells away and aware. \"You are awakened by something moving!\" The camp closes.\n656\t- **Food** runs 0 to 100 for each member.\n657\t  - It drains −1 every ★60 s of play and −15 per full rest. ★Hunger rate scales it.\n658\t  - At 25: \"Cobb is hungry.\" At 0: \"Cobb is starving!\" A starving member loses 1 HP every 30 s and heals nothing from rest.\n659\t  - A Ration gives +50 and an Iron Ration +100. Loaves of the Hearth fills everyone.\n660\t  - **Eat** at camp feeds every member below 60% from the party's smallest food items.\n661\t\n662\t### 4.10 Experience, levels and hidden deeds\n663\t\n664\t- **XP for kills** is split evenly among conscious members, then × class and race multipliers and × ★XP gain.\n665\t- **Base thresholds:** level 2 at 100, 3 at 250, 4 at 500, 5 at 900, 6 at 1500, 7 at 2400 and 8 at 3600 (the demo's cap).\n666\t- **Discoveries.** A found secret gives +25 to each member and plays the `secret` sting. There are 3, marked `secret: true` in the floor data: the floor 1 closet (SW1/H1), the floor 1 illusory wall (I1) and the floor 2 catch (SW2). A hidden deed (special quest) gives +100 to each member, plays a jingle and shows \"A hidden deed is done!\". There are 3 deeds in all; Journal → Deeds counts them.\n667\t- **Level-up.** On the next rest or at once (the default is at once, with the \"level up\" sting): new HP, to-hit and slots, a portrait smile and a toast \"Brannoc has reached level 3!\".\n668\t- **Target pace** (the bot checks it, §14): about level 3 at the end of floor 1, 4 at the end of floor 2, 5 on floor 3, and 5 or 6 at the boss.\n669\t\n670\t### 4.11 Down, death, raising and game over\n671\t\n672\t- **Down.** At 0 HP a member is **Down**: unconscious, greyed portrait, can't act. A Down member hit again becomes **Dead** (bones on the card).\n673\t- **Waking.** Healing wakes a Down member at the healed amount; a full rest wakes them at 1 HP.\n674\t- **Raising the dead.** Raise from Rest (cleric spell level 3), the Scroll of Raising (2 placed) or the **Shrine of the Threshold** (floor 2 Vault, one use) brings a dead member back at 1 HP.\n675\t- **Game over.** If no member is conscious, the screen reads \"THE PARTY IS LOST\" over the frozen view, with Load last save, Load game and Title.\n676\t\n677\t### 4.12 NPCs, dialogue and choices\n678\t\n679\t- **Recruits.** Three NPCs can join, up to 6 in the party: **Tamsin Pell** (floor 1), **Durgan Copperhand** (floor 2) and **Sister Veil** (floor 3). Their scripts are in §7.9.\n680\t- **Dismissing.** Dismiss is in the Party screen, behind a confirm. A dismissed NPC goes home and is gone for good.\n681\t- **How it runs.** Dialogue is `E.Story` nodes through `E.Dialog`:\n682\t  - the speaker's live rig portrait, its mouth moving as it speaks;\n683\t  - a **babble voice** (§6.5);\n684\t  - choices picked with the d-pad and ✕, or a click;\n685\t  - story flags kept in the save (`story.state()`).\n686\t- **Veil's choice** changes floor 3 (§7.4.3).\n687\t- **Barks.** Short, non-modal lines (`modal: false, auto: 2`) for Snick, the Bolt-Holder and party barks.\n688\t\n689\t### 4.13 Messages, Examine, Journal, notes and hints\n690\t\n691\t- **The log.** The three newest lines at the bottom left, fading after 8 s; the full history is in Journal → Log.\n692\t- **Examine** (R3, X or middle click) describes what is in front in the source's voice, for example:\n693\t  - \"A wall of slick stone. One stone sits proud of the rest.\"\n694\t  - \"The Drain Rat looks badly hurt.\"\n695\t  - \"A Rusty Dagger and a Rock lie here.\"\n696\t- **The Journal** (from the Party and Map screens or the pause menu) has four tabs:\n697\t  - **Quests**: the main thread, the NPCs and Veil.\n698\t  - **Notes**: every inscription and note read, readable again.\n699\t  - **Deeds**: found out of 3.\n700\t  - **Log**.\n701\t- **Hints** (Settings → Hints, on by default) are first-time tips that use the player's own glyphs, for example \"Rats! Press □ to strike with everyone who's ready.\", plus each puzzle's escalation.\n702\t\n703\t### 4.14 Automap\n704\t\n705\t- **What it records.** `E.Fog` per floor, with a sight radius of 4 in the dungeon. A cell counts as seen when it is visible; walls are drawn next to seen floor.\n706\t- **The screen** (§5.3) shows:\n707\t  - walls, and doors in their states;\n708\t  - stairs, plates (once seen pressed or stood on), alcoves, inscriptions (a quill), teleporters (once used), the spinner (once noticed);\n709\t  - NPCs, the party arrow, and monster dots for a Ranger's Tracking.\n710\t- **A minimap** in the top-right is *optional*. The default HUD has only the compass, to keep the 10-foot view clean.\n711\t\n712\t### 4.15 Saves\n713\t\n714\t- **Where.** The engine's `game.saves`: 3 slots plus the autosave.\n715\t  - Save and Load sit in Camp and in the pause menu (when out of combat, `saves.canSave`).\n716\t  - The autosave runs on every floor arrival and after the boss.\n717\t- **The title.** Continue loads `latest()`.\n718\t- **Hub sessions.** The client gives the page in-memory storage, so add no code. Hide Save and Load in a session.\n719\t\n720\t### 4.16 Settings (the engine's, plus a *Game* group)\n721\t\n722\t- **The engine's own:** volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, screen filter, frame rate, the 3D Display settings (cards or puppets, resolution, shadows, filter) and Controls (rebinding, dead zone, vibration, **Button symbols: PlayStation / Xbox**).\n723\t- **Ours** (`game.settings.define`, group 'Game'):\n724\t\n725\t  | Setting | Options | Default |\n726\t  |---|---|---|\n727\t  | Difficulty | Easy / Normal / Hard | Normal |\n728\t  | Time in menus | Pause / Slow / Real time | Slow |\n729\t  | Step animation | Smooth / Quick / Instant | Smooth |\n730\t  | Head bob | on / off | on |\n731\t  | Hints | on / off | on |\n732\t  | Level up | At once / On rest | At once |\n733\t  | Voices volume | slider | 0.8 |\n734\t\n735\t---\n736\t\n737\t## 5. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller-first\n738\t\n739\t### 5.1 Principles\n740\t\n741\t- **Resolution.** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`. The HUD is laid out for **320×240 (4:3) up to about 427×240 (16:9)** in game pixels and anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n742\t  - At 1080p one game pixel is about 4.5 screen pixels, so the 5×7 font reads at about 31 px from the sofa.\n743\t  - Body text is the normal 5×7 font; headings use `scale: 2`; nothing interactive uses the tiny 3×5 font.\n744\t- **Few, big things.** At most about 12 focusable things per screen region. Every screen shows ○ Back at the bottom right. Cut density before you shrink anything.\n745\t- **One look: \"lockwork brass.\"**\n746\t  - Panels: `bg ['#1c1813', '#0e0c0a']`, border `#b8924a`, focus `#ffd36a` (double border plus a slow pulse), text `#f0e6d0`.\n747\t  - Stat colours: HP `#e0463c`, spell slots `#4a8aff`, food `#c8a040`.\n748\t  - Rivets: 2×2 dots `#e8c878` in the panel corners.\n749\t  - Title text: `E.font.title` with `colors ['#fff6d8', '#ffd36a', '#c8923a', '#6a4a1a']` and `depthColor '#2a1a08'`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"4b7c7611-6cc5-487e-8ca7-1032f4b9fd43","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:53:38.082Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","content":"  - Forward, back, strafe left and strafe right move one cell.\n  - Turning left or right takes a quarter turn.\n  - **One move is buffered** while a tween runs (the last one pressed), and a held direction repeats as soon as the tween ends.\n- **Durations.** A step takes ★0.28 s and a turn ★0.20 s, eased smoothstep (the kit's `place()`). Settings → *Step animation* offers Smooth (those values), Quick (0.16 s / 0.12 s) or Instant (0 s / 0 s, as the source did).\n- **When things happen.** The party's logical cell changes when the step **starts**, so targeting and monster adjacency use the new cell at once. Arrival effects happen when the step **ends**: plates, pits, teleporters, spinners, trap plates, item messages and auto-hints.\n- **Blocked moves.** A wall, a closed door, gate or secret wall, a monster, or the boss blocks a move. The view lurches forward and back (the kit's `bump`), a thud plays, and a message shows at most once every 2 s:\n  - \"You can't go that way.\"\n  - \"The gate will not budge.\"\n  - \"Something blocks the way!\" (a monster)\n  - \"It is locked.\" (a locked door)\n- **Head bob.** A 0.8-unit dip on each step. It is off under *reduce motion*.\n- **Stairs.** Entering a stair cell fades out over 0.4 s, shows the floor card (the floor's name in the title font and one line of flavour, 1.6 s, skippable with any button), fades in at the paired arrival cell and autosaves.\n  - Floor 1's up-stair is sealed: \"The grate above is bolted fast. There is no way back.\"\n\n### 4.2 Dungeon features and the wiring language\n\nEvery feature is data in `15-data-floors.js` (§7.4). Each wall feature is a `THING` drawn on the wall face (§6.2).\n\n| Feature | Map / table | Behaviour | Interaction (✕ / F / click) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Wooden door `+` | map | swings on hinges; monsters can't pass a closed one | ✕ facing it: open or close |\n| Gate (portcullis) `=` | map + wiring | rises and falls (0.9 s); passable when open > 0.85 | none; the wiring opens it |\n| Locked door `L` / `K` | map + `lock: keyId, kind: 'simple'\\|'warded'` | opens with its key; the key is used up (\"The Iron Key turns. The door opens.\") | ✕ with the key in the Hand or anywhere in a pack (it picks the right one); a thief can pick a *simple* lock |\n| Magic seal `M` | map | a door of runes; opens to the Unlatch spell, scroll or wand | cast Unlatch while facing it |\n| Secret wall `H` | map + wiring | looks exactly like a wall; slides into the floor (1.2 s, grinding) when wired | none; a loose stone, a catch or an event opens it |\n| Illusory wall `I` | map | looks exactly like a wall; walks like floor; passing through plays a 0.15 s dissolve flash and \"The wall is an illusion!\" | walk into it |\n| Pressure plate `P` | map + wiring | down while the party, any item or a medium or large monster is on it. Modes: `hold` (open while down), `latch` (stays done once triggered), `toggle` | stand on it, put an item down on it, throw an item onto it |\n| Dart trap `T` | map | on entry fires darts at 2 members for ★1d4 each (\"Darts fly from the walls!\") | a thief sees it glint; a thief facing it with ✕ disarms it (DEX check: 50% + 10% × DEX bonus + 5% × level; a failure fires it) |\n| Open pit `O` | map | entering it drops the party to the floor below at the pit's landing cell: 1d6 to each member, a camera drop and a fade. Feather Step floats over it. Items put into it fall to the landing. | none; wiring can bridge it |\n| Trapdoor `o` | map | looks like floor (a thief or Find Seams shows thin seams); on entry it opens and falls as a pit, then stays an open pit | none |\n| Teleporter `^` | map + `to: [x, y, dir], silent, on` | moves the party instantly. A *silent* one has no effect (the source's trick); a visible one swirls and whooshes. Wiring can switch it off. | walk in |\n| Spinner `S` | map | sets a random facing on entry (from the `world` RNG stream) with a soft grinding sound (\"You feel a twisting underfoot.\"). Only the compass gives it away. | walk in |\n| Stairs `<` `>` | map + `to: floor, at: [x, y, dir]` | change floors | walk in |\n| Lever | wall feature | toggles up and down (clank); fires its wire on each change | ✕ facing it |\n| Button | wall feature | momentary press (click); fires its wire | ✕ facing it |\n| Loose stone / catch | wall feature | a subtle, visible odd stone or brass rivet (not invisible); glints under Find Seams or the goggles; fires its wire once | ✕ facing it |\n| Alcove | wall feature + `holds: [items], accepts: filter` | a niche that shows its items | ✕: take the top item into the Hand, or place the held item |\n| Keyhole | part of a door or a wall feature | takes keys of its shape | ✕ with the key |\n| Inscription | wall feature + `text` | a carved plaque; the text goes to the log and to Journal → Notes | ✕ or Examine facing it |\n| Candle sconce | wall feature | lit or unlit; a light; part of a sequence wire | ✕ facing it: light it |\n| Great Lock | wall feature (floor 4) | turns over 2.0 s; boss mechanic (§4.7.4) | ✕ facing it |\n| Brazier niche | wall feature `torch` | decor light, from the level's `t` tile (the kit's NICHE trick) | none |\n| Safe room | cell list | no rest interruptions there | none |\n\n**The wiring language** (data, designer-facing). `wires: [{ when, mode, do, fail, once }]`.\n\n- **Triggers (`when`):**\n  - `{ lever: id }`, `{ button: id }`, `{ catch: id }`\n  - `{ plate: id }` and `{ plates: [ids], all: true }`\n  - `{ buttons: [ids], within: s }` (all pressed within s seconds)\n  - `{ enter: [x, y], from?: dir }`, `{ leave: [x, y] }`\n  - `{ alcove: id, accepts: 'kind:key' | itemId }`\n  - `{ killed: [monster ids] }` or `{ group: name }`\n  - `{ flag: name }`\n  - `{ sequence: [feature ids], order: true }` (wrong order: `fail`)\n  - `{ timer: s }` after another wire\n  - `{ loops: n, at: [x, y] }` (the teleporter hint)\n- **Actions (`do` / `fail`):**\n  - doors: `['open'|'close'|'toggle', doorId]`\n  - pits: `['bridge', pitId]`, `['pitOpen', id]`\n  - teleporters: `['teleporter', id, on]`\n  - moving the party or items: `['teleport', [x, y, dir]]`, `['spin']`\n  - text: `['msg', textId]`, `['hint', textId]`, `['story', nodeId]`, `['cutscene', id]`\n  - things in the world: `['give', itemId, [x, y] | alcoveId]`, `['spawn', type, [x, y], n]`\n  - progress: `['xp', n]`, `['flag', name, value]`, `['quest', id, state]`, `['special', id]`\n  - feedback: `['sfx', name]`, `['music', cue]`, `['shake', n]`, `['light', id, on]`\n  - other: `['return']` (an alcove gives its item back), `['reset', ids]`\n- **Lint.** The content lint (§14) checks every id and every coordinate.\n- **Hints escalate.** When `Hints` is on, each puzzle carries `hints: [[seconds or loops, textId], …]`, shown when the player lingers or loops. No puzzle can softlock:\n  - a spare rock sits in every plate room;\n  - the riddle returns its key;\n  - the Bent Key is placed at the riddle;\n  - the Wand of Unlatching is placed before the magic seal;\n  - the pits' landing has a ladder back up.\n\n### 4.3 The party: races, classes, stats, formation\n\n- **Stats.** STR, DEX, CON, INT and WIS each run from 3 to 18. The modifier is −2 (3–5), −1 (6–8), 0 (9–12), +1 (13–15), +2 (16–17) or +3 (18).\n  - STR: melee and thrown to-hit and damage.\n  - DEX: missile to-hit, Defense and cooldown (−4% per point of modifier).\n  - CON: HP per level, poison resistance.\n  - INT: mage slots (+1 level-1 slot at 16+).\n  - WIS: cleric slots (+1 level-1 slot at 15+, +1 level-2 slot at 17+).\n\n| Race | Mods | Classes | Trait |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Human | — | all six | gains 5% more XP |\n| Dwarf | +1 CON, −1 DEX | Fighter, Cleric, Thief | resists poison 50%; has a beard |\n| Elf | +1 DEX, −1 CON | Fighter, Ranger, Mage, Thief | resists Hold 50% |\n| Halfling | +1 DEX, −1 STR | Fighter, Cleric, Thief | +1 to-hit with slings, darts and thrown weapons |\n\n| Class | HP die | To-hit bonus | Armour | Weapons | Abilities | XP × |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Fighter | d10 | +level | all, shields | all | +1 to-hit at every level | 1.0 |\n| Paladin | d10 | +level | all, shields | all | Lay on Hands: heal 2×level once per rest; Mend ×1 from level 3 | 1.2 |\n| Ranger | d8 | +level | up to chain, no shield | all | two weapons with no penalty; +1 to-hit with bows and crossbows; *Tracking* (the automap shows monsters within 4 cells) | 1.15 |\n| Cleric | d8 | +¾ level | up to scale, shields | blunt only: mace, flail, warhammer, staff, sling | prayers; Turn the Dead | 0.95 |\n| Mage | d4 | +½ level | robes only | dagger, staff, darts, sling | spells; reads scrolls | 1.1 |\n| Thief | d6 | +¾ level | leather and studded, buckler | light weapons, short bow, crossbows, darts, sling | picks simple locks (needs Lockpicks); sees and disarms traps and trapdoors; backstab ×2 damage on sleeping, held or unaware monsters | 0.9 |\n\n- **Off-hand weapons** take −2 to-hit, except for Rangers. **Hit points:** level 1 gets the full die plus the CON modifier; each later level gets half the die plus 1 plus the CON modifier, at least 1.\n- **Formation.** Six places in three rows of two: front 1–2, middle 3–4, back 5–6. With four members, rows 1 and 2 are filled. Members are swapped in Camp → Formation, or with ✕ on two portraits in the Party screen.\n  - **Row 1** melees.\n  - **Row 2** melees only with *reach* weapons (spear, glaive).\n  - **Row 3** uses missiles, thrown weapons and spells only.\n\n### 4.4 Character creation (`create` scene)\n\n- **The roster.** A 2×2 roster of slots, each showing **Create** or **Premade**, plus **Quick Start**, which fills all four with the premade party (§7.9).\n- **Editor steps.** Each step is one big panel with ✕ to go on and ○ to go back:\n  1. **Race:** 4 tiles.\n  2. **Class:** the allowed ones.\n  3. **Look:** ◀ ▶ through 6 presets for each race and sex. Each preset is an `E.Humanoid` option set (hair, skin, palette, hat, beard), drawn as a large live portrait.\n  4. **Stats:** 4d6 drop lowest, then race modifiers, re-rolled until the class minimums are met (Fighter STR 9, Paladin STR 12 and WIS 13, Ranger STR 13 and DEX 13, Cleric WIS 9, Mage INT 9, Thief DEX 9). □ re-rolls without limit; ✕ keeps.\n  5. **Name:** a list of 12 suggestions for the race; △ picks a random one; □ opens the on-screen keyboard, a 10×4 letter grid (✕ types, □ deletes, R1 for case, Options when done). Physical keys type directly.\n- **Starting gear by class.**\n  - Fighter: long sword, wooden shield, leather armour.\n  - Paladin: long sword, wooden shield, chain shirt.\n  - Ranger: two short swords, leather armour.\n  - Cleric: mace, Key of the Threshold (holy symbol), leather armour.\n  - Mage: dagger and spellbook (Spark Dart, Find Seams, Slumber), plus a Traveller's Robe.\n  - Thief: short sword, darts ×6, leather armour, Lockpicks.\n  - Everyone gets 1 Ration and starts at food 100%.\n- **Begin the Descent** starts the intro cutscene.\n\n### 4.5 Items, inventory, equipment and the Hand\n\n- **Slots for each member.** `head, neck, body, arms` (bracers), `main, off` (hands), `ring1, ring2, feet, quiver`, plus a **backpack of 12**.\n  - Two-handed items fill main and lock off.\n  - Ammo (bolts, arrows, sling stones) and darts stack up to 30; nothing else stacks.\n  - There is no weight.\n- **The Hand.** The party holds one item at a time, as the source held one on the mouse cursor. With the mouse it follows the cursor; otherwise it sits above the cards, bottom centre, with its name.\n  - **Use ✕** with an item in the Hand:\n    - facing an alcove, it places the item;\n    - facing a keyhole with a fitting key, it inserts it;\n    - with no target, it stows the item in the first free pack slot (the selected member first).\n  - **Hold ✕ for 0.4 s** (**H** on the keyboard) puts it down on the front cell's floor, or on your own cell if the front is blocked. Putting it down on a plate presses the plate; putting it into a pit drops it to the landing.\n  - **Throw ○** (**G**) sends it forward up to ★4 cells (+1 at STR 16+).\n    - It passes over pits and plates.\n    - It stops before walls and closed doors and lands in the last open cell, pressing a plate if it lands on one.\n    - It stops at the first monster and hits it: thrown weapons do their dice plus STR; other items do 1d2. It then drops in that cell.\n- **Picking up.** Picking up (✕ with nothing in the Hand) takes the top item in the front cell if it is open and free of monsters, otherwise in your own cell. Floor items show at the 4 quadrant spots; a fifth item and more show as a little pile.\n- **Use** (□ in the Party screen):\n  - eat a ration, drink a potion;\n  - read a scroll (a mage casts it at once, or uses Scribe at camp to learn it);\n  - read a note;\n  - zap a wand (as a hand action when it is held).\n- **Monster drops.** Each monster has a drop table rolled on the `loot` stream (§7.6). Named monsters have fixed drops. Thieving Latchkins drop what they stole.\n- **Rust.** A Rustling's hit can rust a metal main-hand weapon (−2 damage, up to −4, shown as \"Rusted\"). It is permanent; spare weapons are placed.\n\n### 4.6 Combat (real time)\n\n- **Every hand is an action.** Using a hand does what its item does:\n\n  | Item in the hand | What using it does |\n  |---|---|\n  | weapon | attacks the front cell, if the member's row may reach it |\n  | missile weapon | fires ammo from the quiver along the facing line, up to 6 cells |\n  | thrown weapon (dagger, darts, hand axe, spear) | throws it |\n  | spellbook or holy symbol | opens the cast menu |\n  | wand | zaps |\n  | shield | **Brace**: +3 Defense for 2 s |\n  | nothing | punches (1d2) |\n\n  Using a hand starts its cooldown: the weapon's speed (§7.7) × ★attack cooldown × (1 − 0.04 × DEX modifier). Casting a spell puts **both** of the caster's hands on 2.5 s.\n- **Party Strike** (□, Space, right-click in the view). Every member whose main hand is ready, and whose main-hand item can act from their row, acts at once:\n  - the front row melees;\n  - row 2 attacks with reach weapons;\n  - anyone holding a missile or thrown weapon fires it.\n\n  Spellbooks and holy symbols are skipped, because a spell needs a choice. With nothing in front, the front row \"swings at the air.\"\n- **To-hit.** d20 + to-hit bonus (class) + STR modifier (melee and thrown) or DEX modifier (missile) + weapon bonus + ★party to-hit + buffs ≥ the target's Defense.\n  - A natural 20 always hits for double dice; a natural 1 always misses.\n  - Damage is the weapon's dice + STR modifier (1.5× for two-handed weapons), × ★party damage, at least 1.\n  - A miss shows MISS on the hand slot.\n- **Party Defense.** 10 + armour + shield + DEX modifier + helm, bracers and rings + buffs.\n- **Monster attacks.** A monster's attack roll is d20 + its attack bonus ≥ the member's Defense; its damage is its dice × ★monster damage.\n- **Who gets hit.** It depends on where the attacker stands relative to the party's facing:\n  - from the front: a random conscious member of the front-most occupied row;\n  - from the left: the left column (places 1, 3, 5), front-most first;\n  - from the right: the right column (2, 4, 6);\n  - from behind: the back-most occupied row.\n\n  The HUD shows red chevrons on the edge the hit came from (§5.2), and the hit sound is panned.\n- **Projectiles** (bolts, arrows, darts, thrown items, Spark Darts, Locking Bolts) move cell by cell at their own speed (8 cells/s for missiles, 6 for Spark Dart, 5 for monster bolts) and hit the first creature in their path.\n  - A monster bolt reaching the party's cell hits the party, through the side it came from.\n  - A party projectile hits the monster in the target cell's slot nearest the shooter's column.\n  - Spent ammo drops in the cell where it stopped, with a 50% chance to be recovered.\n- **Statuses** (seconds left, in `member.status` and `mon.status`):\n\n  | Status | Effect |\n  |---|---|\n  | Poisoned | 1 HP every 6 s for 60 s, or until Purge or an Antidote |\n  | Held | can't act for 4 to 10 s; Free Hinge or the Lidless Ward prevents it |\n  | Asleep (monsters) | wakes when damaged; takes ×2 damage |\n  | Slowed | intervals ×1.5 |\n  | Blessed / Steady | +1 to-hit and +1 to saves |\n  | Warded | an absorb pool |\n  | Floating | over pits; no plates |\n  | Seeing | secrets glint |\n  | Turned | undead flee |\n\n  A **save** is a d20 roll ≥ 15 − level/2 − WIS or CON modifier.\n\n### 4.7 Monsters and their AI\n\n#### 4.7.1 Size and cells\n- Small monsters take 1 of 4 units in a cell, medium 2 and large 4; a cell holds 4 units.\n- Monsters never enter the party's cell or walls, closed doors or pits. Fliers ignore pits and plates.\n- Small monsters don't weigh plates; medium and large ones do.\n\n#### 4.7.2 Minds (per type in §7.6)\n- **States.**\n  - `asleep`: placed sleeping; noise wakes it.\n  - `idle` and `patrol`: a list of cells to walk.\n  - `hunt`: paths to the party with `E.Grid` A*. Cells that are full are blocked, so others take the side and back cells, and **flanking happens on its own**.\n  - `engage`: next to the party it faces, winds up and strikes.\n  - `ranged`: in line with a clear view and in range, it shoots.\n  - `keepAway`: casters hold 3 cells off and step back if you close in.\n  - `flee`: cowards below 25% HP run for 6 s; thieves flee after a steal.\n  - `turned`, `held`, `asleep`.\n- **Awareness.**\n  - A monster becomes aware when the party's cell is in its sight radius (★sight) and its field of view (E.Fog-style shadowcasting from the monster, or the symmetric check `fog.visible` from the party, as the kit does).\n  - **Noise** (combat, doors, levers, falls) wakes monsters within 4 cells; hounds hear to 6.\n  - A monster forgets the party after 12 s out of sight and goes back to `patrol`.\n- **Movement.** One cell per `move` seconds (per type), drawn as a glide.\n\n#### 4.7.3 Attacks\n- **Every melee attack telegraphs.** The wind-up (★0.5 s; heavy monsters 0.8 s) is a visible animation with a sound. The strike lands only if the party is still next to the monster when the wind-up ends; otherwise \"The rat's bite snaps on air.\" **This makes the sidestep a skill.**\n- **Re-engaging.** After the party changes cell, a monster next to it waits ★0.6 s before starting a new wind-up (the \"dance\" is fair but not free).\n- **Intervals.** Each type has its own interval × ★monster interval.\n\n#### 4.7.4 The Bolt-Holder (boss, floor 4)\n\nIt hovers over the arena's centre cell (6,3) with **HP ★320** and Defense 15. It is immune to Hold, Sleep and Poison, and it **absorbs lightning**: Forked Lightning heals it for half, and it gloats \"The Bolt-Holder drinks the lightning!\"\n\n| Phase | When | Behaviour |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1: Caged** | the start | It takes ×0.2 damage through the cage and attacks every 3.5 s. Each of the 3 **Great Locks** (west, east and north walls) turned with ✕ (2.0 s, during which the party can't act) slides out 2 of its 7 bolts (\"The Bolt-Holder shrieks!\"). When all 3 are turned, the cage opens. |\n| **2: Unbolted** | all locks turned | It takes full damage, attacks every 2.8 s and **drifts** one cell toward the party's flank every 4 s (it never enters pillar cells). At 50% HP it **relocks** one lock at random (\"It wrenches a bolt home!\"): damage taken is ×0.5 until that lock is turned again. |\n| **3: Wrath** | HP below 25% | It attacks every 2.2 s and summons 2 Gazers every 12 s (4 at most). |\n\nIts attacks, picked by its position and line of sight:\n- **Chain Lightning** (any phase): telegraphed for 0.8 s by a cyan glow and a flicker along the row or column toward the party. The bolt runs along the line until a pillar or wall: 3d6 to 2 members. Leave the line, or put a pillar between you, to dodge it.\n- **Locking Gaze:** the iris narrows for 1.0 s, then 1 or 2 members without the Lidless Ward or Free Hinge are Held for 4 s (\"Your joints lock!\"). It needs line of sight, and pillars block it.\n- **Bolt Rain:** marks the party's cell and 2 open neighbouring cells with glowing rings (`world.arc`) for 1.5 s, then iron bolts slam down: 2d6 to 2 members if the party is still in a marked cell.\n- **Cage Slam** (adjacent only): 2d8 to the side facing it.\n\nOn death the Eye cracks, the bolts fly off, and the north lock wall becomes the **Stair to the Light** (✕ there starts the ending).\n\n### 4.8 Magic\n\n- **Mages** cast from their **spellbook** (an off-hand item). The book holds the spells they know; new ones come from scrolls through Camp → Scribe, which always succeeds and uses up the scroll.\n- **Clerics** need their **holy symbol** in a hand. They may prepare any prayer of a level they can cast.\n- **Memorizing.** Both choose what fills their slots in Camp → Memorize or Pray. The choice persists (\"same as before\" is the default), and slots refill after a full rest. Casting spends a slot.\n- **Scrolls.** A mage can cast a scroll directly, and the scroll is used up. The Scroll of Raising works for clerics and paladins.\n- **Wands** have charges and anyone can use one.\n- **Slots by caster level, 1 to 8:**\n\n  | Caster | Spell level | Lv1 | Lv2 | Lv3 | Lv4 | Lv5 | Lv6 | Lv7 | Lv8 |\n  |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n  | Mage | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 4 | 4 | 4 |\n  | Mage | 2 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 |\n  | Mage | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 |\n  | Cleric | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 3 | 4 | 4 |\n  | Cleric | 2 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 2 | 2 | 3 | 3 | 3 |\n  | Cleric | 3 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 2 |\n\n  Bonus slots come from INT and WIS (§4.3) and from the Robe of the Lintel (+1 level-1 slot).\n- **Targets.**\n  - `front`: the cell in front.\n  - `line`: down the facing line.\n  - `member`: picked on the cards.\n  - `party`.\n  - `door`: the door in front.\n- **Spell effects** are × ★spell power. The spell list is in §7.8.\n\n### 4.9 Camp, rest and food\n\n- **Camp** (L3, C, or the HUD's camp icon) is refused while any aware monster is within 6 cells: \"You may not rest with enemies near.\"\n- **Camp menu tiles:** Rest, Memorize, Pray, Scribe, Formation, Eat, Save Game, Load Game, Settings and Leave Camp.\n- **Resting.** An hour passes every 0.4 s of real time. Each hour every conscious member heals ★1 + CON modifier (at least 1) × ★rest heal.\n  - After 8 hours, slots refill to the prepared spells, Lay on Hands returns, and Down members wake at 1 HP.\n  - During rest, clerics first cast their prepared Mends on the wounded (as the source let the party do), then pray to restore them.\n  - Rest stops early when everyone is full and every slot is ready, or when ○ is pressed.\n- **Interruptions.** Each hour has a ★4% chance (0 in safe rooms and once the floor's wanderer budget is spent: floor 1: 3, floor 2: 3, floor 3: 4, floor 4: 0) of spawning a wanderer group from the floor's spawn list, 2 cells away and aware. \"You are awakened by something moving!\" The camp closes.\n- **Food** runs 0 to 100 for each member.\n  - It drains −1 every ★60 s of play and −15 per full rest. ★Hunger rate scales it.\n  - At 25: \"Cobb is hungry.\" At 0: \"Cobb is starving!\" A starving member loses 1 HP every 30 s and heals nothing from rest.\n  - A Ration gives +50 and an Iron Ration +100. Loaves of the Hearth fills everyone.\n  - **Eat** at camp feeds every member below 60% from the party's smallest food items.\n\n### 4.10 Experience, levels and hidden deeds\n\n- **XP for kills** is split evenly among conscious members, then × class and race multipliers and × ★XP gain.\n- **Base thresholds:** level 2 at 100, 3 at 250, 4 at 500, 5 at 900, 6 at 1500, 7 at 2400 and 8 at 3600 (the demo's cap).\n- **Discoveries.** A found secret gives +25 to each member and plays the `secret` sting. There are 3, marked `secret: true` in the floor data: the floor 1 closet (SW1/H1), the floor 1 illusory wall (I1) and the floor 2 catch (SW2). A hidden deed (special quest) gives +100 to each member, plays a jingle and shows \"A hidden deed is done!\". There are 3 deeds in all; Journal → Deeds counts them.\n- **Level-up.** On the next rest or at once (the default is at once, with the \"level up\" sting): new HP, to-hit and slots, a portrait smile and a toast \"Brannoc has reached level 3!\".\n- **Target pace** (the bot checks it, §14): about level 3 at the end of floor 1, 4 at the end of floor 2, 5 on floor 3, and 5 or 6 at the boss.\n\n### 4.11 Down, death, raising and game over\n\n- **Down.** At 0 HP a member is **Down**: unconscious, greyed portrait, can't act. A Down member hit again becomes **Dead** (bones on the card).\n- **Waking.** Healing wakes a Down member at the healed amount; a full rest wakes them at 1 HP.\n- **Raising the dead.** Raise from Rest (cleric spell level 3), the Scroll of Raising (2 placed) or the **Shrine of the Threshold** (floor 2 Vault, one use) brings a dead member back at 1 HP.\n- **Game over.** If no member is conscious, the screen reads \"THE PARTY IS LOST\" over the frozen view, with Load last save, Load game and Title.\n\n### 4.12 NPCs, dialogue and choices\n\n- **Recruits.** Three NPCs can join, up to 6 in the party: **Tamsin Pell** (floor 1), **Durgan Copperhand** (floor 2) and **Sister Veil** (floor 3). Their scripts are in §7.9.\n- **Dismissing.** Dismiss is in the Party screen, behind a confirm. A dismissed NPC goes home and is gone for good.\n- **How it runs.** Dialogue is `E.Story` nodes through `E.Dialog`:\n  - the speaker's live rig portrait, its mouth moving as it speaks;\n  - a **babble voice** (§6.5);\n  - choices picked with the d-pad and ✕, or a click;\n  - story flags kept in the save (`story.state()`).\n- **Veil's choice** changes floor 3 (§7.4.3).\n- **Barks.** Short, non-modal lines (`modal: false, auto: 2`) for Snick, the Bolt-Holder and party barks.\n\n### 4.13 Messages, Examine, Journal, notes and hints\n\n- **The log.** The three newest lines at the bottom left, fading after 8 s; the full history is in Journal → Log.\n- **Examine** (R3, X or middle click) describes what is in front in the source's voice, for example:\n  - \"A wall of slick stone. One stone sits proud of the rest.\"\n  - \"The Drain Rat looks badly hurt.\"\n  - \"A Rusty Dagger and a Rock lie here.\"\n- **The Journal** (from the Party and Map screens or the pause menu) has four tabs:\n  - **Quests**: the main thread, the NPCs and Veil.\n  - **Notes**: every inscription and note read, readable again.\n  - **Deeds**: found out of 3.\n  - **Log**.\n- **Hints** (Settings → Hints, on by default) are first-time tips that use the player's own glyphs, for example \"Rats! Press □ to strike with everyone who's ready.\", plus each puzzle's escalation.\n\n### 4.14 Automap\n\n- **What it records.** `E.Fog` per floor, with a sight radius of 4 in the dungeon. A cell counts as seen when it is visible; walls are drawn next to seen floor.\n- **The screen** (§5.3) shows:\n  - walls, and doors in their states;\n  - stairs, plates (once seen pressed or stood on), alcoves, inscriptions (a quill), teleporters (once used), the spinner (once noticed);\n  - NPCs, the party arrow, and monster dots for a Ranger's Tracking.\n- **A minimap** in the top-right is *optional*. The default HUD has only the compass, to keep the 10-foot view clean.\n\n### 4.15 Saves\n\n- **Where.** The engine's `game.saves`: 3 slots plus the autosave.\n  - Save and Load sit in Camp and in the pause menu (when out of combat, `saves.canSave`).\n  - The autosave runs on every floor arrival and after the boss.\n- **The title.** Continue loads `latest()`.\n- **Hub sessions.** The client gives the page in-memory storage, so add no code. Hide Save and Load in a session.\n\n### 4.16 Settings (the engine's, plus a *Game* group)\n\n- **The engine's own:** volume, music, effects, mute, reduce motion, shake and flashes, pixel scaling, screen filter, frame rate, the 3D Display settings (cards or puppets, resolution, shadows, filter) and Controls (rebinding, dead zone, vibration, **Button symbols: PlayStation / Xbox**).\n- **Ours** (`game.settings.define`, group 'Game'):\n\n  | Setting | Options | Default |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | Difficulty | Easy / Normal / Hard | Normal |\n  | Time in menus | Pause / Slow / Real time | Slow |\n  | Step animation | Smooth / Quick / Instant | Smooth |\n  | Head bob | on / off | on |\n  | Hints | on / off | on |\n  | Level up | At once / On rest | At once |\n  | Voices volume | slider | 0.8 |\n\n---\n\n## 5. UI and UX: 10-foot, controller-first\n\n### 5.1 Principles\n\n- **Resolution.** `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`. The HUD is laid out for **320×240 (4:3) up to about 427×240 (16:9)** in game pixels and anchored to `r.W` and `r.H`.\n  - At 1080p one game pixel is about 4.5 screen pixels, so the 5×7 font reads at about 31 px from the sofa.\n  - Body text is the normal 5×7 font; headings use `scale: 2`; nothing interactive uses the tiny 3×5 font.\n- **Few, big things.** At most about 12 focusable things per screen region. Every screen shows ○ Back at the bottom right. Cut density before you shrink anything.\n- **One look: \"lockwork brass.\"**\n  - Panels: `bg ['#1c1813', '#0e0c0a']`, border `#b8924a`, focus `#ffd36a` (double border plus a slow pulse), text `#f0e6d0`.\n  - Stat colours: HP `#e0463c`, spell slots `#4a8aff`, food `#c8a040`.\n  - Rivets: 2×2 dots `#e8c878` in the panel corners.\n  - Title text: `E.font.title` with `colors ['#fff6d8', '#ffd36a', '#c8923a', '#6a4a1a']` and `depthColor '#2a1a08'`.","numLines":380,"startLine":370,"totalLines":2145}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0149xGXeVTuxv77vKrBh65qh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh43dW75YGB7SdzjmB3c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xo4ufL9YKv3b2Busd4yzCh","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","offset":750,"limit":420},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1783,"cache_read_input_tokens":22513,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1783},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"15167938-b6a9-401b-9319-82153ec4495e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:53:38.847Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh43dJDDGDA4WDgEicM9","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Xo4ufL9YKv3b2Busd4yzCh":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","offset":750,"limit":420}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xo4ufL9YKv3b2Busd4yzCh","type":"tool_result","content":"750\t- **Keyboard and mouse are native, not stand-ins.** The same screens, with hover moving the focus, click acting, drag and drop in the Party screen, the mouse wheel scrolling lists and the Hand following the cursor (§5.4).\n751\t- **Prompts always follow `input.lastDevice`** (§5.5).\n752\t\n753\t### 5.2 The play HUD (over the full-screen 3D view)\n754\t\n755\t| Element | Where (game pixels) | Content |\n756\t|---|---|---|\n757\t| Compass | top centre, 64×16 | \"W  **N**  E\", with the facing letter at scale 2. It changes instantly on spinners: the only clue. |\n758\t| Floor toast | top left | the floor's name for 3 s on arrival |\n759\t| Party bar | bottom, `H−50` to `H−2` | one card per member in formation order. Row groups are 4 px apart, with tiny labels FRONT, MID and BACK above them. |\n760\t| Card (about 66×46) | — | live portrait 30×30 (`drawPortrait`, wince when hurt, grey when Down, bones when Dead); the name (cut to 7 letters); HP bar and `hp/max`; a 2-px food strip under the portrait; **two hand slots** 15×15 with the item icon, a cooldown wipe (dark, sweeping), a \"ready\" glint and MISS or damage pops; status icons in a row of 6×6 (poison drop, chain, Zz, shield, bowl) |\n761\t| Selected card | — | brass double frame; the L2 and R2 glyph badges under its hand slots |\n762\t| Log | above the bar, left | 3 lines, newest at the bottom, with a shadow |\n763\t| Prompts | above the bar, right | up to 4 `[glyph] Verb`, e.g. `✕ Pull lever · □ Strike · △ Party · Create Map` |\n764\t| Hand | above the bar, centre | held item icon and its name (unless the mouse cursor carries it) |\n765\t| Hit chevrons | screen edges | red chevrons on the left, right or bottom edge for 0.6 s on flank or rear hits; a red vignette on front-row hits |\n766\t| Mouse helpers | top right under the engine corner | small icons for Map, Camp, Party and Journal, mouse only; hidden for the pad |\n767\t| Boss bar | under the compass | THE BOLT-HOLDER, 200×6, with 3 lock pips (turned or locked) |\n768\t| Race HUD | top left | timer `02:13.4`, checkpoint label, crowns, and in a session the standings in seat colours |\n769\t\n770\tKeep clear of `game.cornerArea()`, the engine's top-right menu button.\n771\t\n772\t### 5.3 Screens\n773\t\n774\tEvery screen is drawn over the dimmed 3D view, takes focus navigation (§5.4) and shows its prompts.\n775\t\n776\t- **Party (△, I).**\n777\t  - **Top:** member tabs, one 32-px portrait each (L1 and R1 switch).\n778\t  - **Left:** the paperdoll, a large live rig portrait framed by 20×20 slots: Head, Neck, Body, Arms, Main, Off, Ring, Ring, Feet, Quiver.\n779\t  - **Centre:** the backpack, 4×3 slots of 22×22.\n780\t  - **Right:** details: a 32×32 icon, the name, a type line, stats (damage, speed, Defense, classes) and the description (§7.7 tone).\n781\t  - **Bottom:** `HP 11/11 · Defense 14 · Lv 2 Fighter · XP 140/250 · Food ███░`.\n782\t  - **Actions:** ✕ takes or places (it carries the item into the Hand, or swaps), □ Use, △ Drop at your feet, R2 Sheet. ○ returns a carried item, then closes the screen.\n783\t  - **The Sheet tab** shows the stats and their modifiers, class abilities, resistances and the spellbook or the prayers. Dismiss (NPCs) is here, behind a confirm.\n784\t- **Cast.** Opened by a caster's book or symbol hand.\n785\t  - A centred panel of about 260×150, with level tabs L1, L2, L3 (shoulders switch them).\n786\t  - Rows read \"Spark Dart ×2\", with a description and the target type.\n787\t  - ✕ casts. A `member` target then moves the highlight across the cards (◀ ▶, ✕). A `line`, `front`, `door` or `party` spell fires at once. ○ cancels.\n788\t- **Camp (L3, C).** The view darkens and a small campfire light glows in the cell. Tiles in 2 rows of 5 (§4.9).\n789\t  - **Rest** shows a rest panel: the hours counting, HP ticking up on the cards and ○ Stop.\n790\t  - **Memorize** and **Pray**: known spells on the left (grouped by level, with descriptions) and slots on the right. ✕ on a spell fills the next free slot of its level, ✕ on a slot empties it, △ \"Same as before\".\n791\t  - **Scribe**: scrolls in the party's packs that the mage doesn't know; ✕ learns one.\n792\t  - **Formation**: the 2×3 places; ✕ twice swaps two members.\n793\t  - **Eat**: a confirm, then a summary of who ate what.\n794\t  - **Save** and **Load** open the engine's slot screens (`saves.show`).\n795\t  - **Settings** opens `game.menu('settings')`.\n796\t- **Map (Create, M).**\n797\t  - A parchment-coloured panel with the floor's explored cells at 8 to 10 px per cell, and a legend row at the bottom.\n798\t  - L1 and R1 switch between visited floors; the d-pad or stick pans a map larger than the screen; ○ closes.\n799\t  - Dev *reveal* shows everything.\n800\t- **Journal (J, or the tab from Party or Map).** Quests, Notes, Deeds and Log. Notes open the **Reader**, a parchment page with the note's text at the normal size, paged with the d-pad.\n801\t- **Dialogue.** `E.Dialog` at the bottom: portrait on the left, name in brass and 3 lines a page. ✕ finishes the line, then turns the page; ○ fast-forwards; choices are a vertical list.\n802\t- **Title.**\n803\t  - **The view.** The Drain-gate stage from the intro (§7.3, stage B) at night. The camera pushes slowly toward the grate, the torches flicker, and every 12 s the great bolt slides home with a shake and a thud.\n804\t  - **The logo.** **EYE OF THE BOLT-HOLDER** in `E.font.title`, scale 4, brass colours (§5.1), with a slow glint across it, and \"A thousand locks. One eye.\" under it.\n805\t  - **The menu,** a centred `E.Menu`: Continue (only when a save exists), New Game, Load Game, Rat Run, Settings, About & Credits.\n806\t  - **The bottom line:** the prompts for the last device and the version (`E.versionLabel()`, plus the game's own version).\n807\t  - **Music:** `title`.\n808\t  - **The attract mode** starts after 10 s idle (§9). The touch notice is shown on touch-only devices (§5.7).\n809\t- **Creation:** §4.4. **The floor card:** §4.1. **About and Credits:** §11. **Touch notice:** §5.7.\n810\t- **Game over.** \"THE PARTY IS LOST\" in the title font over the frozen, darkened view; one line from the floor (\"The Drains keep what they take.\"); then Load last save · Load game · Title.\n811\t- **Ending.** The ending cutscene (§7.3), then the credits roll (the §11 Credits page, scrolling), then the stats panel (time, kills, secrets 3, deeds 3, deaths, levels) and Title. The `ending` song plays.\n812\t\n813\t### 5.4 Focus navigation and the mouse\n814\t\n815\t- **One focus system** (`61-ui-kit.js`) for every screen.\n816\t  - Each screen registers its widgets as rects with an id, an action and optional explicit neighbours.\n817\t  - The d-pad and the left stick (menus use the engine's `up/down/left/right`, which include the stick) move to the nearest widget in that direction, by geometry; explicit neighbours override.\n818\t  - Holding a direction repeats (`input.repeat`, 0.35 s, then 0.1 s).\n819\t- **The mouse.**\n820\t  - Moving the pointer over a widget focuses it; a left click acts as ✕; a right click on a slot acts as □; the wheel scrolls lists.\n821\t  - Party screen drag and drop: mouse-down carries the item, mouse-up places it.\n822\t- **Mouse in the 3D view.** Each frame, project the interactables in front with `world.project`: the front wall's feature, the items in the front and own cells, doors, the monsters in front and alcoves. Each gets a screen hotspot.\n823\t  - Hovering one outlines it (a soft brass rim drawn with the pen around the thing) and shows a tooltip, e.g. \"Lever — click to pull\".\n824\t  - A left click on a hotspot uses it. With an item in the Hand, a left click on the lower view puts it down and a left click on the upper view throws it, as in the source.\n825\t  - A right click anywhere in the view is **Party Strike**.\n826\t- **Party cards.** A click on a hand slot uses that hand (the source's core gesture); a click on a portrait selects; a right click on a portrait opens that member in the Party screen.\n827\t\n828\t### 5.5 Prompts and glyphs\n829\t\n830\t- **One painter, `glyph(g, x, y, action)`**, draws a badge for the action's binding on the last device:\n831\t\n832\t  | Device | Badge |\n833\t  |---|---|\n834\t  | Pad, PlayStation (default) | ✕ blue, ○ red, □ pink, △ green, drawn as shapes on a dark disc; L1, R1, L2, R2, L3, R3, Options and Create as rounded labels; D-pad and stick icons |\n835\t  | Pad, Xbox (Settings → Controls → Button symbols) | A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters; LB, RB, LT, RT, View, Menu |\n836\t  | Keyboard | a keycap with the bound key (`input.codeLabel`) |\n837\t  | Mouse | a mouse icon with the left or right button lit |\n838\t\n839\t  Never print raw codes. Build every prompt string from `input.label(action)` through the painter, so rebinding just works.\n840\t- **When the prompts change.** They switch the moment `lastDevice` changes: a pad button or a stick past the dead zone, a key, or a mouse move of more than 4 px or a click.\n841\t\n842\t### 5.6 Input map (all rebindable except the menu actions)\n843\t\n844\t| Action | Gamepad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n845\t|---|---|---|---|\n846\t| Step forward / back | Left stick ↑ ↓, D-pad ↑ ↓ | W / S, ↑ / ↓, Numpad 8 / 5 or 2 | — |\n847\t| Strafe left / right | Left stick ← → | A / D, Numpad 4 / 6 | — |\n848\t| Turn left / right | D-pad ← →, right stick ← → (a flick, repeating every 0.35 s while held) | Q / E, ← / →, Numpad 7 / 9 | — |\n849\t| Use / interact / take / place / stow | ✕ (tap) | F | left click on a thing in the view |\n850\t| Put the Hand's item down | ✕ (hold 0.4 s) | H | left click on the lower view |\n851\t| Throw the Hand's item | ○ | G | left click on the upper view |\n852\t| Party Strike | □ | Space | right click in the view |\n853\t| Select member previous / next | L1 / R1 | Tab / Shift+Tab, or 1–6 | click a portrait |\n854\t| Selected member's main / off hand | R2 / L2 | 1–6 main hand (and select); Shift+1–6 off hand | click a hand slot |\n855\t| Party screen | △ | I | portrait right click, HUD icon |\n856\t| Camp | L3 | C | HUD icon |\n857\t| Examine ahead | R3 | X | middle click |\n858\t| Automap | Create (acts on release; ignored when Options was pressed while Create was held) | M | HUD icon |\n859\t| Journal | (from the Party or Map tabs, or the pause menu) | J | HUD icon |\n860\t| Pause menu | Options | Esc, P | the engine's corner button |\n861\t| Menus | D-pad / stick, ✕ confirm, ○ back, L1 / R1 tabs | arrows, Enter, Backspace or Esc | hover, click, wheel |\n862\t\n863\t**Implementation notes.**\n864\t- Define your own play actions (`fwd, back, strafeL, strafeR, turnL, turnR, use, strike, selPrev, selNext, handMain, handOff, party, camp, examine, map, journal, throw, putDown`) in the play scene's `input` map, as the kit did.\n865\t- **Don't use the engine's merged `left/right` in play**: they merge the d-pad and the stick, and here they mean different things.\n866\t  - Read the left stick from `input.padAxes[0]` and `[1]`, with the dead zone and a 0.5 threshold, for move and strafe.\n867\t  - Read the right stick X from `padAxes[2]`, as a flick with re-arm, for turning.\n868\t  - The d-pad codes are `Pad12` to `Pad15`.\n869\t- **Hub rule.** While Create (`Pad8`) is held, ignore Options (`Pad9`), and don't toggle the map on Create's release if Options went down in between.\n870\t- **Idle detection** (attract mode): any action pressed, any `input.held` code, a stick past 0.3, a pointer move over 4 px or a click.\n871\t\n872\t### 5.7 Touch-only devices\n873\t\n874\t- **Detection.** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` with no `(hover: hover)`, and no keyboard or gamepad input seen yet. On such devices `input.touchEnabled = false`: no touch buttons and no stick.\n875\t- **The title** shows a panel: \"**Controller or keyboard required.** Connect a controller or a keyboard to play.\" The engine's top-right menu button stays and opens Settings.\n876\t- **The attract mode runs** after the same 10 s idle. A touch exits it to the title, which shows the notice again.\n877\t- **The notice hides** the moment a key or a pad button arrives, and play works normally from then on.\n878\t\n879\t---\n880\t\n881\t## 6. Presentation: art, animation and audio\n882\t\n883\t### 6.1 Art direction\n884\t\n885\t- **The base is the engine's native 3D look,** the one the spike showed: code-drawn textures, `world.res = 'pixels'` (chunky low-res pixels), toon light, outlines on, characters as **cards** (`world.look = 'card'`: the rigs drawn as pixel art from the camera's own angle) and fog for the source's distance falloff.\n886\t- **Lighting.** `torchShadows: 2`, `torchLights: 6`, the party's lantern as a `world.light` at the eyes, and `glowLights: 4` for spells.\n887\t- **New art** wherever the source's feel needs it: wall features, doors, floor items, the creatures in §6.3, the boss, the brass UI, item icons and the stages for the title and the cutscenes.\n888\t- **Props.** For generic set dressing (bones, barrels, crates, chains, cobwebs, banners, candles, tables), search the store first with `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"bones\"`, using `kaykit-dungeon-remastered`, `kenney-mini-dungeon` and `kenney-graveyard`. Cut a set with `tools/asset-set.mjs`, load it with `E.addAssets`, and place props with `world.shape(id, …)` or `{ shape }` in the legend.\n889\t\n890\t| Floor | Ground (`level.ground`, `colors`) | Walls (`side`, `color`) | Fog | Ambient `[sky, ground, power]` | Signature dressing |\n891\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n892\t| 1 The Hasp Drains | flagstone `#3e4a44 / #2a332f / #55615a`; a floor colour function paints dark-green **water channels** (6 units wide) down the Rat Channel and Cistern cells | brick `#4a5048` | `#06080a`, 24 → 150 | `#4a5a5a, #1a201e, 2.6` | floor drains (grates), dripping pipes (pen tubes), slime streaks, rusty chains, rat bones, sluice wheels |\n893\t| 2 The Tumbler Halls | tiles `#5a4a3a / #3e3228 / #7a6450` | block `#6a5a48` | `#0a0806`, 40 → 220 | `#7a6048, #2a2018, 3.0` | turning **wall gears** (pen rings of teeth, slowly rotating), anvils, workbenches, cogs on the floor, forge glow (orange lights) in the Workshop |\n894\t| 3 The Gloam Deeps | plain `#2a2434 / #1a1622 / #3a3248` | block `#2e2838` | `#040306`, 20 → 130 | `#3a3a5a, #141220, 2.2` | carved **eye reliefs** on walls (pen), shut-eye banners, bone piles, pale blue candles in the Chapel (lights) |\n895\t| 4 The Eye's Vault | tiles `#3a3e46`; the arena floor colour function adds faint cyan rune lines | block `#30343c` | `#020204`, 30 → 200 | `#2a3040, #0c0e14, 2.0` | the boss's amber light, cyan lightning flashes, the 3 Great Locks' rune lights going out as they turn |\n896\t\n897\t- **Item icons.** Make about 60 of them as `E.sprite` strings, designed at 12×12 and drawn at ×1 on the cards and ×2 in the Party screen. Shade them with `E.tones`.\n898\t- **Item models.** Each floor item also gets a tiny **3D model drawn with the pen**, lying flat at its quadrant spot:\n899\t  - blade (sword, dagger): a thin box with a cross-guard;\n900\t  - axe or mace: a head on a haft;\n901\t  - crossbow; bolts or arrows: thin tubes;\n902\t  - potion: a ball and a neck, in its colour;\n903\t  - scroll: a tube; book: a box;\n904\t  - key: a ring plus a shaft with teeth, in the key's colour;\n905\t  - rock: a grey lump of balls; ration: a wrapped box;\n906\t  - gear: a ring of boxes; ward: a toothed disc.\n907\t\n908\t### 6.2 The 3D presenter (`40–44`)\n909\t\n910\t- **Building a floor.**\n911\t  - Map each cell to 2×2 level tiles (`#` → `##`, open → `..`). A torch wall feature turns the tile on its open side into `t`, the kit's NICHE trick.\n912\t  - Make `new E.World3D(game, { level, look: 'card', res: 'pixels', torchLights: 6, torchShadows: 2, torchPower: 18, ambient, fog, sky: fogColour, camera: { mode: 'fixed' }, overlay: { fov: 60 }, onDraw })`.\n913\t  - Create all of the floor's lights at build time (lantern, candles, forge glows, the boss), as the engine requires.\n914\t  - Build every floor's world at boot and `prepare()` it, so a floor change never compiles a shader mid-game. On a floor change, swap the active world.\n915\t- **Each frame,** from the sim state:\n916\t  - **Camera:** `world.camera.fixed = { x, y, z: EYE + bob, yaw, pitch: −0.04, fov: 74 }`, from the party's tween (the kit's `place()`). A bump adds the lurch, a hit adds `game.shake`, a pit fall drops z by 40 and fades.\n917\t  - **Doors and gates:** `world.thing('door', …, { w: 32, h: 46, swing })`; set `t.open` toward the sim's state and call `world.setSolid` when it opens.\n918\t  - **Secret walls** (and **illusory walls**): a movable wall block that is **indistinguishable from the level's walls**. Try `world.box({ fixed: true, side: <the floor's side style>, color })` first; if it doesn't match, add a `wallBlock` helper to the 3D engine that reuses the level's wall material (risk 3, §13). A secret block sinks into the floor when opened; an illusory one never moves (the party's step through it is masked by the dissolve).\n919\t  - **Wall features:** custom `E.World3D.THINGS`, drawn with the pen on the wall face (local +y out of the wall, as the kit's `wallLever`).\n920\t\n921\t    | THING | How to draw it |\n922\t    |---|---|\n923\t    | `wallLever` | the kit's |\n924\t    | `wallButton` | a square stone and a brass boss that presses in |\n925\t    | `catch` | a slightly proud stone with a crack, or a brass rivet plate; glints under Seeing |\n926\t    | `alcove` | a dark recessed box that draws its items inside |\n927\t    | `keyhole` | per key shape: iron round, warded cross, gear toothed, bone skull, ward discs |\n928\t    | `plaque` | a stone tablet with dark glyph dashes |\n929\t    | `grate`, `pipe`, `chain`, `gearWall` (rotating), `eyeRelief` | decor |\n930\t    | `candle` | a holder, a wax stick and a flame glow when lit, paired with its light |\n931\t    | `greatLock` | a big wheel with rune glow, spun over 2 s |\n932\t    | `undergate` | a great door with 3 keyholes and 7 bolt bars that slide when a ward goes in |\n933\t    | `cage` | bars |\n934\t    | `shrine` | a ring of tiny rat statues |\n935\t    | `clockFace` | a dial with 4 gear sockets and hands that move once the clock is whole |\n936\t    | `altarSlab` | a floor slab |\n937\t    | `stairs` | the kit's `stair` (down), plus `stairUp` |\n938\t    | `pitEdge` | a dark hole with a lip; a bridge slab when bridged |\n939\t    | `plate` | the kit's |\n940\t    | `teleporterField` | a soft swirl glow, only when not silent |\n941\t\n942\t  - **Floor items:** pen models at the quadrant spots of the visible cells (`onDraw`).\n943\t  - **Monsters:** cards (`world.mob` with `rig`, `blob` or `orb`) are steered toward their slot's position with `vx`/`vy` as the kit does (`fly: true` for fliers). Their `anim` comes from the sim: attack phase, hurt flash, `die` pose, sink. Pen-drawn creatures are drawn in `onDraw` from their sim state and a local animation clock (§6.3).\n944\t  - **Projectiles and FX:** `world.glow` for bolts and orbs, pen bars for lightning, `game.particles` for sparks, embers, smoke and damage `text`, and `world.arc` for floor telegraphs (Bolt Rain).\n945\t  - **First-person overlay:** with `world.overlay.pen`, draw the striking member's weapon swinging across its side of the view: left for places 1, 3 and 5, right for 2, 4 and 6. The crossbow rises and kicks; a caster's hand glows in the spell's colour.\n946\t  - **Hover outlines and name tags:** project with `world.project`, then draw them in the HUD.\n947\t- **NullView** implements the same interface (`enter(floor)`, `frame(r)`, `event(e)`, `pick(sx, sy)`) as no-ops for Node.\n948\t\n949\t### 6.3 Creature recipes\n950\t\n951\tThe sizes follow the engine's guidance (heroes `size` 1.2 to 1.4, bosses larger). Every rig gets a real palette.\n952\t\n953\t| Creature | Build | Recipe | Animation notes |\n954\t|---|---|---|---|\n955\t| Drain Rat | card, `E.Blob` | `{ R: 3.6, ears: 'cat', tail: true, feet: true, mouth: 'fangs', colors: { base: '#6a5a50', dk: '#3e342e', lt: '#9a8a7a', eye: '#ff6a4a' } }` | fast squash scurry; the wind-up is a rear-back squash |\n956\t| Bloat Leech | pen | 5 balls (r 4 → 2.5) along a sine curve on the floor, `#b89a9a` with `#7a5a5a` rings; a mouth ring of 6 small balls; a wet glint | the body undulates; it lunges out 6 units on the strike |\n957\t| Latchkin | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: .85, build: 'chibi', hunch: .5, hood: true, outfit: 'tunic', bladeLen: 5, eyeGlow: '#ffd24a', colors: { skin: '#7a8a5a', hair: '#4a3a2a', cloth: '#6a4a32', pants: '#3a2e22', metal: '#9a9aa8' } }`, plus a pen ring of keys at the belt | the `thrust` move; it stays in a crouch-run while fleeing |\n958\t| Drain Ooze | card, `E.Blob` | `{ R: 8, face: 'front', mouth: true, colors: { base: '#5a8a3a', dk: '#2e5a22', lt: '#9ad06a', spec: '#e8ffd0' } }` | a slow wobble; it slams by squashing down; it splashes green particles |\n959\t| Rat Knot | pen | 6 rat-body balls in a ring with ear cones, tails as tubes knotted at the middle | the ring wobbles and turns; two bites a strike |\n960\t| Snick, the Latch-Boss | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.0, build: 'bulky', hunch: .3, hat: { style: 'crown', color: '#c8a040' }, armor: true, outfit: 'coat', bladeLen: 9, eyeGlow: '#ffd24a', colors: { skin: '#7a8a5a', cloth: '#5a3a2a', plate: '#a08040' } }`, plus a pen cape of hanging keys | the `overhead` move; it rattles its keys at 50% when it calls for help |\n961\t| Tinker Skeleton | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.2, build: 'skeleton', outfit: 'coat', bladeLen: 7, colors: { bone: '#d8cfb4', cloth: '#5a4030', metal: '#8a8a90' } }`, plus a pen hammer head on the blade | the `overhead` and `slash` moves; it collapses into bones on death |\n962\t| Rustling | card, `E.Blob` | `{ R: 4.2, horns: true, feet: true, mouth: true, colors: { base: '#b8642a', dk: '#7a3a18', lt: '#e8a060' } }` | the horns twitch; it throws rust-flake particles when it hits |\n963\t| Brass Spider | pen | abdomen ball r 7 and cephalothorax r 4.5 in `#b8923a`; 8 legs of 2 tubes each with procedural tetrapod gait (sine phase lifts); 4 red eye glows | legs step; it lunges on the strike; it falls apart into parts on death |\n964\t| Clockwork Sentinel | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.6, build: 'bulky', armor: true, hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#8a6a3a' }, bladeLen: 14, eyeGlow: '#ff8a3a', colors: { cloth: '#6a4a2a', plate: '#b08a4a', metal: '#c8a868', skin: '#5a4a3a' } }`, plus a pen wind-up key turning on its back | the `twohand` move with a 0.8 s wind-up; the key spins faster during it |\n965\t| Lidless Hound | pen | a quadruped (adapt the 3D horror kit's dogs, `src/starter3d/40-horror.js`): pale `#c8b8b0`, smooth eyeless head, long jaw | run, leap and bite |\n966\t| Gloam Seer | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', outfit: 'robe', hood: true, weapon: 'staff', eyeGlow: '#8ae0ff', colors: { cloth: '#d8d0e8', hair: '#3a2a4a', skin: '#b8b0c8', trim: '#6a4a9a' } }`, plus an eye-sigil glow over the staff | the `cast` pose while charging a Locking Bolt (cyan glow growing) |\n967\t| Gloam Warden | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.35, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#2a2434' }, weapon: 'staff', eyeGlow: '#e8e8ff', colors: { cloth: '#2a2434', plate: '#4a4458', metal: '#9a98a8', skin: '#b8b0c8' } }`, plus a pen halberd blade on the staff | the `thrust` move (reaches two cells) |\n968\t| Gazer | card orb plus pen | an orb `{ color: '#e8a040', r: 4 }`; pen iris (a flattened ball `#3a8a6a`), pupil, two lid slabs, 3 fin cones | blinks; the pupil tracks the party; it glows before Spark Gaze |\n969\t| Lockjaw Golem | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.9, build: 'bulky', armor: true, weapon: null, eyeGlow: '#7ad8ff', colors: { cloth: '#3a3e46', plate: '#5a606a', skin: '#4a4e56' } }`, plus a pen **padlock head** (box body and shackle tube) instead of a hat | the `haymaker` move; heavy footstep shakes |\n970\t| **The Bolt-Holder** | pen | an eye ball r 14 (sclera `#e8e0c8` with red vein bars); a flattened amber iris r 7 (`#e8a030`) facing the party; a black pupil r 3.5 that dilates; upper and lower lid slabs that blink and narrow for the Gaze; **7 radial iron bolts** (bars 22 long and 2.5 thick, with 5×5×3 heads) that slide out 2 per Great Lock (the 7th, the keystone, never moves); cyan lightning arcs (jagged pen bars) between neighbouring bolt heads; a ±3 bob; an amber `world.light`; red tint and crack lines in phase 3 | it must not read as a beholder: no eyestalks, no mouth, no tentacles. It reads as a caged iron lantern-eye. |\n971\t| Ghosts (race) | card, `E.Humanoid` | a hooded cloak in the player's seat colour, `fade 0.45`, a lantern glow in the seat colour, and the hub name as a HUD tag | they glide between the cells they report |\n972\t\n973\t**People** (portraits and cutscenes):\n974\t- the party (§7.9);\n975\t- Keywarden Imelda Strake: heroic, band crown, coat, navy and silver;\n976\t- Magister Abel Lintel: robe, pointed hat, long white hair;\n977\t- Ysolde Vane: robe, long hair, ghost blue, drawn translucent;\n978\t- Tamsin: size 0.9, ponytail, green hood;\n979\t- Durgan: bulky, braided beard, iron helmet;\n980\t- Veil: robe, hood, eyes shut (draw them closed).\n981\t\n982\t### 6.4 Animation list (new, on the engine's rigs, tweens and pen)\n983\t\n984\t- **Party view.** Step glide with bob; turn ease; bump lurch; pit fall (drop and fade); teleport (a cut or a swirl); spinner (a short wobble only when *visible*: none for silent spinners); hit shake; level-up glow on the card; KO slump (the portrait tinted grey); death toll (the card turns to bones); first-person weapon swings for each weapon family (blade, axe, blunt, spear thrust, crossbow, bow, sling, throw, punch); casting hand glows.\n985\t- **World.** Gates rising with a chain rattle and wooden doors swinging; levers flipping and buttons and catches pressing in; plates sinking; secret walls sinking; the trapdoor dropping open; the pit bridge rising; teleporter swirl; gears turning; candles lighting and guttering; the Great Locks spinning; the Undergate's 7 bolts drawing back one by one; the clock's hands moving once it is whole; the boss's bolts sliding, lids blinking, iris dilating and lightning arcing.\n986\t- **Monsters.** Idle, move glide, wind-up telegraph (the move's `wind` phase, at least 0.5 s), strike, hurt flash and recoil, death (the `die` pose, then sink; blobs squash flat; constructs fall apart into pen parts; the leech curls).\n987\t- **UI.** Cooldown wipes; damage pops; card shake; chevrons; focus pulse; toast slides; the title logo shimmer.\n988\t\n989\t### 6.5 Audio: code only, no files\n990\t\n991\t- **The music director.** It plays a song per floor through `game.audio.music(song, { fade: 1 })`. Every dungeon song carries a `fight` track (drums and bass, `on: false`) that comes in with `audio.layer('fight', 1, .5)` when any monster engages and goes out 4 s after the last one. Stings use `audio.sting`. All songs are original, in the step-sequencer format (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes }] }`).\n992\t\n993\t  | Song | Feel | Seed motif (original) |\n994\t  |---|---|---|\n995\t  | `title`, \"The City of a Thousand Locks\" | 76 bpm, D minor, bell pulses over a low triangle | `D4 - F4 A4 G4 - F4 E4 \\| D4 - - - C4 E4 G4 F4 \\| E4 - D4 C#4 D4 - - -` |\n996\t  | `drains` | 60 bpm, a sparse drone, a lonely pulse every 8 bars, drips as sfx | low `A1` drone; a `pulse25` figure `A3 . C4 . E4 . . . \\| D4 . C4 . B3 . . .` |\n997\t  | `tumbler` | 96 bpm in 6/8, ticking percussion | `drums` `h . h c . h` over `E2 . B2 E2 . B2` |\n998\t  | `gloam` | 54 bpm, sine pads, a tritone colour | `C3` plus `F#3` pads, a choir-like sine line in long notes |\n999\t  | `vault` / `boss` | 138 bpm, saw bass, lightning arpeggios | `A2 A2 . A2 C3 . A2 . \\| G2 G2 . G2 Bb2 . G2 .`, arps `A4 E5 A5 E5` |\n1000\t  | `camp` | 70 bpm, a lullaby | the title motif in a major key, triangle |\n1001\t  | `ending` | 84 bpm, D major | the title motif resolved |\n1002\t  | stings | — | `levelUp`, `deed`, `secret`, `wardFound`, `gameOver`, `victory`, `npcJoin` |\n1003\t\n1004\t- **Sound effects** (`audio.define`, the engine's voice format; the kit's `SOUNDS` are the template). Define every one of these:\n1005\t  - steps per floor: wet stone, metal grating, dust, stone;\n1006\t  - turn, bump, door open and close, gate grind, lever clank, button click, catch click, plate thunk, secret-wall grind, illusion shimmer, trapdoor, fall and land;\n1007\t  - teleporter whoosh (visible ones only), spinner grind, dart volley;\n1008\t  - key turn, lock fail, lockpick scratch and snap, alcove place and take;\n1009\t  - item pickup, drop and throw whoosh, land clatter, eat crunch, drink glug, scroll unroll, wand zap;\n1010\t  - swings (blade, axe, blunt), crossbow and bow twangs, sling whirr;\n1011\t  - hits (flesh, metal, bone, ooze), miss whiff, crit clang, party hurt;\n1012\t  - level up, death toll, raise;\n1013\t  - each spell: cast, travel loop and impact;\n1014\t  - monster voices for each type (squeak, slurp, chitter, rattle, clank, growl, chant, gaze hum);\n1015\t  - boss: roar, lightning crack, bolt slam, lock turn and shriek;\n1016\t  - UI: move, confirm, back, error, tab;\n1017\t  - ambience loops per floor: drips, distant gears, whispers, a low hum.\n1018\t\n1019\t  Pan by bearing with the patched `{ pan }` (§3.9).\n1020\t- **Voices: the babble synth** (`50-audio.js`, on `game.audio.ctx` and routed to the voice bus).\n1021\t  - **Per-speaker profile:** `{ base: Hz, range: semitones, rate: syllables/s, wave, formant: ×, breath: 0..1, vib: [Hz, depth], echo: 0..1 }`. The profiles:\n1022\t\n1023\t    | Speaker | base Hz | range | rate | wave | other |\n1024\t    |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1025\t    | Narrator | 95 | 3 | 9 | triangle | echo 0.2 |\n1026\t    | Keywarden | 170 | 4 | 12 | square | |\n1027\t    | Lintel | 120 | 5 | 10 | saw | |\n1028\t    | Tamsin | 300 | 7 | 15 | pulse | |\n1029\t    | Durgan | 105 | 4 | 11 | square | |\n1030\t    | Ysolde | 220 | 5 | 9 | sine | echo 0.4 |\n1031\t    | Veil | 240 | 3 | 10 | triangle | breath 0.5 |\n1032\t    | Snick | 260 | 8 | 16 | square | |\n1033\t    | Bolt-Holder | 70 | 6 | 8 | saw | ring mod 30 Hz, echo 0.5 |\n1034\t\n1035\t    The party members also get profiles from their race and sex.\n1036\t  - **Driving it.** Set `talk.voice = null`. Each update, read `talk.shown`; for every two new letters (and on every vowel), emit a syllable.\n1037\t  - **The syllable:**\n1038\t    - The vowel picks a formant pair (F1/F2): a 800/1200, e 400/2000, i 300/2300, o 450/800, u 325/700, × `formant`.\n1039\t    - The letter's code picks the pitch inside `range`, so a word always sounds the same.\n1040\t    - Plosives (b d g k p t) add a 10 ms noise click; fricatives (f s v z h) add a 40 ms high-passed noise; nasals (m n) add a low hum.\n1041\t    - `?` rises at the end, `!` is louder and higher, `.` pauses 0.15 s and `,` pauses 0.08 s.\n1042\t  - **The voice:** an oscillator into two parallel band-pass filters (Q 8) into a gain envelope (5 ms attack, 0.6× hold, 30 ms release), then the bus.\n1043\t  - **Barks:** short babble for party pain (\"Oof!\"), effort (\"Hah!\"), level-up (\"Yes!\") and casting, and for monster taunts.\n1044\t  - **Test it in Node.** `babble.plan(text, profile)` returns syllable events (time, pitch, F1, F2, noise) and must be deterministic (§14). In Node there is no AudioContext, so the synth is a no-op.\n1045\t\n1046\t---\n1047\t\n1048\t## 7. Content\n1049\t\n1050\t### 7.1 World bible (all original)\n1051\t\n1052\t- **Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks.** A walled river port that grew rich on locks. Its sigil is a key crossed with a bolt. It is ruled by the **Council of Keys** under **High Keywarden Imelda Strake**, stern and practical. The city's wizard is **Magister Abel Lintel**, dry, old and worried.\n1053\t- **The Tumblerwrights' Guild.** The finest locksmiths in the world. Their halls sat under the city until a disaster sealed them a century ago. Their last guildmistress, **Ysolde Vane**, haunts the halls.\n1054\t- **The Undergate.** The guild's masterwork: a gate beneath the city's foundations that seals something older, which this demo only hints at. To watch it, the guild forged **the Watcher**, an eye that never closes, and gave it **seven Great Bolts** to hold. The Watcher learned to love holding. It now calls itself **the Bolt-Holder** and turns the locks of Hasp from below: \"Every lock is a promise. I keep them all.\"\n1055\t- **Its servants.**\n1056\t  - **Latchkins**: small sewer goblins who worship keys and steal iron. Their chief is **Snick, the Latch-Boss**.\n1057\t  - The guild's own clockwork and its dead workers, wound up again.\n1058\t  - The **Gloam-folk** of the deep, pale people who believe the Eye is a god and call themselves the **Lidless**: their Seers, Wardens and hounds.\n1059\t- **The quest.** Gather the **Three Wards** (copper from the Drains, brass from the Guild Vault, iron from the Lidless Reliquary), open the Undergate and close the Eye.\n1060\t- **The humour.** The title is playful; the prose is earnest. Wit comes in small doses: Tamsin's quips, dry item descriptions, the Rat King's crown.\n1061\t\n1062\t### 7.2 Writing style guide\n1063\t\n1064\tThe model is the source's voice: terse, second-person, past the fourth wall never.\n1065\t\n1066\t- **Messages** are one short sentence, present tense, addressed to \"you\" or naming a member. Use plain words and full stops; keep exclamation marks for danger and wonder.\n1067\t  - \"You hear a click in the distance.\"\n1068\t  - \"There is a loose stone here.\"\n1069\t  - \"The bolt will not move.\"\n1070\t  - \"This key does not fit.\"\n1071\t  - \"The Iron Key turns. The door opens.\"\n1072\t  - \"A hidden door grinds open!\"\n1073\t  - \"The wall is an illusion!\"\n1074\t  - \"The floor gives way!\"\n1075\t  - \"You feel a twisting underfoot.\"\n1076\t  - \"Nothing happens.\"\n1077\t  - \"Darts fly from the walls!\"\n1078\t  - \"Cobb is hungry.\"\n1079\t  - \"Odelia is poisoned!\"\n1080\t  - \"Brannoc swings at the air.\"\n1081\t  - \"The Drain Rat dies.\"\n1082\t  - \"You may not rest with enemies near.\"\n1083\t  - \"You are awakened by something moving!\"\n1084\t  - \"A hidden deed is done!\"\n1085\t  - \"Ysa has reached level 3!\"\n1086\t- **Inscriptions** are in CAPITALS, formal and a little archaic: \"NOT EVERY STONE IS STONE.\"\n1087\t- **Narration** (intro and ending) is stately, in the third person, with short paragraphs and one image each.\n1088\t- **NPCs** speak formally but each in their own voice:\n1089\t  - Tamsin: quick and cheeky.\n1090\t  - Durgan: gruff and practical.\n1091\t  - Ysolde: sorrowful and old-fashioned.\n1092\t  - Veil: hushed and strange, always talking about eyes.\n1093\t  - The Bolt-Holder: grand, slow, with CAPITALS for its emphasis.\n1094\t- **Item descriptions** are 1 to 2 dry sentences: \"It has seen better days, and better locks.\"\n1095\t- **Never** use modern slang, game-mechanic jargon in the fiction (say \"slots\" only in menus), or the source's names.\n1096\t\n1097\t### 7.3 Story beats and cutscenes\n1098\t\n1099\tThe cutscenes are 3D stages, small `E.World3D` sets built at boot, with rigs, a camera moved by `game.script` and `game.tween`, dialogue boxes, babble voices and stings. Any button speeds the text; holding Options or Esc for 1 s skips the cutscene.\n1100\t\n1101\t**INTRO** (about 75 s):\n1102\t- *Stage A*, the Council Hall at night: pillars, banners, two braziers, a long table, Strake and Lintel.\n1103\t- *Stage B*, the Drain-gate: a stair down into the dark, a grate, and the party's four rigs.\n1104\t\n1105\t1. NARRATOR: \"Hasp. The city of a thousand locks.\"\n1106\t2. NARRATOR: \"For three hundred years its Tumblerwrights forged the finest locks in the world, and its people slept soundly behind them.\"\n1107\t3. NARRATOR: \"Then the locks began to turn by themselves.\"\n1108\t4. STRAKE: \"Doors bolt themselves in empty houses. Wells cap shut. Last night the east granary locked forty men inside and would not open for axes.\"\n1109\t5. LINTEL: \"It comes from below. Past the Drains, past the old guild halls… from the Undergate itself.\"\n1110\t6. STRAKE: \"You were recommended to me as people who open things. Go down. Find what holds our bolts, and break its grip.\"\n1111\t7. *(Stage B)* NARRATOR: \"The Drain-gate groans open. You step down into the dark.\"\n1112\t8. NARRATOR: \"Behind you, the grate crashes shut. A great bolt slides home with a sound like a closing tomb.\"\n1113\t   - The camera shakes, a pen-drawn bolt slides across the grate, and the torches gutter.\n1114\t9. NARRATOR: \"Somewhere far below, something blinks.\"\n1115\t   - Cut to black. An amber eye opens for 0.5 s, with the boss's hum.\n1116\t10. Play begins on floor 1 with the log line \"The grate above is bolted fast. There is no way back.\"\n1117\t\n1118\t**Mid-game beats** (in-dungeon, through E.Story):\n1119\t- Tamsin freed (floor 1).\n1120\t- Snick's barks.\n1121\t- Ysolde's ghost twice (floor 2).\n1122\t- Durgan (floor 2).\n1123\t- Veil's choice (floor 3).\n1124\t- The Undergate opening (floor 3, a 10 s in-engine moment: the camera holds, seven bolts draw back one by one, a deep chord).\n1125\t- The Bolt-Holder's taunts (floor 4).\n1126\t\n1127\t**ENDING** (about 60 s):\n1128\t- *Stage C*, the Vault after the battle: light pours from the cracked Eye.\n1129\t- *Stage D*, the city square at dawn: houses, doors swinging open, Strake, Lintel and the party.\n1130\t\n1131\t1. NARRATOR: \"The Eye cracks from lid to lid, and its light goes out.\"\n1132\t2. NARRATOR: \"One by one, the seven Great Bolts fall silent.\"\n1133\t3. *(Stage D)* NARRATOR: \"Above, in the city of a thousand locks, every door swings open at once.\"\n1134\t4. STRAKE: \"Hasp is in your debt. The guild's last forge will cast your names into the Undergate itself.\"\n1135\t5. LINTEL: \"Yet the Undergate was built to keep something in. With its Watcher gone…\"\n1136\t6. NARRATOR: \"Far beneath the city, in a darkness older than locks, something tries the door.\"\n1137\t7. Title card **THE END**, then the credits roll (§11), then the stats: time, kills, secrets found out of 3, hidden deeds out of 3, deaths and the party's levels. Back to the title.\n1138\t\n1139\t### 7.4 The floors\n1140\t\n1141\t- **Legend** (one character per cell):\n1142\t\n1143\t  | Char | Meaning |\n1144\t  |---|---|\n1145\t  | `#` | wall |\n1146\t  | `.` | floor |\n1147\t  | `@` | start or arrival |\n1148\t  | `<` `>` | stairs |\n1149\t  | `+` | wooden door |\n1150\t  | `=` | gate |\n1151\t  | `L` `K` | locked door |\n1152\t  | `M` | magic seal |\n1153\t  | `H` | secret wall |\n1154\t  | `I` | illusory wall |\n1155\t  | `P` | plate |\n1156\t  | `T` | dart trap |\n1157\t  | `O` | pit |\n1158\t  | `o` | trapdoor |\n1159\t  | `^` | teleporter |\n1160\t  | `S` | spinner |\n1161\t  | `U` | the Undergate |\n1162\t  | `*` | pit landing |\n1163\t  | `B` | the boss |\n1164\t\n1165\t- **Coordinates** are (x, y) with y = 0 at the top; the rulers above each map give x.\n1166\t- **Wall features** are given as *(stand x, y, face)*.\n1167\t- **Checked during planning:** every walkthrough below was checked with a reachability script (each target reachable with only the doors opened so far, and every gated area unreachable before its gate). It came to 157 checks, including both of Veil's branches and the Sump. Your content lint repeats them (§14).\n1168\t\n1169\t#### 7.4.1 Floor 1: The Hasp Drains (22×17)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"6dd9d35f-a5fa-451c-9ab2-b4051a0019e3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:53:38.988Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261103-185330__x53986f/work/PLAN.md","content":"- **Keyboard and mouse are native, not stand-ins.** The same screens, with hover moving the focus, click acting, drag and drop in the Party screen, the mouse wheel scrolling lists and the Hand following the cursor (§5.4).\n- **Prompts always follow `input.lastDevice`** (§5.5).\n\n### 5.2 The play HUD (over the full-screen 3D view)\n\n| Element | Where (game pixels) | Content |\n|---|---|---|\n| Compass | top centre, 64×16 | \"W  **N**  E\", with the facing letter at scale 2. It changes instantly on spinners: the only clue. |\n| Floor toast | top left | the floor's name for 3 s on arrival |\n| Party bar | bottom, `H−50` to `H−2` | one card per member in formation order. Row groups are 4 px apart, with tiny labels FRONT, MID and BACK above them. |\n| Card (about 66×46) | — | live portrait 30×30 (`drawPortrait`, wince when hurt, grey when Down, bones when Dead); the name (cut to 7 letters); HP bar and `hp/max`; a 2-px food strip under the portrait; **two hand slots** 15×15 with the item icon, a cooldown wipe (dark, sweeping), a \"ready\" glint and MISS or damage pops; status icons in a row of 6×6 (poison drop, chain, Zz, shield, bowl) |\n| Selected card | — | brass double frame; the L2 and R2 glyph badges under its hand slots |\n| Log | above the bar, left | 3 lines, newest at the bottom, with a shadow |\n| Prompts | above the bar, right | up to 4 `[glyph] Verb`, e.g. `✕ Pull lever · □ Strike · △ Party · Create Map` |\n| Hand | above the bar, centre | held item icon and its name (unless the mouse cursor carries it) |\n| Hit chevrons | screen edges | red chevrons on the left, right or bottom edge for 0.6 s on flank or rear hits; a red vignette on front-row hits |\n| Mouse helpers | top right under the engine corner | small icons for Map, Camp, Party and Journal, mouse only; hidden for the pad |\n| Boss bar | under the compass | THE BOLT-HOLDER, 200×6, with 3 lock pips (turned or locked) |\n| Race HUD | top left | timer `02:13.4`, checkpoint label, crowns, and in a session the standings in seat colours |\n\nKeep clear of `game.cornerArea()`, the engine's top-right menu button.\n\n### 5.3 Screens\n\nEvery screen is drawn over the dimmed 3D view, takes focus navigation (§5.4) and shows its prompts.\n\n- **Party (△, I).**\n  - **Top:** member tabs, one 32-px portrait each (L1 and R1 switch).\n  - **Left:** the paperdoll, a large live rig portrait framed by 20×20 slots: Head, Neck, Body, Arms, Main, Off, Ring, Ring, Feet, Quiver.\n  - **Centre:** the backpack, 4×3 slots of 22×22.\n  - **Right:** details: a 32×32 icon, the name, a type line, stats (damage, speed, Defense, classes) and the description (§7.7 tone).\n  - **Bottom:** `HP 11/11 · Defense 14 · Lv 2 Fighter · XP 140/250 · Food ███░`.\n  - **Actions:** ✕ takes or places (it carries the item into the Hand, or swaps), □ Use, △ Drop at your feet, R2 Sheet. ○ returns a carried item, then closes the screen.\n  - **The Sheet tab** shows the stats and their modifiers, class abilities, resistances and the spellbook or the prayers. Dismiss (NPCs) is here, behind a confirm.\n- **Cast.** Opened by a caster's book or symbol hand.\n  - A centred panel of about 260×150, with level tabs L1, L2, L3 (shoulders switch them).\n  - Rows read \"Spark Dart ×2\", with a description and the target type.\n  - ✕ casts. A `member` target then moves the highlight across the cards (◀ ▶, ✕). A `line`, `front`, `door` or `party` spell fires at once. ○ cancels.\n- **Camp (L3, C).** The view darkens and a small campfire light glows in the cell. Tiles in 2 rows of 5 (§4.9).\n  - **Rest** shows a rest panel: the hours counting, HP ticking up on the cards and ○ Stop.\n  - **Memorize** and **Pray**: known spells on the left (grouped by level, with descriptions) and slots on the right. ✕ on a spell fills the next free slot of its level, ✕ on a slot empties it, △ \"Same as before\".\n  - **Scribe**: scrolls in the party's packs that the mage doesn't know; ✕ learns one.\n  - **Formation**: the 2×3 places; ✕ twice swaps two members.\n  - **Eat**: a confirm, then a summary of who ate what.\n  - **Save** and **Load** open the engine's slot screens (`saves.show`).\n  - **Settings** opens `game.menu('settings')`.\n- **Map (Create, M).**\n  - A parchment-coloured panel with the floor's explored cells at 8 to 10 px per cell, and a legend row at the bottom.\n  - L1 and R1 switch between visited floors; the d-pad or stick pans a map larger than the screen; ○ closes.\n  - Dev *reveal* shows everything.\n- **Journal (J, or the tab from Party or Map).** Quests, Notes, Deeds and Log. Notes open the **Reader**, a parchment page with the note's text at the normal size, paged with the d-pad.\n- **Dialogue.** `E.Dialog` at the bottom: portrait on the left, name in brass and 3 lines a page. ✕ finishes the line, then turns the page; ○ fast-forwards; choices are a vertical list.\n- **Title.**\n  - **The view.** The Drain-gate stage from the intro (§7.3, stage B) at night. The camera pushes slowly toward the grate, the torches flicker, and every 12 s the great bolt slides home with a shake and a thud.\n  - **The logo.** **EYE OF THE BOLT-HOLDER** in `E.font.title`, scale 4, brass colours (§5.1), with a slow glint across it, and \"A thousand locks. One eye.\" under it.\n  - **The menu,** a centred `E.Menu`: Continue (only when a save exists), New Game, Load Game, Rat Run, Settings, About & Credits.\n  - **The bottom line:** the prompts for the last device and the version (`E.versionLabel()`, plus the game's own version).\n  - **Music:** `title`.\n  - **The attract mode** starts after 10 s idle (§9). The touch notice is shown on touch-only devices (§5.7).\n- **Creation:** §4.4. **The floor card:** §4.1. **About and Credits:** §11. **Touch notice:** §5.7.\n- **Game over.** \"THE PARTY IS LOST\" in the title font over the frozen, darkened view; one line from the floor (\"The Drains keep what they take.\"); then Load last save · Load game · Title.\n- **Ending.** The ending cutscene (§7.3), then the credits roll (the §11 Credits page, scrolling), then the stats panel (time, kills, secrets 3, deeds 3, deaths, levels) and Title. The `ending` song plays.\n\n### 5.4 Focus navigation and the mouse\n\n- **One focus system** (`61-ui-kit.js`) for every screen.\n  - Each screen registers its widgets as rects with an id, an action and optional explicit neighbours.\n  - The d-pad and the left stick (menus use the engine's `up/down/left/right`, which include the stick) move to the nearest widget in that direction, by geometry; explicit neighbours override.\n  - Holding a direction repeats (`input.repeat`, 0.35 s, then 0.1 s).\n- **The mouse.**\n  - Moving the pointer over a widget focuses it; a left click acts as ✕; a right click on a slot acts as □; the wheel scrolls lists.\n  - Party screen drag and drop: mouse-down carries the item, mouse-up places it.\n- **Mouse in the 3D view.** Each frame, project the interactables in front with `world.project`: the front wall's feature, the items in the front and own cells, doors, the monsters in front and alcoves. Each gets a screen hotspot.\n  - Hovering one outlines it (a soft brass rim drawn with the pen around the thing) and shows a tooltip, e.g. \"Lever — click to pull\".\n  - A left click on a hotspot uses it. With an item in the Hand, a left click on the lower view puts it down and a left click on the upper view throws it, as in the source.\n  - A right click anywhere in the view is **Party Strike**.\n- **Party cards.** A click on a hand slot uses that hand (the source's core gesture); a click on a portrait selects; a right click on a portrait opens that member in the Party screen.\n\n### 5.5 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- **One painter, `glyph(g, x, y, action)`**, draws a badge for the action's binding on the last device:\n\n  | Device | Badge |\n  |---|---|\n  | Pad, PlayStation (default) | ✕ blue, ○ red, □ pink, △ green, drawn as shapes on a dark disc; L1, R1, L2, R2, L3, R3, Options and Create as rounded labels; D-pad and stick icons |\n  | Pad, Xbox (Settings → Controls → Button symbols) | A green, B red, X blue, Y yellow letters; LB, RB, LT, RT, View, Menu |\n  | Keyboard | a keycap with the bound key (`input.codeLabel`) |\n  | Mouse | a mouse icon with the left or right button lit |\n\n  Never print raw codes. Build every prompt string from `input.label(action)` through the painter, so rebinding just works.\n- **When the prompts change.** They switch the moment `lastDevice` changes: a pad button or a stick past the dead zone, a key, or a mouse move of more than 4 px or a click.\n\n### 5.6 Input map (all rebindable except the menu actions)\n\n| Action | Gamepad (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Step forward / back | Left stick ↑ ↓, D-pad ↑ ↓ | W / S, ↑ / ↓, Numpad 8 / 5 or 2 | — |\n| Strafe left / right | Left stick ← → | A / D, Numpad 4 / 6 | — |\n| Turn left / right | D-pad ← →, right stick ← → (a flick, repeating every 0.35 s while held) | Q / E, ← / →, Numpad 7 / 9 | — |\n| Use / interact / take / place / stow | ✕ (tap) | F | left click on a thing in the view |\n| Put the Hand's item down | ✕ (hold 0.4 s) | H | left click on the lower view |\n| Throw the Hand's item | ○ | G | left click on the upper view |\n| Party Strike | □ | Space | right click in the view |\n| Select member previous / next | L1 / R1 | Tab / Shift+Tab, or 1–6 | click a portrait |\n| Selected member's main / off hand | R2 / L2 | 1–6 main hand (and select); Shift+1–6 off hand | click a hand slot |\n| Party screen | △ | I | portrait right click, HUD icon |\n| Camp | L3 | C | HUD icon |\n| Examine ahead | R3 | X | middle click |\n| Automap | Create (acts on release; ignored when Options was pressed while Create was held) | M | HUD icon |\n| Journal | (from the Party or Map tabs, or the pause menu) | J | HUD icon |\n| Pause menu | Options | Esc, P | the engine's corner button |\n| Menus | D-pad / stick, ✕ confirm, ○ back, L1 / R1 tabs | arrows, Enter, Backspace or Esc | hover, click, wheel |\n\n**Implementation notes.**\n- Define your own play actions (`fwd, back, strafeL, strafeR, turnL, turnR, use, strike, selPrev, selNext, handMain, handOff, party, camp, examine, map, journal, throw, putDown`) in the play scene's `input` map, as the kit did.\n- **Don't use the engine's merged `left/right` in play**: they merge the d-pad and the stick, and here they mean different things.\n  - Read the left stick from `input.padAxes[0]` and `[1]`, with the dead zone and a 0.5 threshold, for move and strafe.\n  - Read the right stick X from `padAxes[2]`, as a flick with re-arm, for turning.\n  - The d-pad codes are `Pad12` to `Pad15`.\n- **Hub rule.** While Create (`Pad8`) is held, ignore Options (`Pad9`), and don't toggle the map on Create's release if Options went down in between.\n- **Idle detection** (attract mode): any action pressed, any `input.held` code, a stick past 0.3, a pointer move over 4 px or a click.\n\n### 5.7 Touch-only devices\n\n- **Detection.** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` with no `(hover: hover)`, and no keyboard or gamepad input seen yet. On such devices `input.touchEnabled = false`: no touch buttons and no stick.\n- **The title** shows a panel: \"**Controller or keyboard required.** Connect a controller or a keyboard to play.\" The engine's top-right menu button stays and opens Settings.\n- **The attract mode runs** after the same 10 s idle. A touch exits it to the title, which shows the notice again.\n- **The notice hides** the moment a key or a pad button arrives, and play works normally from then on.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Presentation: art, animation and audio\n\n### 6.1 Art direction\n\n- **The base is the engine's native 3D look,** the one the spike showed: code-drawn textures, `world.res = 'pixels'` (chunky low-res pixels), toon light, outlines on, characters as **cards** (`world.look = 'card'`: the rigs drawn as pixel art from the camera's own angle) and fog for the source's distance falloff.\n- **Lighting.** `torchShadows: 2`, `torchLights: 6`, the party's lantern as a `world.light` at the eyes, and `glowLights: 4` for spells.\n- **New art** wherever the source's feel needs it: wall features, doors, floor items, the creatures in §6.3, the boss, the brass UI, item icons and the stages for the title and the cutscenes.\n- **Props.** For generic set dressing (bones, barrels, crates, chains, cobwebs, banners, candles, tables), search the store first with `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"bones\"`, using `kaykit-dungeon-remastered`, `kenney-mini-dungeon` and `kenney-graveyard`. Cut a set with `tools/asset-set.mjs`, load it with `E.addAssets`, and place props with `world.shape(id, …)` or `{ shape }` in the legend.\n\n| Floor | Ground (`level.ground`, `colors`) | Walls (`side`, `color`) | Fog | Ambient `[sky, ground, power]` | Signature dressing |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 The Hasp Drains | flagstone `#3e4a44 / #2a332f / #55615a`; a floor colour function paints dark-green **water channels** (6 units wide) down the Rat Channel and Cistern cells | brick `#4a5048` | `#06080a`, 24 → 150 | `#4a5a5a, #1a201e, 2.6` | floor drains (grates), dripping pipes (pen tubes), slime streaks, rusty chains, rat bones, sluice wheels |\n| 2 The Tumbler Halls | tiles `#5a4a3a / #3e3228 / #7a6450` | block `#6a5a48` | `#0a0806`, 40 → 220 | `#7a6048, #2a2018, 3.0` | turning **wall gears** (pen rings of teeth, slowly rotating), anvils, workbenches, cogs on the floor, forge glow (orange lights) in the Workshop |\n| 3 The Gloam Deeps | plain `#2a2434 / #1a1622 / #3a3248` | block `#2e2838` | `#040306`, 20 → 130 | `#3a3a5a, #141220, 2.2` | carved **eye reliefs** on walls (pen), shut-eye banners, bone piles, pale blue candles in the Chapel (lights) |\n| 4 The Eye's Vault | tiles `#3a3e46`; the arena floor colour function adds faint cyan rune lines | block `#30343c` | `#020204`, 30 → 200 | `#2a3040, #0c0e14, 2.0` | the boss's amber light, cyan lightning flashes, the 3 Great Locks' rune lights going out as they turn |\n\n- **Item icons.** Make about 60 of them as `E.sprite` strings, designed at 12×12 and drawn at ×1 on the cards and ×2 in the Party screen. Shade them with `E.tones`.\n- **Item models.** Each floor item also gets a tiny **3D model drawn with the pen**, lying flat at its quadrant spot:\n  - blade (sword, dagger): a thin box with a cross-guard;\n  - axe or mace: a head on a haft;\n  - crossbow; bolts or arrows: thin tubes;\n  - potion: a ball and a neck, in its colour;\n  - scroll: a tube; book: a box;\n  - key: a ring plus a shaft with teeth, in the key's colour;\n  - rock: a grey lump of balls; ration: a wrapped box;\n  - gear: a ring of boxes; ward: a toothed disc.\n\n### 6.2 The 3D presenter (`40–44`)\n\n- **Building a floor.**\n  - Map each cell to 2×2 level tiles (`#` → `##`, open → `..`). A torch wall feature turns the tile on its open side into `t`, the kit's NICHE trick.\n  - Make `new E.World3D(game, { level, look: 'card', res: 'pixels', torchLights: 6, torchShadows: 2, torchPower: 18, ambient, fog, sky: fogColour, camera: { mode: 'fixed' }, overlay: { fov: 60 }, onDraw })`.\n  - Create all of the floor's lights at build time (lantern, candles, forge glows, the boss), as the engine requires.\n  - Build every floor's world at boot and `prepare()` it, so a floor change never compiles a shader mid-game. On a floor change, swap the active world.\n- **Each frame,** from the sim state:\n  - **Camera:** `world.camera.fixed = { x, y, z: EYE + bob, yaw, pitch: −0.04, fov: 74 }`, from the party's tween (the kit's `place()`). A bump adds the lurch, a hit adds `game.shake`, a pit fall drops z by 40 and fades.\n  - **Doors and gates:** `world.thing('door', …, { w: 32, h: 46, swing })`; set `t.open` toward the sim's state and call `world.setSolid` when it opens.\n  - **Secret walls** (and **illusory walls**): a movable wall block that is **indistinguishable from the level's walls**. Try `world.box({ fixed: true, side: <the floor's side style>, color })` first; if it doesn't match, add a `wallBlock` helper to the 3D engine that reuses the level's wall material (risk 3, §13). A secret block sinks into the floor when opened; an illusory one never moves (the party's step through it is masked by the dissolve).\n  - **Wall features:** custom `E.World3D.THINGS`, drawn with the pen on the wall face (local +y out of the wall, as the kit's `wallLever`).\n\n    | THING | How to draw it |\n    |---|---|\n    | `wallLever` | the kit's |\n    | `wallButton` | a square stone and a brass boss that presses in |\n    | `catch` | a slightly proud stone with a crack, or a brass rivet plate; glints under Seeing |\n    | `alcove` | a dark recessed box that draws its items inside |\n    | `keyhole` | per key shape: iron round, warded cross, gear toothed, bone skull, ward discs |\n    | `plaque` | a stone tablet with dark glyph dashes |\n    | `grate`, `pipe`, `chain`, `gearWall` (rotating), `eyeRelief` | decor |\n    | `candle` | a holder, a wax stick and a flame glow when lit, paired with its light |\n    | `greatLock` | a big wheel with rune glow, spun over 2 s |\n    | `undergate` | a great door with 3 keyholes and 7 bolt bars that slide when a ward goes in |\n    | `cage` | bars |\n    | `shrine` | a ring of tiny rat statues |\n    | `clockFace` | a dial with 4 gear sockets and hands that move once the clock is whole |\n    | `altarSlab` | a floor slab |\n    | `stairs` | the kit's `stair` (down), plus `stairUp` |\n    | `pitEdge` | a dark hole with a lip; a bridge slab when bridged |\n    | `plate` | the kit's |\n    | `teleporterField` | a soft swirl glow, only when not silent |\n\n  - **Floor items:** pen models at the quadrant spots of the visible cells (`onDraw`).\n  - **Monsters:** cards (`world.mob` with `rig`, `blob` or `orb`) are steered toward their slot's position with `vx`/`vy` as the kit does (`fly: true` for fliers). Their `anim` comes from the sim: attack phase, hurt flash, `die` pose, sink. Pen-drawn creatures are drawn in `onDraw` from their sim state and a local animation clock (§6.3).\n  - **Projectiles and FX:** `world.glow` for bolts and orbs, pen bars for lightning, `game.particles` for sparks, embers, smoke and damage `text`, and `world.arc` for floor telegraphs (Bolt Rain).\n  - **First-person overlay:** with `world.overlay.pen`, draw the striking member's weapon swinging across its side of the view: left for places 1, 3 and 5, right for 2, 4 and 6. The crossbow rises and kicks; a caster's hand glows in the spell's colour.\n  - **Hover outlines and name tags:** project with `world.project`, then draw them in the HUD.\n- **NullView** implements the same interface (`enter(floor)`, `frame(r)`, `event(e)`, `pick(sx, sy)`) as no-ops for Node.\n\n### 6.3 Creature recipes\n\nThe sizes follow the engine's guidance (heroes `size` 1.2 to 1.4, bosses larger). Every rig gets a real palette.\n\n| Creature | Build | Recipe | Animation notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Drain Rat | card, `E.Blob` | `{ R: 3.6, ears: 'cat', tail: true, feet: true, mouth: 'fangs', colors: { base: '#6a5a50', dk: '#3e342e', lt: '#9a8a7a', eye: '#ff6a4a' } }` | fast squash scurry; the wind-up is a rear-back squash |\n| Bloat Leech | pen | 5 balls (r 4 → 2.5) along a sine curve on the floor, `#b89a9a` with `#7a5a5a` rings; a mouth ring of 6 small balls; a wet glint | the body undulates; it lunges out 6 units on the strike |\n| Latchkin | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: .85, build: 'chibi', hunch: .5, hood: true, outfit: 'tunic', bladeLen: 5, eyeGlow: '#ffd24a', colors: { skin: '#7a8a5a', hair: '#4a3a2a', cloth: '#6a4a32', pants: '#3a2e22', metal: '#9a9aa8' } }`, plus a pen ring of keys at the belt | the `thrust` move; it stays in a crouch-run while fleeing |\n| Drain Ooze | card, `E.Blob` | `{ R: 8, face: 'front', mouth: true, colors: { base: '#5a8a3a', dk: '#2e5a22', lt: '#9ad06a', spec: '#e8ffd0' } }` | a slow wobble; it slams by squashing down; it splashes green particles |\n| Rat Knot | pen | 6 rat-body balls in a ring with ear cones, tails as tubes knotted at the middle | the ring wobbles and turns; two bites a strike |\n| Snick, the Latch-Boss | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.0, build: 'bulky', hunch: .3, hat: { style: 'crown', color: '#c8a040' }, armor: true, outfit: 'coat', bladeLen: 9, eyeGlow: '#ffd24a', colors: { skin: '#7a8a5a', cloth: '#5a3a2a', plate: '#a08040' } }`, plus a pen cape of hanging keys | the `overhead` move; it rattles its keys at 50% when it calls for help |\n| Tinker Skeleton | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.2, build: 'skeleton', outfit: 'coat', bladeLen: 7, colors: { bone: '#d8cfb4', cloth: '#5a4030', metal: '#8a8a90' } }`, plus a pen hammer head on the blade | the `overhead` and `slash` moves; it collapses into bones on death |\n| Rustling | card, `E.Blob` | `{ R: 4.2, horns: true, feet: true, mouth: true, colors: { base: '#b8642a', dk: '#7a3a18', lt: '#e8a060' } }` | the horns twitch; it throws rust-flake particles when it hits |\n| Brass Spider | pen | abdomen ball r 7 and cephalothorax r 4.5 in `#b8923a`; 8 legs of 2 tubes each with procedural tetrapod gait (sine phase lifts); 4 red eye glows | legs step; it lunges on the strike; it falls apart into parts on death |\n| Clockwork Sentinel | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.6, build: 'bulky', armor: true, hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#8a6a3a' }, bladeLen: 14, eyeGlow: '#ff8a3a', colors: { cloth: '#6a4a2a', plate: '#b08a4a', metal: '#c8a868', skin: '#5a4a3a' } }`, plus a pen wind-up key turning on its back | the `twohand` move with a 0.8 s wind-up; the key spins faster during it |\n| Lidless Hound | pen | a quadruped (adapt the 3D horror kit's dogs, `src/starter3d/40-horror.js`): pale `#c8b8b0`, smooth eyeless head, long jaw | run, leap and bite |\n| Gloam Seer | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.25, build: 'heroic', outfit: 'robe', hood: true, weapon: 'staff', eyeGlow: '#8ae0ff', colors: { cloth: '#d8d0e8', hair: '#3a2a4a', skin: '#b8b0c8', trim: '#6a4a9a' } }`, plus an eye-sigil glow over the staff | the `cast` pose while charging a Locking Bolt (cyan glow growing) |\n| Gloam Warden | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.35, build: 'heroic', armor: true, hat: { style: 'helmet', color: '#2a2434' }, weapon: 'staff', eyeGlow: '#e8e8ff', colors: { cloth: '#2a2434', plate: '#4a4458', metal: '#9a98a8', skin: '#b8b0c8' } }`, plus a pen halberd blade on the staff | the `thrust` move (reaches two cells) |\n| Gazer | card orb plus pen | an orb `{ color: '#e8a040', r: 4 }`; pen iris (a flattened ball `#3a8a6a`), pupil, two lid slabs, 3 fin cones | blinks; the pupil tracks the party; it glows before Spark Gaze |\n| Lockjaw Golem | card, `E.Humanoid` | `{ size: 1.9, build: 'bulky', armor: true, weapon: null, eyeGlow: '#7ad8ff', colors: { cloth: '#3a3e46', plate: '#5a606a', skin: '#4a4e56' } }`, plus a pen **padlock head** (box body and shackle tube) instead of a hat | the `haymaker` move; heavy footstep shakes |\n| **The Bolt-Holder** | pen | an eye ball r 14 (sclera `#e8e0c8` with red vein bars); a flattened amber iris r 7 (`#e8a030`) facing the party; a black pupil r 3.5 that dilates; upper and lower lid slabs that blink and narrow for the Gaze; **7 radial iron bolts** (bars 22 long and 2.5 thick, with 5×5×3 heads) that slide out 2 per Great Lock (the 7th, the keystone, never moves); cyan lightning arcs (jagged pen bars) between neighbouring bolt heads; a ±3 bob; an amber `world.light`; red tint and crack lines in phase 3 | it must not read as a beholder: no eyestalks, no mouth, no tentacles. It reads as a caged iron lantern-eye. |\n| Ghosts (race) | card, `E.Humanoid` | a hooded cloak in the player's seat colour, `fade 0.45`, a lantern glow in the seat colour, and the hub name as a HUD tag | they glide between the cells they report |\n\n**People** (portraits and cutscenes):\n- the party (§7.9);\n- Keywarden Imelda Strake: heroic, band crown, coat, navy and silver;\n- Magister Abel Lintel: robe, pointed hat, long white hair;\n- Ysolde Vane: robe, long hair, ghost blue, drawn translucent;\n- Tamsin: size 0.9, ponytail, green hood;\n- Durgan: bulky, braided beard, iron helmet;\n- Veil: robe, hood, eyes shut (draw them closed).\n\n### 6.4 Animation list (new, on the engine's rigs, tweens and pen)\n\n- **Party view.** Step glide with bob; turn ease; bump lurch; pit fall (drop and fade); teleport (a cut or a swirl); spinner (a short wobble only when *visible*: none for silent spinners); hit shake; level-up glow on the card; KO slump (the portrait tinted grey); death toll (the card turns to bones); first-person weapon swings for each weapon family (blade, axe, blunt, spear thrust, crossbow, bow, sling, throw, punch); casting hand glows.\n- **World.** Gates rising with a chain rattle and wooden doors swinging; levers flipping and buttons and catches pressing in; plates sinking; secret walls sinking; the trapdoor dropping open; the pit bridge rising; teleporter swirl; gears turning; candles lighting and guttering; the Great Locks spinning; the Undergate's 7 bolts drawing back one by one; the clock's hands moving once it is whole; the boss's bolts sliding, lids blinking, iris dilating and lightning arcing.\n- **Monsters.** Idle, move glide, wind-up telegraph (the move's `wind` phase, at least 0.5 s), strike, hurt flash and recoil, death (the `die` pose, then sink; blobs squash flat; constructs fall apart into pen parts; the leech curls).\n- **UI.** Cooldown wipes; damage pops; card shake; chevrons; focus pulse; toast slides; the title logo shimmer.\n\n### 6.5 Audio: code only, no files\n\n- **The music director.** It plays a song per floor through `game.audio.music(song, { fade: 1 })`. Every dungeon song carries a `fight` track (drums and bass, `on: false`) that comes in with `audio.layer('fight', 1, .5)` when any monster engages and goes out 4 s after the last one. Stings use `audio.sting`. All songs are original, in the step-sequencer format (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ wave, vol, notes }] }`).\n\n  | Song | Feel | Seed motif (original) |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `title`, \"The City of a Thousand Locks\" | 76 bpm, D minor, bell pulses over a low triangle | `D4 - F4 A4 G4 - F4 E4 \\| D4 - - - C4 E4 G4 F4 \\| E4 - D4 C#4 D4 - - -` |\n  | `drains` | 60 bpm, a sparse drone, a lonely pulse every 8 bars, drips as sfx | low `A1` drone; a `pulse25` figure `A3 . C4 . E4 . . . \\| D4 . C4 . B3 . . .` |\n  | `tumbler` | 96 bpm in 6/8, ticking percussion | `drums` `h . h c . h` over `E2 . B2 E2 . B2` |\n  | `gloam` | 54 bpm, sine pads, a tritone colour | `C3` plus `F#3` pads, a choir-like sine line in long notes |\n  | `vault` / `boss` | 138 bpm, saw bass, lightning arpeggios | `A2 A2 . A2 C3 . A2 . \\| G2 G2 . G2 Bb2 . G2 .`, arps `A4 E5 A5 E5` |\n  | `camp` | 70 bpm, a lullaby | the title motif in a major key, triangle |\n  | `ending` | 84 bpm, D major | the title motif resolved |\n  | stings | — | `levelUp`, `deed`, `secret`, `wardFound`, `gameOver`, `victory`, `npcJoin` |\n\n- **Sound effects** (`audio.define`, the engine's voice format; the kit's `SOUNDS` are the template). Define every one of these:\n  - steps per floor: wet stone, metal grating, dust, stone;\n  - turn, bump, door open and close, gate grind, lever clank, button click, catch click, plate thunk, secret-wall grind, illusion shimmer, trapdoor, fall and land;\n  - teleporter whoosh (visible ones only), spinner grind, dart volley;\n  - key turn, lock fail, lockpick scratch and snap, alcove place and take;\n  - item pickup, drop and throw whoosh, land clatter, eat crunch, drink glug, scroll unroll, wand zap;\n  - swings (blade, axe, blunt), crossbow and bow twangs, sling whirr;\n  - hits (flesh, metal, bone, ooze), miss whiff, crit clang, party hurt;\n  - level up, death toll, raise;\n  - each spell: cast, travel loop and impact;\n  - monster voices for each type (squeak, slurp, chitter, rattle, clank, growl, chant, gaze hum);\n  - boss: roar, lightning crack, bolt slam, lock turn and shriek;\n  - UI: move, confirm, back, error, tab;\n  - ambience loops per floor: drips, distant gears, whispers, a low hum.\n\n  Pan by bearing with the patched `{ pan }` (§3.9).\n- **Voices: the babble synth** (`50-audio.js`, on `game.audio.ctx` and routed to the voice bus).\n  - **Per-speaker profile:** `{ base: Hz, range: semitones, rate: syllables/s, wave, formant: ×, breath: 0..1, vib: [Hz, depth], echo: 0..1 }`. The profiles:\n\n    | Speaker | base Hz | range | rate | wave | other |\n    |---|---|---|---|---|---|\n    | Narrator | 95 | 3 | 9 | triangle | echo 0.2 |\n    | Keywarden | 170 | 4 | 12 | square | |\n    | Lintel | 120 | 5 | 10 | saw | |\n    | Tamsin | 300 | 7 | 15 | pulse | |\n    | Durgan | 105 | 4 | 11 | square | |\n    | Ysolde | 220 | 5 | 9 | sine | echo 0.4 |\n    | Veil | 240 | 3 | 10 | triangle | breath 0.5 |\n    | Snick | 260 | 8 | 16 | square | |\n    | Bolt-Holder | 70 | 6 | 8 | saw | ring mod 30 Hz, echo 0.5 |\n\n    The party members also get profiles from their race and sex.\n  - **Driving it.** Set `talk.voice = null`. Each update, read `talk.shown`; for every two new letters (and on every vowel), emit a syllable.\n  - **The syllable:**\n    - The vowel picks a formant pair (F1/F2): a 800/1200, e 400/2000, i 300/2300, o 450/800, u 325/700, × `formant`.\n    - The letter's code picks the pitch inside `range`, so a word always sounds the same.\n    - Plosives (b d g k p t) add a 10 ms noise click; fricatives (f s v z h) add a 40 ms high-passed noise; nasals (m n) add a low hum.\n    - `?` rises at the end, `!` is louder and higher, `.` pauses 0.15 s and `,` pauses 0.08 s.\n  - **The voice:** an oscillator into two parallel band-pass filters (Q 8) into a gain envelope (5 ms attack, 0.6× hold, 30 ms release), then the bus.\n  - **Barks:** short babble for party pain (\"Oof!\"), effort (\"Hah!\"), level-up (\"Yes!\") and casting, and for monster taunts.\n  - **Test it in Node.** `babble.plan(text, profile)` returns syllable events (time, pitch, F1, F2, noise) and must be deterministic (§14). In Node there is no AudioContext, so the synth is a no-op.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Content\n\n### 7.1 World bible (all original)\n\n- **Hasp, the City of a Thousand Locks.** A walled river port that grew rich on locks. Its sigil is a key crossed with a bolt. It is ruled by the **Council of Keys** under **High Keywarden Imelda Strake**, stern and practical. The city's wizard is **Magister Abel Lintel**, dry, old and worried.\n- **The Tumblerwrights' Guild.** The finest locksmiths in the world. Their halls sat under the city until a disaster sealed them a century ago. Their last guildmistress, **Ysolde Vane**, haunts the halls.\n- **The Undergate.** The guild's masterwork: a gate beneath the city's foundations that seals something older, which this demo only hints at. To watch it, the guild forged **the Watcher**, an eye that never closes, and gave it **seven Great Bolts** to hold. The Watcher learned to love holding. It now calls itself **the Bolt-Holder** and turns the locks of Hasp from below: \"Every lock is a promise. I keep them all.\"\n- **Its servants.**\n  - **Latchkins**: small sewer goblins who worship keys and steal iron. Their chief is **Snick, the Latch-Boss**.\n  - The guild's own clockwork and its dead workers, wound up again.\n  - The **Gloam-folk** of the deep, pale people who believe the Eye is a god and call themselves the **Lidless**: their Seers, Wardens and hounds.\n- **The quest.** Gather the **Three Wards** (copper from the Drains, brass from the Guild Vault, iron from the Lidless Reliquary), open the Undergate and close the Eye.\n- **The humour.** The title is playful; the prose is earnest. Wit comes in small doses: Tamsin's quips, dry item descriptions, the Rat King's crown.\n\n### 7.2 Writing style guide\n\nThe model is the source's voice: terse, second-person, past the fourth wall never.\n\n- **Messages** are one short sentence, present tense, addressed to \"you\" or naming a member. Use plain words and full stops; keep exclamation marks for danger and wonder.\n  - \"You hear a click in the distance.\"\n  - \"There is a loose stone here.\"\n  - \"The bolt will not move.\"\n  - \"This key does not fit.\"\n  - \"The Iron Key turns. The door opens.\"\n  - \"A hidden door grinds open!\"\n  - \"The wall is an illusion!\"\n  - \"The floor gives way!\"\n  - \"You feel a twisting underfoot.\"\n  - \"Nothing happens.\"\n  - \"Darts fly from the walls!\"\n  - \"Cobb is hungry.\"\n  - \"Odelia is poisoned!\"\n  - \"Brannoc swings at the air.\"\n  - \"The Drain Rat dies.\"\n  - \"You may not rest with enemies near.\"\n  - \"You are awakened by something moving!\"\n  - \"A hidden deed is done!\"\n  - \"Ysa has reached level 3!\"\n- **Inscriptions** are in CAPITALS, formal and a little archaic: \"NOT EVERY STONE IS STONE.\"\n- **Narration** (intro and ending) is stately, in the third person, with short paragraphs and one image each.\n- **NPCs** speak formally but each in their own voice:\n  - Tamsin: quick and cheeky.\n  - Durgan: gruff and practical.\n  - Ysolde: sorrowful and old-fashioned.\n  - Veil: hushed and strange, always talking about eyes.\n  - The Bolt-Holder: grand, slow, with CAPITALS for its emphasis.\n- **Item descriptions** are 1 to 2 dry sentences: \"It has seen better days, and better locks.\"\n- **Never** use modern slang, game-mechanic jargon in the fiction (say \"slots\" only in menus), or the source's names.\n\n### 7.3 Story beats and cutscenes\n\nThe cutscenes are 3D stages, small `E.World3D` sets built at boot, with rigs, a camera moved by `game.script` and `game.tween`, dialogue boxes, babble voices and stings. Any button speeds the text; holding Options or Esc for 1 s skips the cutscene.\n\n**INTRO** (about 75 s):\n- *Stage A*, the Council Hall at night: pillars, banners, two braziers, a long table, Strake and Lintel.\n- *Stage B*, the Drain-gate: a stair down into the dark, a grate, and the party's four rigs.\n\n1. NARRATOR: \"Hasp. The city of a thousand locks.\"\n2. NARRATOR: \"For three hundred years its Tumblerwrights forged the finest locks in the world, and its people slept soundly behind them.\"\n3. NARRATOR: \"Then the locks began to turn by themselves.\"\n4. STRAKE: \"Doors bolt themselves in empty houses. Wells cap shut. Last night the east granary locked forty men inside and would not open for axes.\"\n5. LINTEL: \"It comes from below. Past the Drains, past the old guild halls… from the Undergate itself.\"\n6. STRAKE: \"You were recommended to me as people who open things. Go down. Find what holds our bolts, and break its grip.\"\n7. *(Stage B)* NARRATOR: \"The Drain-gate groans open. You step down into the dark.\"\n8. NARRATOR: \"Behind you, the grate crashes shut. A great bolt slides home with a sound like a closing tomb.\"\n   - The camera shakes, a pen-drawn bolt slides across the grate, and the torches gutter.\n9. NARRATOR: \"Somewhere far below, something blinks.\"\n   - Cut to black. An amber eye opens for 0.5 s, with the boss's hum.\n10. Play begins on floor 1 with the log line \"The grate above is bolted fast. There is no way back.\"\n\n**Mid-game beats** (in-dungeon, through E.Story):\n- Tamsin freed (floor 1).\n- Snick's barks.\n- Ysolde's ghost twice (floor 2).\n- Durgan (floor 2).\n- Veil's choice (floor 3).\n- The Undergate opening (floor 3, a 10 s in-engine moment: the camera holds, seven bolts draw back one by one, a deep chord).\n- The Bolt-Holder's taunts (floor 4).\n\n**ENDING** (about 60 s):\n- *Stage C*, the Vault after the battle: light pours from the cracked Eye.\n- *Stage D*, the city square at dawn: houses, doors swinging open, Strake, Lintel and the party.\n\n1. NARRATOR: \"The Eye cracks from lid to lid, and its light goes out.\"\n2. NARRATOR: \"One by one, the seven Great Bolts fall silent.\"\n3. *(Stage D)* NARRATOR: \"Above, in the city of a thousand locks, every door swings open at once.\"\n4. STRAKE: \"Hasp is in your debt. The guild's last forge will cast your names into the Undergate itself.\"\n5. LINTEL: \"Yet the Undergate was built to keep something in. With its Watcher gone…\"\n6. NARRATOR: \"Far beneath the city, in a darkness older than locks, something tries the door.\"\n7. Title card **THE END**, then the credits roll (§11), then the stats: time, kills, secrets found out of 3, hidden deeds out of 3, deaths and the party's levels. Back to the title.\n\n### 7.4 The floors\n\n- **Legend** (one character per cell):\n\n  | Char | Meaning |\n  |---|---|\n  | `#` | wall |\n  | `.` | floor |\n  | `@` | start or arrival |\n  | `<` `>` | stairs |\n  | `+` | wooden door |\n  | `=` | gate |\n  | `L` `K` | locked door |\n  | `M` | magic seal |\n  | `H` | secret wall |\n  | `I` | illusory wall |\n  | `P` | plate |\n  | `T` | dart trap |\n  | `O` | pit |\n  | `o` | trapdoor |\n  | `^` | teleporter |\n  | `S` | spinner |\n  | `U` | the Undergate |\n  | `*` | pit landing |\n  | `B` | the boss |\n\n- **Coordinates** are (x, y) with y = 0 at the top; the rulers above each map give x.\n- **Wall features** are given as *(stand x, y, face)*.\n- **Checked during planning:** every walkthrough below was checked with a reachability script (each target reachable with only the doors opened so far, and every gated area unreachable before its gate). It came to 157 checks, including both of Veil's branches and the Sump. Your content lint repeats them (§14).\n\n#### 7.4.1 Floor 1: The Hasp Drains (22×17)","numLines":420,"startLine":750,"totalLines":2145}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Xo4ufL9YKv3b2Busd4yzCh","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
