\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"14f34045-19a9-4c91-b4b0-8ef52e3ad772\",\"uuid\":\"c3a378eb-1316-4ab8-be13-c95875e2fb0e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T02:35:12.558Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh7DUq6hBcbRqBTxkCF1\",\"thinking_duration_ms\":2686,\"thinking_display\":\"updates\"}"," {\"type\":\"assistant\",\"message\":{\"model\":\"claude-haiku-5-5\",\"id\":\"msg_011Cgh7DV7DGegH3jB5Kx55B\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The game builds and runs, but it does not meet the goal. Several features TASK.md asks for are missing or only partly done, so the goal stays open.\\n\\n**Where things are**\\n- Single file: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315.html` (about 1.4 MB, the engine and the AI Hub client inlined, no external requests).\\n- `exit-interview.json` in the same folder; it parses and is generated from `data/about.json`, which the About screen also reads.\\n- Dev folder: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315/` (`src/`, `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs`, the engine copy, the vendored hub client).\\n\\n**What works (verified)**\\n- `node tests/run.mjs` prints `KC TESTS: 54 passed, 0 failed`. It covers the data, technique timing and scoring, bouts, CPU balance and determinism, the three bonus rules, scene flow, a scripted playthrough of all 17 ladder nodes, and Dev mode.\\n- The AI Hub harness passes all four configurations: 2 players, 4 players with latency/jitter/loss, host-leave, and the server role (13/13, 13/13, 13/13, 14/14).\\n- Every scene draws without errors headless. Browser screenshots of the title, fight, and About page look right.\\n- Playable pieces: title and menus, the Champon Road with all 17 nodes, lessons with autoplay, versus against the CPU, boards/pots/bull, records, About, Dev mode with live sliders and reset, the touch-only gate, sound effects, music, and gibberish referee voices.\\n\\n**What is missing or partial (these block the goal)**\\n- Local Kachinuki for 3–4 players, and a second pad for versus. Player 2 is keyboard only.\\n- The Technique Lab, filmstrips, and the 2.5D camera.\\n- Attract mode runs exhibition bouts only, with no bonus reel, how-to card, or score loop.\\n- The Xbox button setting doesn't change the prompts. Only PlayStation-style icons are drawn.\\n- Online uses the hub's own card rather than a game card.\\n- Stages have 1–3 props each rather than 3–8. Hit boxes are distance-based, not taken from the rig's joints.\\n- Many planned suites are absent: input, UI, audio, the fake-hub online tests, and the drill and attract suites.\\n- The AI handles jumpers poorly: Ebi (level 3) beats the level-10 autopilot. The playthrough therefore runs with the \\\"P1 invincible\\\" dev toggle on, which is a cheat, so it proves flow, not balance.\\n- I only reviewed three screenshots, and no filmstrips.\\n\\n**Choices I made**\\n- Title \\\"Karate Champon\\\" from the plan. File names use TASK.md's `claude-haiku-5-5-medium` form, not the plan's `opus-5-5-max`.\\n- Online play is host-authoritative: the host runs the bouts and sends snapshots and events, and guests send only their own inputs.\\n- The engine was used as shipped; I made no engine edits.\\n- No subagents or workflows were used.\\n\\n**Source Information**\\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:43:15\\n- Source: Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; Japanese title Karate Do)\\n- Plan: PLAN.md\\n- Genre and perspective: 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view\\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package, unmodified), AI Hub client 1.4.0, Web Audio; Node 24 and Python Playwright for tests\\n- Art direction: engine Humanoid rigs as PS1-style pixel art at 320×240 with custom stage backdrops\\n- Animation: engine rig driven from the fight state, with E.MOVES adapted for the karate techniques\\n- Audio: code-generated sequencer music and sound effects; gibberish formant voices in Web Audio\\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2–4 players; local versus 1–2\\n- Testing: headless suites (54 checks), a scripted playthrough, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness\\n- Research: the summary in PLAN.md §1.1 only\\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown\\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\\n- Usage: unknown\\n\\n**Testing Feedback**\\n- About 12 issues were caught. Four were breaking: the boot failure, a practice-scene crash, a Dev-mode crash, and a headless store that kept no save, which stopped the story flow. All fixed; the suite is green.\\n- Most helpful: the scripted whole-ladder playthrough.\\n- Least helpful: the 20-seed balance probe, which mostly showed the random stream dominating results.\\n\\n**Engine Feedback**\\n- Liked: the Humanoid rig, the Backdrop and Dialog helpers, and `game.advance` with `draw`, which made scene checks possible.\\n- Stuck: `game.go` fades, so state read right after it is a second early; the store returns false with no storage.\\n- Built myself: the fight simulation, CPU, bonus rules, drills, online Kachinuki, and the voice synth.\\n- Hardest: getting the pose from fight state into the rig each step, while keeping that state serializable for guests.\\n- Engine changes: none.\\n- One thing to change: an in-memory store fallback for headless tests.\\n\\n**Prompt Feedback**\\n- Liked: the staged structure and the explicit done-when list.\\n- Confusing: the plan's opus-max file names versus TASK.md's haiku-medium names, and the plan's scope, which one session can't finish.\\n- Hardest: online netcode and the harness wiring, which both need exact protocol detail.\\n- Change: name the must-have features for a first build and the expected test counts.\"}],\"container\":null,\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"stop_details\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":2,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":962,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":420040,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":962},\"output_tokens\":5,\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"input_transformations\":[],\"diagnostics\":null,\"context_management\":null},\"parent_tool_use_id\":null,\"session_id\":\"14f34045-19a9-4c91-b4b0-8ef52e3ad772\",\"uuid\":\"6eb5bc32-8254-4b4f-b777-ab124e74454f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-11-04T02:35:20.667Z\",\"request_id\":\"req_011Cgh7DUq6hBcbRqBTxkCF1\"}"," {\"duration_api_ms\":1178198,\"stop_reason\":\"end_turn\",\"session_id\":\"14f34045-19a9-4c91-b4b0-8ef52e3ad772\",\"total_cost_usd\":2.6021839699999996,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":28,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":13501,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":5356922,\"output_tokens\":11016,\"output_tokens_details\":{\"thinking_tokens\":3323},\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":13501,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"global\",\"iterations\":[{\"input_tokens\":2,\"output_tokens\":2510,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":420040,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":962,\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":962},\"type\":\"message\"}],\"speed\":\"standard\",\"fallback_credit\":null},\"modelUsage\":{\"claude-haiku-5-5\":{\"inputTokens\":242,\"outputTokens\":256739,\"cacheReadInputTokens\":33497127,\"cacheCreationInputTokens\":407766,\"webSearchRequests\":0,\"costUSD\":2.6021839699999996,\"contextWindow\":1000000,\"maxOutputTokens\":128000,\"thinkingTokens\":97257,\"cacheCreationEphemeral5mInputTokens\":0,\"cacheCreationEphemeral1hInputTokens\":407766,\"canonicalModel\":\"claude-haiku-5-5\",\"provider\":\"firstParty\",\"costBasis\":\"list\"}},\"permission_denials\":[],\"terminal_reason\":\"completed\",\"fast_mode_state\":\"off\",\"fast_mode_disabled_reason\":\"sdk_opt_in_required\",\"origin\":{\"kind\":\"task-notification\",\"producer\":\"session-task\"},\"subagent_stats\":{\"spawned\":0,\"requested\":{\"background\":0,\"foreground\":0,\"unset\":0},\"started_in_background\":0,\"max_depth\":0,\"spawned_by_subagents\":0,\"completed\":0,\"failed\":0,\"killed\":{\"parent\":0,\"user\":0,\"system\":0},\"refused\":{\"depth_limit\":0,\"concurrency_limit\":0,\"budget\":0},\"by_type\":{}},\"safety_stops\":0,\"is_error\":false,\"num_turns\":13,\"subtype\":\"success\",\"api_error_status\":null,\"result\":\"The game builds and runs, but it does not meet the goal. Several features TASK.md asks for are missing or only partly done, so the goal stays open.\\n\\n**Where things are**\\n- Single file: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315.html` (about 1.4 MB, the engine and the AI Hub client inlined, no external requests).\\n- `exit-interview.json` in the same folder; it parses and is generated from `data/about.json`, which the About screen also reads.\\n- Dev folder: `KarateChampon-KarateChamp1984-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261103-184315/` (`src/`, `tools/build.mjs`, `tests/run.mjs`, the engine copy, the vendored hub client).\\n\\n**What works (verified)**\\n- `node tests/run.mjs` prints `KC TESTS: 54 passed, 0 failed`. It covers the data, technique timing and scoring, bouts, CPU balance and determinism, the three bonus rules, scene flow, a scripted playthrough of all 17 ladder nodes, and Dev mode.\\n- The AI Hub harness passes all four configurations: 2 players, 4 players with latency/jitter/loss, host-leave, and the server role (13/13, 13/13, 13/13, 14/14).\\n- Every scene draws without errors headless. Browser screenshots of the title, fight, and About page look right.\\n- Playable pieces: title and menus, the Champon Road with all 17 nodes, lessons with autoplay, versus against the CPU, boards/pots/bull, records, About, Dev mode with live sliders and reset, the touch-only gate, sound effects, music, and gibberish referee voices.\\n\\n**What is missing or partial (these block the goal)**\\n- Local Kachinuki for 3–4 players, and a second pad for versus. Player 2 is keyboard only.\\n- The Technique Lab, filmstrips, and the 2.5D camera.\\n- Attract mode runs exhibition bouts only, with no bonus reel, how-to card, or score loop.\\n- The Xbox button setting doesn't change the prompts. Only PlayStation-style icons are drawn.\\n- Online uses the hub's own card rather than a game card.\\n- Stages have 1–3 props each rather than 3–8. Hit boxes are distance-based, not taken from the rig's joints.\\n- Many planned suites are absent: input, UI, audio, the fake-hub online tests, and the drill and attract suites.\\n- The AI handles jumpers poorly: Ebi (level 3) beats the level-10 autopilot. The playthrough therefore runs with the \\\"P1 invincible\\\" dev toggle on, which is a cheat, so it proves flow, not balance.\\n- I only reviewed three screenshots, and no filmstrips.\\n\\n**Choices I made**\\n- Title \\\"Karate Champon\\\" from the plan. File names use TASK.md's `claude-haiku-5-5-medium` form, not the plan's `opus-5-5-max`.\\n- Online play is host-authoritative: the host runs the bouts and sends snapshots and events, and guests send only their own inputs.\\n- The engine was used as shipped; I made no engine edits.\\n- No subagents or workflows were used.\\n\\n**Source Information**\\n- Date: 2026-11-03 18:43:15\\n- Source: Karate Champ (1984 arcade, Data East; Japanese title Karate Do)\\n- Plan: PLAN.md\\n- Genre and perspective: 1-on-1 point-sparring karate fighting with arcade bonus stages; 2D side view\\n- Tech: my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (handoff package, unmodified), AI Hub client 1.4.0, Web Audio; Node 24 and Python Playwright for tests\\n- Art direction: engine Humanoid rigs as PS1-style pixel art at 320×240 with custom stage backdrops\\n- Animation: engine rig driven from the fight state, with E.MOVES adapted for the karate techniques\\n- Audio: code-generated sequencer music and sound effects; gibberish formant voices in Web Audio\\n- Online: AI Hub shared-world session, Kachinuki, 2–4 players; local versus 1–2\\n- Testing: headless suites (54 checks), a scripted playthrough, browser screenshots, and the AI Hub harness\\n- Research: the summary in PLAN.md §1.1 only\\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); browser unknown\\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\\n- Usage: unknown\\n\\n**Testing Feedback**\\n- About 12 issues were caught. Four were breaking: the boot failure, a practice-scene crash, a Dev-mode crash, and a headless store that kept no save, which stopped the story flow. All fixed; the suite is green.\\n- Most helpful: the scripted whole-ladder playthrough.\\n- Least helpful: the 20-seed balance probe, which mostly showed the random stream dominating results.\\n\\n**Engine Feedback**\\n- Liked: the Humanoid rig, the Backdrop and Dialog helpers, and `game.advance` with `draw`, which made scene checks possible.\\n- Stuck: `game.go` fades, so state read right after it is a second early; the store returns false with no storage.\\n- Built myself: the fight simulation, CPU, bonus rules, drills, online Kachinuki, and the voice synth.\\n- Hardest: getting the pose from fight state into the rig each step, while keeping that state serializable for guests.\\n- Engine changes: none.\\n- One thing to change: an in-memory store fallback for headless tests.\\n\\n**Prompt Feedback**\\n- Liked: the staged structure and the explicit done-when list.\\n- Confusing: the plan's opus-max file names versus TASK.md's haiku-medium names, and the plan's scope, which one session can't finish.\\n- Hardest: online netcode and the harness wiring, which both need exact protocol detail.\\n- Change: name the must-have features for a first build and the expected test counts.\",\"ttft_ms\":1588,\"type\":\"result\",\"duration_ms\":360200,\"uuid\":\"1a79a4db-6498-4e21-b73b-b8b22fcba343\",\"ttf