{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghBXy4wjRSWxR2QHrC7H","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**, a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler. The single HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in this folder's root. The one test command (`node tests/run.mjs`) passes: 2,738 checks across 13 suites, including the browser and AI Hub harness runs. The bundled file boots from `file://` with no errors.\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Campaign:** four floors (the Drains, the Tumbler Halls, the Gloam Deeps, the Eye's Vault). There are 16 monster types, 18 spells, three hidden deeds, three NPCs who can join, Veil's two-way choice, an intro and an ending.\n- **The boss:** the Bolt-Holder fight has three Great Locks to turn, telegraphed attacks and three phases.\n- **Puzzles:** levers, buttons, plates, keys, magic seals, secret and illusory walls, pits, teleporters and a spinner, all wired as data.\n- **Interface:** one focus system works the same with a controller, keyboard or mouse. Prompts follow the device you used last, with PlayStation shapes by default and Xbox letters in Settings. Touch-only devices get the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice.\n- **Dev Mode** (from the pause menu): 22 live tuning sliders with resets, cheats, warps, party tools, spawning, debug overlays and a sound test.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 seconds idle on the title and loops nine bot-played scenes; any input returns to the title.\n- **Multiplayer:** the Rat Run is a 1–4 player ghost race in AI Hub sessions, with the game's own card. On its own it's a solo time trial against your best-run ghost. All three harness scenarios pass.\n\n**Limitations**\n- **Balance:** the full-game test uses a fixed seed. While tuning, the walkthrough bot won the whole campaign on 5 of 8 random seeds and lost the rest at the boss (one also died on floor 2).\n- **Speed under software rendering:** in headless Chromium the game ran at roughly a fifth of real speed. I couldn't test on a real GPU here.\n- **Plan items not done:** party-screen items move by click-to-take and click-to-place, not drag and drop. I didn't do the planned motion filmstrips or screenshots at three resolutions; I reviewed screenshots at 1280×720 only. The cutscene sets are simple pen-drawn stages.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. You see through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with eased steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment map and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (the 2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3 from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0. Plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro look: low-resolution code-drawn 3D with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Monsters and people are pixel-art cards; some creatures, the wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are drawn in code. The menus use a brass \"lockwork\" style with 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's built-in character rigs and attack moves (wind-ups, strikes, death poses), plus code-drawn creatures and mechanisms. The first-person camera is eased (steps, turns, bumps, head bob), with first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs with a combat layer that fades in during fights, seven short fanfares, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and a custom Web Audio voice synth for Animalese/Simlish-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); solo time trial against the local best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:** headless Node suites through `game.advance` and `game.debug`, including a bot playthrough of the whole game on both of Veil's branches. Playwright runs the bundled file and the AI Hub harness. Screenshots were used only for looks.\n- **Research:** the provided plan's research section, the engine's manuals and the crawler kit, and the AI Hub protocol README. No other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 30. Lint found 5 decorations not placed on walls. The rules suites found Slumber being undone by its own noise, voice consonants that never clicked, and a secret counted twice. The walkthrough bot found a wrong lever cell in its own script, a button puzzle that needed retries, a too-strict lever rule, the ending stair failing once the dead boss was removed, and four balance spikes. The browser and hub runs found a crash after leaving the attract mode, timers running on game time instead of the clock, WebGPU errors in the hub frames, and progress reports paced on game time. Screenshots found about eight layout problems.\n2. **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** About 8: the ending stair not starting the ending (the game couldn't be finished), the attract-exit crash, Slumber never working, four balance spikes that wiped every bot run (Snick, the Gauntlet Seers, the Barracks Wardens, the boss), and zero progress reports in hub sessions.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough. It plays all four floors and both branches by the real rules and found the unfinishable ending, the puzzle and wiring mistakes and every balance wall within minutes.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the per-screen text-fit layout check. It passed from the start and found nothing the screenshots didn't.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit and the code-drawing tools. A fixed first-person camera, levels written as text, objects drawn by code and live character portraits made a convincing dungeon quickly, and headless `game.advance` made the simulation testable in Node.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes. Wall colours come from the level legend, not the level's colour option. The pause key is handled before the scene sees it, which I worked around by making the scene's pausable flag a computed property. Secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a deterministic simulation with no view code (grid party, wiring language, real-time combat, monster AI, the boss), a walkthrough bot that plays it, and one focus-navigation UI for controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D world, the 2D overlay, the input map and the pause menu in step across many menu screens, and knowing which engine behaviours (pause, menu keys, mouse detection) run before the scene does.\n5. **What I changed, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume bus and `audio.bus()` for the babble synth;\n   - sound panning (`{ pan }`) so sounds come from their direction;\n   - the Button symbols setting limited to PlayStation (default) and Xbox;\n   - a beard option on the humanoid rig, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One change I'd make:** a built-in grid-crawler layer (cells, wall features, doors and secret walls sharing the level's wall material), so a dungeon crawler doesn't have to rebuild those on the free 3D world.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** it's concrete about the deliverables (file names, the About screen, the exit interview) and leaves the design open; the plan gave a complete target I could check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session can finish in full detail, and it wasn't clear how much of its quantity (every screenshot review, every suite) was required versus every system.\n3. **Hardest thing:** making a real-time party crawler beatable by an automated bot while keeping it fair for people, and fitting a 10-foot, controller-first UI around a dense genre.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity, so a build can scale its ambition to what can actually be finished and tested."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":422,"cache_read_input_tokens":734583,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":422},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"78664883-3550-4514-8444-7fbf938a8d7d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T03:32:17.572Z","request_id":"req_011CghBXxkqNFtSDamNpJvrZ"}
