{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghEK6Ufj1xYwg7HWfBZT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the two open items: balance now holds across every seed tried, and the cutscene sets are rebuilt. The full test command passes (2,749 checks across 13 suites), `exit-interview.json` parses, and `src/` has no TODOs or placeholders.\n\n**Balance.** The bot now beats the whole campaign on all 32 seeds I tried, across both of Veil's branches; before this round it won 6 of 10. Most of the old failures traced to real bugs, not difficulty:\n- **Boss bugs (game-breaking):**\n  - After relocking a bolt, the Eye could drift onto the cell you must stand in to re-turn that lock, which trapped the fight at half damage. It now stays off the three lock cells.\n  - The boss's map cell stayed blocked after its death, which could cut off the stair to the ending. Fixed.\n- **Bot play mistakes** (so it now plays the way a sensible player would):\n  - It never memorized spells into the slots it gained by levelling, so healing ran dry at the boss. It now fills every slot at camp.\n  - It handed back-row members a sling or bow with no ammunition. Party Strike also now skips empty missile weapons.\n  - It left fallen fighters in the front row, so the healthy members behind couldn't fight. It now swaps them out.\n  - It carried some internal state from one run into the next. That's now reset.\n- **Small tuning:** the floor 2 Workshop opens with two Rustlings instead of three, their weapon rust is rarer, and the boss's last phase summons one Gazer at a time instead of two.\n\nThe playthrough test now also runs eight extra seeds on both branches and requires every one to reach the ending.\n\n**Cutscenes.** I rebuilt all four sets:\n- **Council Hall:** a long table with chairs and candles, a burning hearth, banners and the city seal. Strake and Lintel are framed properly and gesture as they speak.\n- **Drain-gate:** worn steps, the grate with its bolt sliding home, chains and a drain. The party is seen from behind.\n- **Vault:** the cracked Eye dims and sinks while its seven bolts scatter across the floor.\n- **City square:** an open courtyard under a dawn sky with the sun, six house fronts whose doors swing open one by one, a fountain, lamps and bunting. The party cheers and townsfolk wave.\n\nThe camera eases slowly through each set.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. The view is through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with eased steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment automap and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Monsters and people are flat pixel-art figures; some creatures, the wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are built from simple shapes in code. Four built cutscene sets. The menus use a brass \"lockwork\" style with hand-made 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs, attack moves and poses; an eased first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms (levers, gates, secret walls, Great Locks, the boss's bolts, doors swinging in the city square); first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs (floor themes add a combat layer during fights), seven short fanfares, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and a custom Web Audio voice synth for Animalese/Simlish-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); on its own, a solo time trial against the local best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:** headless Node test suites, including the bot playthrough of both of Veil's branches and an eight-seed balance sweep; Playwright checks of the bundled file and the AI Hub harness; screenshots at three resolutions and slow-motion filmstrips, used for looks only.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and crawler kit, and the AI Hub README; no other research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35:\n   - Content checks found 5 decorations not placed on walls.\n   - The rules suites found Slumber being undone by its own noise, a voice rule that never fired, and a secret counted twice.\n   - The bot and the balance sweep found an unfinishable ending, two boss bugs (the blocked lock cell and the blocked boss cell), puzzle-rule mistakes and five balance spikes.\n   - The browser and hub runs found an attract-exit crash, timers running on game time instead of the clock, WebGPU errors in the hub frames, and hub progress reports that never fired.\n   - Screenshots and filmstrips found about 10 look problems.\n2. **How many were critical / game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - the unfinishable ending;\n   - the two boss bugs;\n   - the attract-exit crash;\n   - Slumber never working;\n   - the balance spikes that wiped every run;\n   - no progress reports in hub sessions.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. Once the bot played sensibly, every failure it found was a real game bug or balance wall.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit and drawing shapes in code, plus headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Where I got stuck or guessed wrong:** wall colours come from the level legend; the pause key is handled before the scene sees it; secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a deterministic game simulation with no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many menu screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel with `audio.bus()`, for the babble synth;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - the button-symbols setting limited to PlayStation (default) and Xbox;\n   - beards on character rigs, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer: cells, wall features, doors and secret walls that share the level's wall material.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the deliverables are concrete, and the plan gave a complete target to check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it was unclear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** a real-time party crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a 10-foot, controller-first interface.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1219,"cache_read_input_tokens":824665,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1219},"output_tokens":718,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"36fe2a68-29a5-4667-b224-a455558faef1","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:08:36.515Z","request_id":"req_011CghEK657gL2439xiTe2hZ"}
