{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghEzHgwRbneAmVYNoKH6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've closed the evidence gaps the stop hook raised. The full test command passes (2,756 checks across 13 suites), the rebuilt HTML and `exit-interview.json` are in the root, the JSON parses, and `src/` has no TODOs or placeholders.\n\n- **Balance on untuned seeds:** the bot finished the whole campaign on all 32 seeds I had never used while tuning (1000–1031, alternating Veil's two choices). The playthrough suite now keeps eight more untuned seeds (2001–2008) as a standing check, and all of those pass too.\n- **Xbox glyphs in a real browser:** the browser suite now checks in headless Chromium that prompts show PlayStation shapes by default (✕, □) and that the Xbox setting turns them into A and X. A screenshot of the Xbox prompts is in `shots/xbox-prompts.png`.\n- **Touch-only devices:** this was already checked in Chromium emulating a phone (390×844, touch, mobile): the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice shows and touch controls are off.\n- **Drag and drop:** a real-mouse drag in Chromium moves the sword from the hand slot to the pack. The Node tests now also cover:\n  - armour onto the body;\n  - ammunition into the quiver;\n  - a potion between pack slots;\n  - a refused drop onto a slot that can't take the item, with the reason shown.\n\n  This found a real bug: dropping onto an occupied slot left the swapped-out item stuck in the Hand. It now goes back to the slot the drag started from.\n- **Filmstrips at 1920×1080:** seven slow-motion strips (lever, gate, wind-up, strike, illusory-wall walk-through, boss bolts, lightning), in `shots/film-1920-*.png`. All read as intended.\n- **Cutscenes:** these are the rebuilt sets from the last round, not the earlier \"simple\" ones: the Council Hall, the Drain-gate, the Vault with the Eye dimming as it sinks, and the dawn city square with a crowd. I also reworded the About text that said creatures were built \"from simple shapes\".\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. The view is through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with smooth steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment map and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. There are four built cutscene sets and a brass \"lockwork\" menu style with hand-made 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs, attack moves and poses; a smooth first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms (levers, gates, secret walls, Great Locks, the boss's bolts, doors swinging open); first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs (with a fight layer that comes in during combat), seven short jingles, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and a custom Web Audio synth for Animalese/Simlish-style voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); on its own, a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:** headless Node test suites, including the bot playthrough of both of Veil's branches and an untuned eight-seed balance sweep; Playwright checks in headless Chromium of the bundled file (glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop, the attract mode, an emulated phone) and the AI Hub harness; screenshots at three resolutions and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used only for looks.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and crawler kit, and the AI Hub README; no other research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 36, including this round's drag-and-drop swap bug.\n2. **How many were critical / game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - the ending stair not starting the ending;\n   - two boss bugs (a blocked lock cell, the boss's cell staying blocked after death);\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - Slumber never working;\n   - the balance spikes that wiped runs;\n   - hub sessions sending no progress reports.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed balance sweep. Once the bot's own play was fixed, its failures pointed at real game bugs and balance problems. It won all 32 untuned seeds.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit and drawing scenery in code, plus headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Wall colours come from the level legend; the pause key is handled before the scene sees it; secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a game simulation that runs the same way every time and has no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many menu screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel, for the babble voices;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - the button-symbols setting limited to PlayStation (default) and Xbox;\n   - beards on character rigs, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer: cells, wall features, doors and secret walls that share the level's wall material.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the deliverables are concrete, and the plan gave a complete target to check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it was unclear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** making a real-time party crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a TV-distance, controller-first interface.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1226,"cache_read_input_tokens":836742,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1226},"output_tokens":758,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"ae472d1f-7143-4fa2-b099-64545f95d61d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:17:25.972Z","request_id":"req_011CghEzHKrs2qRirfxEKyoC"}
