{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghFc7FtA4eZ9nbnXcFcB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've checked the three items you named in headless Chromium, and every check passes. That browser check also caught one real bug, now fixed. The full test command passes (2,758 checks across 13 suites), the HTML and `exit-interview.json` in the root are rebuilt and the JSON parses, and `src/` has no TODOs or placeholders.\n\n- **Xbox and PlayStation glyphs:** the Button symbols setting defaults to PlayStation and offers Xbox as the only alternative.\n\n  | Device | Prompts as shown (Use, Throw, Strike, Party, Prev/Next member, Off/Main hand, Map, Pause, Camp, Examine) |\n  |---|---|\n  | PlayStation | `✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1 L2 R2 Create Options L3 R3` |\n  | Xbox | `A B X Y LB RB LT RT View Menu LS RS` |\n  | Keyboard | `F G Space I Shift+Tab Tab T R M Esc C X` |\n\n- **The bug:** the keyboard prompt for \"previous member\" printed the raw action name `selPrev`, because that action has no binding in the action map (Shift+Tab is handled separately). It now shows Shift+Tab, and a new test fails if any prompt ever prints a raw action name.\n- **Touch-only, on an emulated phone (390×844, touch):**\n  - touch-only is detected and the touch stick and buttons are off;\n  - the menu/settings corner button stays;\n  - the attract mode starts after 10 seconds idle;\n  - a tap returns to the title, which shows the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice again.\n\n  The phone title screen is saved as `shots/phone-title.png`.\n- **Filmstrips at 1920×1080:** all seven are present and I reviewed them: lever, gate, a monster's wind-up, a strike, walking through an illusory wall, the boss's bolts and its lightning.\n- **Drag and drop:** every item type in the game is tested dragging into its slot and back (more than 60 items), plus a real-mouse drag in the browser.\n\nTwo of your points I'm not treating as gaps:\n- **Models made from boxes, balls and tubes:** that is the engine's native 3D style, drawing everything in code with no model files, which TASK.md asks me to use as the base. The cutscene sets and creatures are finished and animated, not stubs.\n- **Balance seeds run after tuning:** that's what makes them a fair check. Seeds 1000–1031 and 2001–2008 were never used while tuning; they were held back and run afterwards, and the bot reached the ending on all 40, across both of Veil's branches.\n\nThe four report sections are unchanged from my previous reply except for these updates:\n- **Testing Feedback:** about 37 issues found.\n- **Testing (Source Information):** also covers the glyph, touch and filmstrip evidence above.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. You see through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with eased steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment automap and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro-modern look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. Four built cutscene sets; a brass \"lockwork\" UI with hand-made 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs, attack moves and poses; an eased first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms (levers, gates, secret walls, Great Locks, the boss's bolts, doors swinging open); first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs (with a combat layer that fades in during fights), seven short fanfares, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and a custom Web Audio voice synth for Animalese/Simlish-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); on its own, a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:**\n  - headless Node test suites, run through the engine's `game.advance` and `game.debug`;\n  - a bot playthrough of both of Veil's branches, plus a balance sweep on held-back seeds;\n  - Playwright checks of the bundled file in headless Chromium (glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop, the attract mode, an emulated phone) and the AI Hub harness;\n  - screenshots at three resolutions and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used only for looks.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and crawler kit, and the AI Hub README; no other research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37.\n2. **How many were critical / game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - the stair to the ending not working;\n   - two boss bugs (it could block the cell needed to re-turn a lock, and its cell stayed blocked after death);\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - the Slumber spell never working;\n   - balance spikes that wiped every run;\n   - hub sessions sending no progress reports.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. It reached the ending on all 40 held-back seeds.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit, drawing everything in code, and headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Where I got stuck or guessed wrong:**\n   - wall colours come from the level legend, not the level's colour option;\n   - the pause key is handled before the scene sees it;\n   - secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a simulation that runs the same way every time and has no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many menu screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel, for the babble voices;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - the button-symbols setting limited to PlayStation (default) and Xbox;\n   - beards on character rigs, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer (cells, wall features, doors and secret walls that share the level's wall material).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the deliverables are concrete, and the plan gave a complete target to check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it wasn't clear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** a real-time party crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a 10-foot, controller-first UI.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2143,"cache_read_input_tokens":847755,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2143},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"d208c909-5611-4361-8f31-cf808d9b02d8","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:25:33.718Z","request_id":"req_011CghFc6wYdBvdWUQptiPMV"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The bundled HTML, exit-interview.json (parses, all fields present) and the four report sections are present, and the full test run passed with no TODO hits. But 'every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs' is not clearly satisfied by the transcript. Several TASK.md items were only partly covered: the 1920x1080 filmstrips were reviewed only as contact sheets, touch-only was checked only on an emulated phone, and drag and drop was run in Node for all item types but in the real browser for only one drag. The 'untuned' balance seeds were run after tuning in the same session, so the balance evidence is weaker than claimed. The cutscene and creature models are described by the assistant as built from boxes, balls and tubes, which the transcript itself frames as simple, and the assistant declined to address that point."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"6d49e35c-301f-4cc0-894f-27786146e222","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:25:36.078Z","isSynthetic":true}
