{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghG89nXiKTn478iGoMBq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Real-browser drag and drop now covers every kind of item: one of each, dragged with the actual mouse into its slot and back to the pack. The browser suite passes with this check (14 checks), and the full suite was green just before (2,758 checks across 13 suites). The HTML and `exit-interview.json` are in the root, the JSON parses, and `src/` has no TODOs or placeholders.\n\nOn the other points, here is what was actually checked and why I'm not changing the game for them:\n- **Filmstrips:** I captured seven 1920×1080 slow-motion filmstrips and reviewed them frame by frame as contact sheets, the plan's own method. They led to two fixes: levers now flip smoothly, and the first-person sword swing is raised and lit.\n- **Touch-only:** the only touch hardware available here is Chromium's mobile emulation, which simulates a real touchscreen. On it, touch-only is detected, the touch stick and buttons are off, the menu button stays, the attract mode runs after 10 seconds idle, and a tap returns to the title with the \"Controller or keyboard required\" notice.\n- **Balance:** the 40 seeds (1000–1031 and 2001–2008) were never used while tuning. Running them after tuning is what makes them a fair test. The bot reached the ending on all 40, across both of Veil's branches.\n- **Models built from boxes, balls and tubes:** that is the engine's native 3D style (no model files), which TASK.md asks me to use as the base. The cutscene sets and creatures are complete and animated, not stubs.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. You see through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with smooth steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment automap and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro-modern look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. Four built cutscene sets; a brass \"lockwork\" UI with hand-made 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs, attack moves and poses; a smoothly eased first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms (levers, gates, secret walls, Great Locks, the boss's bolts, doors swinging open); first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs (a fight layer fades in during combat), seven short fanfares, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and a custom Web Audio voice synth for Animalese/Simlish-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); on its own, a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:**\n  - headless Node test suites, run through the engine's `game.advance` and `game.debug`;\n  - a bot playthrough of both of Veil's branches and a balance sweep on held-back seeds;\n  - Playwright checks of the bundled file in headless Chromium: glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop of every item kind, the attract mode, an emulated phone;\n  - the AI Hub harness;\n  - screenshots at three resolutions and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used only for looks.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and crawler kit, and the AI Hub README; no other research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37.\n2. **How many were critical / game-breaking?** About 10:\n   - the stair to the ending not working;\n   - two boss bugs (it could block the cell needed to re-turn a lock, and its cell stayed blocked after death);\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - the Slumber spell never working;\n   - balance spikes that wiped every run;\n   - hub sessions sending no progress reports.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. It reached the ending on all 40 held-back seeds.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit, drawing everything in code, and headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Where I got stuck or guessed wrong:**\n   - wall colours come from the level legend, not the level's colour option;\n   - the pause key is handled before the scene sees it;\n   - secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a simulation that runs the same way every time and has no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many menu screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel, for the babble voices;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - the button-symbols setting limited to PlayStation (default) and Xbox;\n   - beards on character rigs, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer (cells, wall features, doors and secret walls that share the level's wall material).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the deliverables are concrete, and the plan gave a complete target to check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it wasn't clear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** a real-time party crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a 10-foot, controller-first UI.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1770,"cache_read_input_tokens":853147,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1770},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"7235b809-3d9f-4845-ac16-534b52229c82","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:32:18.590Z","request_id":"req_011CghG87ver4PZRaDSsUMYL"}
