{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghHz9CJyfmwNLHqfQEMX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished: **Eye of the Bolt-Holder**, a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler inspired by Eye of the Beholder, playable from one HTML file. It covers four floors, three NPCs who can join, the Bolt-Holder boss fight, an intro and ending cutscene, a Rat Run race for 1–4 players in an AI Hub session, Dev Mode, an attract mode and a touch-only notice.\n\nThe single HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in the folder root, and the JSON parses with every field answered. The last full test run passed all 13 suites (2,758 checks), and `src/` has no TODOs or placeholders.\n\nThis round's final check also passed: in headless Chromium, every one of the game's 79 items was dragged with a real mouse into its slot and back. During that long run I froze monsters and turned on god mode so nothing could interrupt it.\n\nOn the points from the last few rounds:\n- **Balance:** the bot beat the whole campaign on 40 seeds that were never used while tuning (1000–1031 and 2001–2008), across both of Veil's choices. Before the fixes it won 6 of 10.\n- **Touch-only:** checked in Chromium's mobile emulation, the only touch device available here. The notice shows, there are no touch controls, the settings button stays, and the attract mode runs.\n- **Filmstrips:** seven slow-motion strips at 1920×1080, reviewed frame by frame as contact sheets. They led to two fixes (smoother lever flips and a raised, lit sword swing).\n- **Models:** the cutscene sets and creatures use the engine's native style (everything drawn in code, no model files), which TASK.md asks for as the base. They are complete and animated.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. You see through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with smooth steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment map and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro-modern look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. Four cutscene sets; a brass \"lockwork\" UI with hand-made 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs, attack moves and poses; a smooth first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms (levers, gates, secret walls, Great Locks, the boss's bolts, doors swinging open); first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs (each with a combat layer that fades in during fights), seven short fanfares, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and a custom Web Audio voice synth for Animalese/Simlish-style babble.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); on its own, a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:**\n  - headless Node tests through the engine's `game.advance` and `game.debug`;\n  - a bot that plays the whole game, on both of Veil's branches;\n  - a balance sweep on seeds held back from tuning;\n  - Playwright runs in headless Chromium: controller glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop of all 79 items, the attract mode, an emulated phone;\n  - the AI Hub harness;\n  - screenshots at three resolutions and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used only to judge looks.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and crawler kit, and the AI Hub README; no other research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** About 10:\n   - the stair to the ending not working;\n   - two boss bugs (it could block the cell needed to re-turn a lock, and its cell stayed blocked after death);\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - the Slumber spell never working;\n   - balance spikes that wiped every run;\n   - hub sessions sending no progress reports.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. It reached the ending on all 40 held-back seeds.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit, drawing everything in code, and headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Wall colours come from the level legend, not the level's colour option; the pause key is handled before the scene sees it; secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a simulation that runs the same way every time and has no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many menu screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel for the babble voices;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - the button-symbols setting limited to PlayStation (default) and Xbox;\n   - beards on character rigs, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer (cells, wall features, doors and secret walls that share the level's wall material).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the deliverables are concrete, and the plan gave a complete target to check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it wasn't clear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** a real-time party crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a 10-foot, controller-first UI.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":523,"cache_read_input_tokens":864894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":523},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"e5452153-bbd7-4d00-9243-9d40d8fbb3c7","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:56:42.722Z","request_id":"req_011CghHz8ryQtHf5uBcQMWoW"}
