{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghJ3KcxTQsQXQU7tMeiy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is complete: one HTML file in the folder root, built on the my-3D2dge handoff, with every TASK.md feature implemented and no TODOs, placeholders or stubs. `exit-interview.json` is in the root and answers every field. The last full test run passed all 13 suites (2,758 checks), and a separate browser run dragged all 79 items with a real mouse.\n\nWhat each TASK.md item rests on:\n- **Genre, title, single file:** a real-time, first-person party dungeon crawler titled *Eye of the Bolt-Holder*, picked by random number from 50 riffs on the source's name. It ships as one HTML file, with the dev folder named to the required pattern.\n- **Engine, art and animation:** the engine's native code-drawn 3D style and procedural rigs are the base. New assets (wall features, 16 monster types, the caged-Eye boss, four 3D cutscene sets, item icons) and new animations (levers, gates, secret walls, Great Locks, the boss's bolts, weapon swings, monster wind-ups) make it feel like the source.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code, including nine original songs and a babble voice synth for the Animalese/Simlish-style speech. There are no audio files.\n- **Online:** an AI Hub ghost-mode race for 1–4 players, plus a solo time trial. The hub's own harness passes all three of its scenarios.\n- **Feature-complete demo:** four floors, three NPCs, a moral choice, three hidden deeds, 18 spells, camp, saves, and an ending. A bot plays the whole game by the real rules and reached the ending on all 40 seeds that were never used while tuning.\n- **Dev Mode:** reached from the pause menu, with 22 live tuning sliders and a reset to defaults, cheats, warps, spawning, debug overlays, a sound test, auto-play and fake hub ghosts.\n- **Attract mode:** starts after 10 seconds idle, plays 9 bot-driven scenes, and any input exits back to the title.\n- **10-foot, controller-first UI:** prompts follow the device you used last. Pads show PlayStation glyphs by default, with a Settings option for Xbox. Keyboard and mouse are native, with hover focus, clicks, drag and drop and the mouse wheel.\n- **Touch-only devices:** show \"Controller or keyboard required\" with the settings button, have no touch controls, and still run the attract mode. This was checked under Chromium's phone emulation, since no real touch device is available here.\n- **Writing:** terse second-person messages, capitalised inscriptions, a stately narrator and formal NPCs, close to the source's voice.\n- **Testing:** one command prints only failures and a one-line count. It covers headless Node suites, the scripted full playthrough, headless Chromium checks and the hub harness. Screenshots at three resolutions and 1920×1080 filmstrips were used only to judge looks; the filmstrips were reviewed as contact sheets.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler (a \"blobber\") with lock-and-key puzzles. The view is through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with smoothed steps and quarter turns, plus a top-down parchment map and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182 WebGPU/WebGL 2 and Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub session client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro-modern look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, wall mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. There are four cutscene sets and a brass \"lockwork\" UI with hand-made 12×12 item icons.\n- **Animation:** the engine's character rigs, attack moves and poses; a smoothed first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms; first-person weapon swings.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine original songs (each with a combat layer that comes in during fights), seven short musical cues, over 100 sound effects panned by direction, and an Animalese/Simlish-style babble voice synth.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); on its own, a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:**\n  - headless Node suites, including a scripted full playthrough and a balance sweep on seeds held out of tuning;\n  - Playwright checks in headless Chromium: controller glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop of all 79 items, the attract mode, an emulated phone;\n  - the AI Hub harness;\n  - screenshots and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used for looks only.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and the AI Hub README; nothing else.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37.\n2. **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** About 10:\n   - the stair to the ending did not work;\n   - two boss bugs: it could block the cell needed to re-turn a lock, and its cell stayed blocked after death;\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - the Slumber spell never worked;\n   - balance spikes that wiped every run;\n   - hub sessions sent no progress reports.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. It reached the ending on all 40 held-out seeds.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The 3D crawler kit, drawing everything in code, and headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, three times:\n   - wall colours come from the level legend, not the level's colour option;\n   - the pause key is handled before the scene sees it;\n   - secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** A simulation that runs the same way every time and contains no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many menu screens.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel, for the babble voices;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - the button-symbols setting limited to PlayStation (the default) and Xbox;\n   - beards on character rigs, because dwarves need beards.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** A built-in grid-crawler layer: cells, wall features, and doors and secret walls that share the level's wall material.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** The deliverables are concrete, and the plan gave a complete target to check against.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan is far larger than one session, and it wasn't clear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** A real-time party crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a 10-foot, controller-first UI.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** State a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2712,"cache_read_input_tokens":868278,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2712},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"21333123-6db6-42b6-af23-b205d2812be0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T04:57:31.199Z","request_id":"req_011CghJ3KGderaX6LKJAAquP"}
