{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghJiu2Ujf3XQrvsmrMu6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"*Eye of the Bolt-Holder* is finished. This round I made two final visual upgrades:\n\n- **Cutscenes:** the camera now cuts to a framed shot of Strake or Lintel whenever they speak, in both the Council Hall and the dawn city square. Narration keeps the slow wide shots.\n- **Creatures:** the brass spider gained knee joints, clawed feet, fangs and plated segments; the Lidless Hound gained ribs and a mane; the Rat Knot's tails gained rings.\n\nThe single HTML file and `exit-interview.json` are in the folder root, and the JSON parses with every field answered. The full test command passes all 13 suites (2,759 checks), and `src/` has no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler with lock-and-key puzzles. You see through the party's eyes on a four-way grid with smooth steps and quarter turns, plus a parchment automap and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3, from jsDelivr); AI Hub client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro-modern look: code-drawn low-resolution 3D with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. Four cutscene sets; a brass \"lockwork\" UI.\n- **Animation:** the engine's rigs, moves and poses; a smoothly eased first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms; first-person weapon swings; cutscene camera cuts to the speaker.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine songs with combat layers, seven fanfares, over 100 panned sound effects, and an Animalese/Simlish-style babble voice synth.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race); a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:**\n  - headless Node suites, including a full scripted playthrough and a balance sweep on seeds held out of tuning;\n  - Playwright checks in Chromium: glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop of all 79 items, the attract mode, an emulated phone;\n  - the AI Hub harness;\n  - screenshots and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used for looks only.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and the AI Hub README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** About 10:\n   - the ending stair didn't work;\n   - two boss bugs (it could block the cell needed to re-turn a lock, and its cell stayed blocked after death);\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - Slumber never worked;\n   - balance spikes wiped every run;\n   - hub sessions sent no progress reports.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. It reached the ending on all 40 held-out seeds.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the per-screen text-fit check. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit, drawing everything in code, and headless `game.advance`, which made the whole game testable in Node.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - wall colours come from the level legend;\n   - the pause key is handled before the scene sees it;\n   - secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a deterministic simulation with no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system shared by controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - PlayStation and Xbox as the only button-symbol sets;\n   - beards on character rigs.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer (cells, wall features, doors and secret walls that share the wall material).\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the concrete deliverables, and a complete plan to check against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it wasn't clear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** a real-time crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, in a 10-foot, controller-first UI.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":886579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":344},"output_tokens":833,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5572b217-8052-4a87-b3f3-49ea658fe605","uuid":"f0b05fdd-da9a-4f39-b4a3-c05cf1b3ea83","timestamp":"2026-11-04T05:06:19.343Z","request_id":"req_011CghJisC6vWcU4UrzHqug8"}
