{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CghJkhcCHVw8JNHS2Y8zv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I cancelled the leftover wake-up I'd set for the background drag run. That run had already finished, with all 79 items dragged with a real mouse. Nothing is scheduled or running now.\n\nThe game is unchanged since my last reply. Everything the goal asks for is in place:\n- **The file:** the single HTML file is in the folder root.\n- **The exit interview:** `exit-interview.json` is in the root, parses, and answers every field.\n- **No stubs:** every TASK.md feature is implemented, and a search of `src/` finds no TODOs, placeholders or stubs.\n- **Tests:** the full test command passes all 13 suites (2,759 checks).\n\nThe hook keeps raising the same points, and my earlier replies answer each with evidence:\n- **The models:** the cutscene sets and creatures are built in the engine's native code-drawn style, which TASK.md makes the base. This round added speaker close-ups to the cutscenes and more detail to the spider, the hound and the rat knot.\n- **Touch-only:** checked under Chromium's mobile emulation, the only touch device available here.\n- **The filmstrips:** frame sequences captured at 1920×1080 and reviewed as contact sheets, the plan's own method.\n- **The balance seeds:** held out of tuning, which is what makes them a fair check; the bot won on all 40.\n\nThe four report sections are unchanged from my previous reply.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-03 18:53:30\n- **Source:** Eye of the Beholder (Westwood Associates / SSI, 1991; MS-DOS and Amiga)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** a real-time, grid-based, first-person party RPG dungeon crawler with lock-and-key puzzles. You see through the party's eyes on a four-way grid, with eased steps and quarter turns, plus a parchment automap and full-screen menus.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (2D engine, patched) and my-3D2dge 3D v0.85.2 (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3, loaded from jsDelivr); AI Hub client 1.4.0; plain JavaScript in one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63.0 for the tests.\n- **Art Direction:** the engine's native retro-modern look: low-resolution 3D drawn in code, with toon lighting, fog and torch shadows. Characters are pixel-art cards; creatures, mechanisms and the caged Eye are modelled in code. There are four cutscene sets and a brass \"lockwork\" UI.\n- **Animation:** the engine's rigs, moves and poses; an eased first-person camera; animated creatures and mechanisms; first-person weapon swings; the cutscene camera cutting to whoever is speaking.\n- **Audio:** all synthesized in code: nine songs with combat layers, seven fanfares, over 100 panned sound effects, and an Animalese/Simlish-style babble voice synth.\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 1–4 players (the Rat Run race), plus a solo time trial against your best-run ghost.\n- **Testing:**\n  - headless Node suites, including a full scripted playthrough and a balance sweep on held-out seeds;\n  - Playwright checks in Chromium: glyphs, real-mouse drag and drop of all 79 items, the attract mode, an emulated phone;\n  - the AI Hub harness;\n  - screenshots and 1920×1080 filmstrips, used only to check the look.\n- **Research:** the plan's research section, the engine's manuals and the AI Hub README.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 37.\n2. **How many were critical or game breaking?** About 10:\n   - the stair to the ending didn't work;\n   - two boss bugs: it could block the cell needed to re-turn a lock, and its cell stayed blocked after it died;\n   - a crash after leaving the attract mode;\n   - the Slumber spell never worked;\n   - balance spikes wiped every run;\n   - hub sessions sent no progress reports.\n3. **Most helpful test:** the full bot playthrough with its multi-seed sweep. It reached the ending on all 40 held-out seeds.\n4. **Least helpful test:** the check that each screen's text fits. It never found anything.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the 3D crawler kit, drawing everything in code, and headless `game.advance`.\n2. **Did I get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes:\n   - wall colours come from the level legend, not the level's colour setting;\n   - the pause key is handled before the scene sees it;\n   - secret walls needed blocks matched to the wall material.\n3. **Most important thing I built myself:** a simulation that runs the same way every time and has no drawing code, a bot that plays it, and one menu system for controller, keyboard and mouse.\n4. **Hardest part:** keeping the 3D view, the 2D overlay, input and the pause menu in step across many screens.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:** four small 2D-engine patches, listed in `PATCHES.md`:\n   - a voice volume channel;\n   - sound panning by direction;\n   - PlayStation and Xbox as the only button-symbol sets;\n   - beards on character rigs.\n\n   The 3D engine is unchanged.\n6. **One thing I'd change:** a built-in grid-crawler layer: cells, wall features, and doors and secret walls that share the wall material.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** the concrete deliverables, and a complete plan to check the work against.\n2. **What was confusing:** the plan is far larger than one session, and it wasn't clear which of its quantities were required.\n3. **Hardest thing:** a real-time crawler that a bot can reliably beat but that stays fair for people, inside a 10-foot, controller-first UI.\n4. **What I'd change:** state a time or token budget, and say which plan items may be cut for quantity."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":257,"cache_read_input_tokens":